window.__BIRINCI_SEARCH__ = [{"title": "A Lawful Morsel", "stem": "a-lawful-morsel", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "a lawful morsel faith and spirituality even if the flour is fresh and the water perfectly clean, spoiled yeast will not produce sound dough; nor will sound bread come from such dough. to raise a child of good character, one must nourish that child with lawful and honestly earned food. — imam al-ghazali moral: a child’s moral formation begins with the food placed on the family table. lawful earnings and honest nourishment help lay the foundation of sound character. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Adversity and Blessing", "stem": "calamity-and-blessing", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "adversity and blessing faith and spirituality if adversity brings you closer to god, it is a blessing. if blessing takes you away from god, it is adversity. moral: a hardship that brings you closer to god is a blessing, and a blessing that takes you away from him is adversity. the measure is not comfort, but direction. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Blessings given by God", "stem": "blessings-god-has-given", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "blessings given by god faith and spirituality whoever god has blessed, he will definitely look at how he uses that blessing: if he has given you power - to your justice; if he has given you position - to your mercy; if he has given you wealth - to your morality; if he has given you peace - to your gratitude; if he has given you a life partner - to your loyalty; if he has given you children - to how you raise them. moral: every blessing is also a test. it is not what you have acquired that shows who you are, but how you deal with them. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Church bell", "stem": "the-church-bell", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "church bell faith and spirituality in ancient times, there was a rule in a country: when a commoner died, the church bell was rung once, when a nobleman died twice, when one of the king's relatives died three times, and when the king himself died four times. one day, a court where everyone sought justice unjustly convicted a citizen, and on this occasion the church bell was rung five times. the people came to the church in confusion and asked: \"father, who is more important than the king that the church bell was rung five times?\" the priest replied: \"of course there is - justice. today justice has died.\" moral: when justice dies, something greater than the king dies. stand by the truth so that injustice does not go unpunished. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Confidence is half the battle!", "stem": "faith-is-half-of-success", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "confidence is half the battle! faith and spirituality a school principal invited several of his fellow teachers into his office and said to them: — since you are our best, most capable teachers, we are entrusting you with a group of the most intelligent and capable students we have selected. your task is to provide them with a very good education and raise them to a higher level over the next year. i wish you success. teachers, students, and parents considered this initiative a very good idea and were eagerly awaiting the end of the school year. finally, the school year ended. after the tests, it turned out that those students had shown higher results than other schoolchildren in the city. the principal congratulated the teachers and said: “i have a confession to make. those students we entrusted to you were not the most capable children at all - they were average. we chose them by chance.” hearing this, the teachers became proud and began to consider the students’ success a result of their professionalism. the principal continued: “i also have a confession. you weren't the most capable teachers either! i picked your names at random from a bag of papers i had stuffed into a bag. you succeeded because you believed.” moral: belief awakens the hidden power within a person; and high expectations are sometimes the first reason for high results. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Dervish Spoons", "stem": "the-dervishs-spoons", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "dervish spoons faith and spirituality one day, the dervish was asked: — what is the difference between true lovers and those who only talk about love? — wait, i will show you now, — said the dervish. he called several so-called lovers and set a table for them. after the guests took their places, hot soup was brought to the table, followed by unusually long spoons called “dervish spoons”. the dervish stated his condition: — you will eat your soup by holding the ends of these spoons. the people agreed. but the spoons were so long that it was impossible to bring the soup to their mouths — with every attempt, the soup spilled on them, and the people burned themselves. as a result, they left the table hungry and embarrassed. then the dervish called those who knew what true love was. people with radiant faces and eyes full of love came and sat down at the table. the dervish gave them the same condition. this time the scene was different: everyone filled their spoons and handed them to the person in front of them, and one person fed the other. the table was filled with joy, and everyone stood up with full bellies and gratitude. after the guests dispersed, the dervish turned to the questioners: — have you seen it? whoever sees only himself at the table of life remains hungry. whoever thinks of his brother or friend and extends his hand to him is also satisfied. in the market of life, it is not always the buyer who benefits, but the giver. moral: whoever thinks only of himself remains hungry at the table of life, while whoever feeds the person in front of him is also satisfied. the giver always wins. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Dervish's Robe", "stem": "the-dervishs-robe", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "dervish's robe faith and spirituality one day, a wounded bird came to the king and complained that a dervish had broken its wing. the king immediately summoned the dervish. — this bird is complaining about you. why did you break its wing? — he asked. the dervish tried to defend himself: — thank god, the king, i wanted to hunt this bird. i approached it, but it did not run away. i went up to it, but it did not move again. thinking that it would surrender and trying to catch it, it tried to run away at the moment when i wanted to catch it — and its wing was broken. the king turned his face to the bird: — this man is right. why did you not run away? it did not attack it suddenly. you could have seen it approaching and flown away in time. and now you come to me and complain. the bird tried to justify itself: — i did not run away because i saw it in the guise of a dervish. if it were a hunter, i would have flown away immediately. but i thought, \"the dervish will not harm you, they fear god.\" the king was silent for a moment, then he ordered: — the bird is right. break the dervish's arm! the bird immediately protested: please, don't do that! — why? — the king asked. — if you break his arm, he will do the same thing again after he recovers. it is best to have him remove his dervish cloak so that birds like me will not be deceived again. moral: outward holiness does not make a person genuine. look at the actions, not the clothes - clothes do not make a person a dervish. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Drinking the syrup of martyrdom", "stem": "drinking-the-sherbet-of-martyrdom", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "drinking the syrup of martyrdom faith and spirituality during the ottoman period, soldiers who were seriously injured in wars and were dying would drink syrup made from zamzam water from small barrels they carried with them, so that their mouths would be sweetened before they became martyrs. the expression “drinking the syrup of martyrdom” originated from here. moral: behind every expression, there is a history, a sacrifice. the words in our language carry the memory of our past. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Fasting", "stem": "to-be-fasting", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "fasting faith and spirituality a few young men see an old man sitting in a corner eating during ramadan. they approach him and ask him jokingly: — come on, uncle, are you eating, aren't you fasting? the old man: — of course, i'm fasting, i just drink water and eat. the young men laugh: — what kind of fasting is this? the old man says: — of course, i'm fasting. i don't lie, i don't look at anyone with a bad eye, i don't make fun of anyone, i don't waste, i don't insult anyone, i don't gossip, i don't covet anyone's property. because i suffer from a certain illness, i can't force myself to fast, just because i have a stomach ache. — then he turns to the young men and asks: — and what about you, are you fasting? the young men look embarrassed and lower their heads: — no, we're not fasting, we're just not eating. moral: if a person abstains from food and does not abstain from lying, backbiting, insulting, and evil deeds, he is not fully experiencing the spiritual spirit of fasting. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Forty days", "stem": "the-fortieth-day-rite", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "forty days faith and spirituality there is a custom in turkic countries where the mother and baby do not leave the house for forty days after birth, and no guests come to their house. it is said that this is an ancient turkish custom and dates back to the goyturks. when the time came, the child's fortieth birthday was celebrated as follows: the mother and child were invited to the house of one of their close relatives, and the owner of the house would give gifts in the name of the baby - small money so that he would be prosperous throughout his life; cotton so that he would live long and have a soft heart until his hair turned gray; sugar so that his mouth would always be sweet and his face would always smile; wheat so that his house would be blessed; salt so that his mouth would not run out of flavor; eggs so that his stomach would be full and his back strong. moral: a blessing and love are hidden in each of our customs. the gifts given to a baby are actually beautiful wishes that are kept for his whole life. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Friend of God", "stem": "friend-of-god", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "friend of god faith and spirituality an old man sees a child without shoes, feels sorry for him and buys a pair of shoes for the child. the child takes the shoes in his hands and looks at them for a while, then raises his head and asks: — are you god? the old man's hands tremble, his eyes fill with tears: — no, my child, i am a simple person. the child thinks for a moment, then says quietly: — then you are a friend of god. last night, while crying, i told god that i had no shoes. moral: sometimes goodness does not come from heaven, but through the hands of good people. perhaps it is in your hands to be the answer to someone's prayer. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Hajj pilgrimage", "stem": "the-hajj-pilgrimage", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "hajj pilgrimage faith and spirituality when the time comes for a person on hajj pilgrimage to throw stones at the devil, instead of throwing them, he starts hitting himself with them. a young man asks in surprise: “uncle, why are you hitting yourself with stones?” the man replies: “son, tell me, what do we have to do with the devil here? we need to stone the devil within ourselves, educate our souls, and purify ourselves.” * * * the heat is in the fire, not in the hair, the miracle is in the head, not in the crown. whatever you seek, seek it within yourself, not in jerusalem, mecca, or the hajj. moral: the real struggle is not with the devil outside, but with one’s own soul. the miracle is not in the stones or crowns, but within one’s own soul. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Haram money", "stem": "forbidden-money", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "haram money faith and spirituality one day, while walking on the road, a sum of money fell from the hand of a person walking in front of me. usually, in such cases, i would warn him by saying, “you dropped your money,” but this time i followed the devil, picked up the money from the ground and put it in my pocket. when i got home, i told my wife the story. — put some more on top, let's go to the cinema, — she said. “let's do it.” i said. we decided to go to the cinema on the weekend. the lady insisted: “you promise now, but then you suddenly refuse; it's better to buy the tickets online now so that we can be sure.” i said “let's do it” and bought the tickets online. i am a repairman myself. a paid client of mine called and said, “come to my garden on saturday, we have some work to do.” i asked, “can i come on sunday?”, but my client said, “no, it's not possible.” since we bought the cinema tickets on saturday, as if they were the other way around, i lost a lot of money from this. in short, saturday arrived. as my wife and i were getting ready to leave the house, we unexpectedly met my mother-in-law and father-in-law in front of the door. i was about to say, “we’re going to the movies,” and continue on our way, when my wife quickly moved away from me and said, “we’re going to the movies, come on, come with us.” i realized how helpless i was. since my mother-in-law doesn’t like public transportation, we had to take a taxi to the cinema. of course, i had to pay for the taxi. we got to the cinema. i bought two extra tickets. moreover, i had to buy a box of popcorn for everyone before the movie started — it couldn’t be otherwise, the groom has to entertain everyone, because he found money on the way! i didn’t even spend as much money on the sacrificial meat as i had spent on that popcorn. since we quickly emptied the popcorn boxes, i had to go buy new ones during the movie. in short, i paid a lot for the money i found, but my punishment wasn’t over yet. well, what we were going to see was still ahead of us. finally, the movie ended, and we went out into the street. we went out, but what a way! i ran into one of my old acquaintances. since we had a long-standing enmity, my father-in-law and mother-in-law were with me, but he said a few harsh words to me. i couldn't stand it. a fight broke out. it broke out, it broke out! i hit him, he hit me. my father-in-law also got involved. in short, by the time the police arrived, my nose and my father-in-law's arm were broken. although we ended our fight peacefully at the police station, i had to pay for both my and my father-in-law's hospital expenses due to the injuries we received. recently, i was walking along the road again. i saw someone buying doner in front of me. when the seller was giving me the change, the coins fell to the ground. the man bent down and didn't pick up the change. i couldn't bear it and said, “oh brother, at least don't think about yourself, think about the person who will find this money; ”don't put people in trouble, please take that money from the ground.“” moral: money that is not earned by the sweat of your brow does not benefit a person; often you lose much more than you earn. do not touch what is not lawful. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Help Them Yourself, O Exalted God!", "stem": "help-yourself-o-exalted-god", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "help them yourself, o exalted god! faith and spirituality to the restless, left without a home, to the orphan, left alone, to the needy, left hungry and thirsty, to the sick, left in hardship, to the one whose heart is wounded, whose eyes wait on the road, to the hopeless, left without a way, to the heedless, left without light, to the one who still has a wish, who still dreams, to the faithless, left without you — help them yourself, o exalted god! moral: the last refuge of the helpless, the lonely, and the hopeless is the creator. prayer is the language of deepest need. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "If fate allows, we'll meet", "stem": "if-fate-allows-we-will-meet", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "if fate allows, we'll meet faith and spirituality the man who always says \"we'll meet\" now says \"if fate allows, we'll meet.\" there's something strange here. i haven't changed, my attitude towards him is as warm as before. i would like to meet him again with great pleasure. but this \"if fate allows\" seems to be a barrier between us. i'm unaware of his fate, and he's unaware of mine. i wonder if these two fates know about each other, can they agree among themselves and make us meet? maybe we shouldn't expect anything from fate, and this is just the embodiment of the saying \"out of sight, out of mind\"? i never liked this expression \"if fate allows\". moral: don't let the coldness between you and your loved ones linger. don't postpone it by saying \"if fate allows\" - meet today, take heart today. bakhtiyar sirajov"}, {"title": "Light of the Universe", "stem": "the-light-of-the-universe", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "light of the universe faith and spirituality british artist william holman hunt's \"light of the universe\" is on display at the royal academy in london. the painting depicts a man standing at a door at night with a lantern in one hand, looking philosophical. the man knocks on the door with the other hand, hoping that it will open. an art critic who studied the painting asked hunt: \"it's a beautiful painting, but i don't understand its meaning. will the door that the man is knocking on never open? perhaps you forgot to pull the doorknob?\" hunt smiled and replied: \"this is no ordinary door. this door embodies the human heart. since the door only opens from the inside, it has no outside handle. if that door is not opened for you from the inside, you cannot enter.\" moral: the door of the heart opens only from the inside. love and goodness do not force their way in - you must let them in yourself. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "May prayer come and find you", "stem": "let-prayer-come-and-find-you", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "may prayer come and find you faith and spirituality our elders have said that you — be the one who holds the hand of a poor person, the one who caresses the head of an orphan, the one who covers the feet of a child, the one who is wide and the one who is strong, so that the prayer that you need may come and find you. moral: a person who extends a hand to the needy and caresses the head of an orphan is himself worthy of prayer. the hand you do good to will one day be opened to you too. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Moderate eating keeps the doctor away, honest living keeps the judge away", "stem": "eat-less-and-stay-on-the-right-path", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "moderate eating keeps the doctor away, honest living keeps the judge away faith and spirituality moderate eating keeps the doctor away; honest living keeps the judge away. “moderate eating keeps the doctor away” — people who avoid overeating and excess, practicing self-control are more likely to stay healthy and need medical care less often. “honest living keeps the judge away” — people who live honestly, fairly, and lawfully, while respecting the rights of others, are less likely to face disputes or legal problems. moral: eat in moderation and act with integrity, so that you need neither a doctor nor a judge. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "My Alif Has Been Dotted", "stem": "my-alif-has-been-dotted", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "my alif has been dotted faith and spirituality there is an expression in turkish: “elifim noktalandı.” it comes from writing the letters “alif” (ا) and “ha” (ه) side by side in the old ottoman script (اه). because the “ha” after “alif” resembles a dot, people say “alifim noktalandı,” and metaphorically it means “my joy turned to sorrow.” the letter “alif,” with its long, thin appearance, expresses freedom and beauty, while the letter “ha,” with its bent, almost crushed appearance, expresses distress and collapse. when these two letters come together, the cry “ah” appears. moral: sometimes the change of a letter — of a moment — turns joy into sadness. every expression in our language carries the trace of an emotion, a story. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Not to deviate from the right path", "stem": "not-leaving-the-right-path", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "not to deviate from the right path faith and spirituality one day, a man who was wasting his time in idleness, idleness, and eating inheritances got tired of this situation and went to the king to seek advice on how to live a righteous life without deviating from the right path and asked him to show him a way. the king thought for a while and ordered the man to do the following: \"take this jar full of olive oil and carry it from one end of the city to the other without spilling even a drop. if a single drop falls on the ground, know that your neck will be cut off.\" he also assigned two soldiers to the man to supervise. the man gathered all his strength, used his intelligence, and carried the jar to the other end of the city without spilling even a drop. then he returned and went to the king again and said, \"may the king live, i have carried out your order perfectly.\" the king asked, \"what did you see in the city, what did you witness?\" there was a market in the city that day, and the crowd was so large that even a pin would not fall to the ground. nevertheless, the man replied to the king: \"may the king live, i was so careful not to spill the oil in the jar on the ground for the sake of my life that i could not take my eyes off the jar for a moment to look around. for this reason, i did not see anyone or witness any event.\" after this lesson, the king gave the man his advice with peace of mind: \"if you are so careful in everything you do, in every task you are given, if you devote yourself to your work, and if you do not forget that god is always watching over you, you will never stray from the right path.\" moral: a person who approaches everything he does with full attention, and who does not forget that god is watching him, will never stray from the right path. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "O Allah, find a solution to our problems", "stem": "o-god-heal-our-sorrows", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "o allah, find a solution to our problems faith and spirituality in the name of the most gracious and the most merciful, find a solution to our problems, in the name of the forgiving and the relenting, forgive our sins and accept our repentance, in the name of the provider, bless our sustenance, in the name of the healer, heal our sick, in the name of the opener, open the doors of goodness. moral: when a person realizes his weakness and prays sincerely, his heart finds peace. prayer is the language of hope and trust. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "O God, first give to the mountains and stones", "stem": "o-god-give-first-to-the-mountains-and-stones", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "o god, first give to the mountains and stones faith and spirituality there was a prayer in ancient turkic lands: — o god, first give to the mountains and stones, give to the forest, animals, water. then give to people, to my next door neighbor, to the needy. if there is any left, give to me last! moral: true generosity is to think of others first, then yourself. the culture of sharing of an entire people lives in this prayer. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Pray while washing dishes", "stem": "pray-while-washing-your-dishes", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "pray while washing dishes faith and spirituality pray while washing dishes. be grateful that you have dishes to wash. because it shows that you have something to eat, that you fed someone, that you cared for someone, that you prepared food and set the table. think of those who don't even have a dish to wash, who don't have anyone to set the table for. moral: be grateful for the simple things you have: having dishes to wash means you have a table, you have loved ones. gratitude goes a long way. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Ramadan Prayer", "stem": "ramadan-prayer", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "ramadan prayer faith and spirituality may the almighty god accept our prayers, bless our sustenance, heal our sick, forgive our sins, open doors of goodness for us, and fill our hearts with love and mercy! may you have a blessed ramadan! moral: sincere prayer fills the heart with hope and mercy. the wish for blessings, healing, and forgiveness brings a person closer to the creator. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Sacrificial meat", "stem": "sacrificial-meat", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "sacrificial meat faith and spirituality i was in the 5th grade of school. we didn't need anyone. my father, who was a builder, had joined a cooperative that was under construction. we dreamed that “it won't take six or seven months, and we will move into our own house.” one morning we woke up to a terrible shaking. there had been an earthquake. thankfully, nothing bad had happened to us or to those around us. no one in our building was hurt. but although our house and our lives were safe, this event brought another trouble to our family: as a result of the earthquake, construction work had stopped, and my father was unemployed. no matter how hard we tried to save, the money my father earned from the repair jobs he found from time to time was not enough to support our household. my father couldn't find work all winter. he would go with hope and return sad. there were a few days left before ramadan. my father came home happily - he had found a job. “prepare your documents, come,” they said. he announced the news with joy and left the house at lightning speed. “i have to deliver today,” he said. a few hours had passed when our neighbor came in with a panicked expression on his face. “don’t be afraid, but your father has had a minor accident,” he said. when we arrived at the hospital, my father’s face was pale. his arms and legs were bandaged. a car speeding through a red light had hit my father. when we prayed that he was alive, my father tried to hide the tears that were flowing from his eyes, embarrassedly saying, “why did i survive? i made your burden heavier.” after a while, my father came home. our stove wasn’t burning, our house was cold. several of our neighbors had called the municipality and asked for coal for us. “no way,” they said — we had to register in advance. and so, our “sugar-free” ramadan holiday came and went. despite all the difficulties, the weather had warmed up a little, the bandage on my father’s arm had come off, but his leg was still bandaged. then came eid al-adha. i thought to myself: “there are twelve apartments in our building, many of them will sacrifice. at least they will give us a share, and my mother will cook my favorite meat dishes.” as i watched from the window, the sacrificial animals were slaughtered, skinned, and bowls of meat were carried to the houses in the empty area opposite. every time the door was opened, the delicious smell of meat being roasted in the houses spread to our house. but unfortunately, no one brought us a piece of meat. meanwhile, my grandmother called from the village to congratulate us on our holiday. she sensed that i was worried by the sound of my voice as she spoke. she asked insistently and anxiously: “has your father gotten worse?” “no,” i said slowly. “the neighbors didn’t give us sacrificial meat.” in the summer months, we would go to the village to my grandmother. she had a cow and five or six chickens. her cow gave good milk. when she milked it, my grandmother would sing songs to her. on the third day of the holiday, there was a knock on our door early in the morning. it was my grandmother who came. we welcomed her with joy. she hugged us all, crying. “my lambs, my lambs, i brought you a lot of meat,” she said. we filled the refrigerator to the brim with meat. my sister quickly chopped the meat. the delicious smell of the meat cooking further fueled my appetite, which had been growing for two days. my mouth was watering as i stood at the stove. i had no patience to wait for the table. i picked up the fork and dipped it into the meat. i was whistling as i brought the skewer to my mouth when my father’s words to my grandmother almost pierced my ears: — oh, mother, oh! why did you slaughter that cow? my heart was pounding, my head was spinning. i threw the fork in my hand and ran outside. my grandmother's hope, the cow she milked while singing songs, had been dashed because my soul craved meat? the table was set, they pulled me by the arm and insisted on making me sit down. i reluctantly and shyly dipped my fork in again. the skewer was stuck in my throat. tears fell from my eyes. “what happened, what's wrong with you?” they asked. — my tooth!… — i said. — my tooth hurts a lot. moral: a holiday is a real holiday when it is everyone's holiday. it is humanity's duty not to forget those who do not see meat in their homes, those who are in need, in the midst of abundance. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Stoning the Devil", "stem": "stoning-the-devil", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "stoning the devil faith and spirituality when the time comes for a person on the hajj pilgrimage to throw stones at the devil, instead of throwing them, he starts hitting himself with them. a young man around him asks in surprise: — uncle, why are you hitting yourself with a stone? the man replies: — son, tell me, what do we have to do with the devil here? we need to stone the devil inside us, educate our own souls, and purify ourselves. moral: the real struggle is not with the devil outside, but with one's own soul. purifying oneself is more difficult than condemning others, but it is more valuable. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The Dervish's Dress", "stem": "the-dervishs-clothes", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "the dervish's dress faith and spirituality the dervish was asked: — why is your dress so untidy? the dervish replied: — since i am constantly busy improving my interior, i have no time to improve my exterior. moral: a person who beautifies his inner world does not make external defects a criterion for measurement. because what makes a person beautiful is not his clothes, but the purity of his heart and the light of his soul. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The Idle Devil", "stem": "the-idle-devil", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "the idle devil faith and spirituality a dervish was lost in thought when he suddenly saw the devil next to him. \"why are you sitting so harmlessly?\" he asked the devil. the devil, tired, raised his head and answered the question with a question: \"you people cause so much harm and pain to each other that i have no work to do?\" moral: people oppress each other so much that the devil has no work to do. most evil comes not from outside, but from within us. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The most beautiful blessing", "stem": "the-most-beautiful-portion", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "the most beautiful blessing faith and spirituality the dervish was asked: — what is the most beautiful blessing that can happen to a person? the dervish said: — it is to meet someone who wants to listen to you. moral: the most beautiful blessing that can happen to a person is a person who wants to listen to you sincerely. being listened to is another name for being loved. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The Mullah and the Scholar", "stem": "the-mullah-and-the-scholar", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "the mullah and the scholar faith and spirituality a scholar from another country comes to tamerlane; after much talk through an interpreter, he says he wishes to meet the scholars of this land. tamerlane gathers all the scholars. none of them dares to debate the foreigner. at last, seeing their fear, the mullah takes the debate upon himself. a gathering is held. the foreign scholar draws a circle on the ground and faces the mullah. the mullah thinks a moment and divides the circle in two. seeing the scholar silent, he draws another line, divides it into four, pulls three parts toward himself and acts as if giving one part to the foreigner. the scholar agrees. then the scholar holds out a fist with the back of the hand toward the ground and waits. the mullah answers by turning his hand the other way, fingers pointing down. the scholar agrees to that too. a third time the scholar gestures as if eating. the mullah quickly takes an egg from his pocket and flaps his arms like bird wings. the scholar laughs, kisses the mullah’s hand, and the mullah leaves. through the interpreter tamerlane asks the scholar: “we understood nothing of these signs.” the scholar says: “views of the world differ. by drawing that circle i meant that the earth is round, and i wanted your scholar’s opinion. mashallah — he quickly drew the equator, then divided the globe into four and showed that three parts are water and one is land. then i asked about the life of plants on the globe. your scholar answered rightly again — that all plants live by rain and the sun’s rays.” “as a third question i wanted his view of humans and animals; with an egg he quickly reminded me that birds too belong to that kind.” after the scholar left, tamerlane called the mullah and asked him about the exchange. the mullah said: “ah, the scholar… i thought he was truly a deep man. turns out he was a simple glutton… first he drew a circle on the ground and said to me, if only there were a pan of omelette here now. i quickly halved it and said they wouldn’t give it all to you — we’re here too. i saw my friend wasn’t satisfied. i quickly split it again and took three shares myself, so he’d see what greed gets you. the man saw the omelette slipping away and agreed. the second time he raised his hand to the sky as if to say: how about a pot of pilaf? i held my hand palm-down and said: if there’s any black on top, i’ll eat it. the third time he showed me his belly and said: i’ve come from the road, i’m hungry. i quickly took the egg from my pocket and said: for your sake — i’m hungrier than you, and i’ve only one egg in my pocket; don’t insist, i can’t give it.” moral: the same sign is read differently in each person’s world. we often see the other in the mirror of our own thoughts. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The Pleasure of Allah", "stem": "gods-pleasure", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "the pleasure of allah faith and spirituality haja ubaydullah ahrar says: \"i have examined all the paths that lead to the pleasure of allah; i have found that the shortest is to please people. i have examined all the paths that lead away from the pleasure of allah; i have found that the most dangerous is to break a heart.\" moral: the shortest path to pleasure is to please people, and the most dangerous path that leads away from it is to break a heart. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The power of truth", "stem": "the-power-of-truth", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "the power of truth faith and spirituality the sword of truth is not bent, nor is its wrist twisted. moral: truth is unbending and unyielding. even though time passes and power changes, in the end, the righteous one stands firm. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The Price of a Miracle", "stem": "the-price-of-a-miracle", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "the price of a miracle faith and spirituality (a true story) lisa was eight years old when she heard her parents talking about her younger brother, oliver. all the little girl knew was that her brother was very sick and that her parents had no money for his treatment. only a very expensive operation could save oliver. there was no one to lend them money. lisa heard her father whisper helplessly to her tearful mother, “only a miracle can save him now.” the girl went into her room and took out a glass jar that she had kept in a secret place in the closet. she poured out all the money in the jar on the floor and counted it carefully—she did this several times. when she was finished counting, she carefully put the coins in the jar, closed the lid, and went out the back door to the pharmacy. as soon as she reached the pharmacy, she went inside and patiently waited for the pharmacist to pay attention to her. the pharmacist was busy, his mind was busy talking. although lisa tried to get his attention with various movements and sounds, the man remained silent. finally, the little girl took some of her money out of the container and slammed it on the glass counter. the pharmacist was startled: — my daughter, what do you want? — he asked nervously and, without waiting for her answer, added: — i am talking to my brother right now, whom i have not seen for a long time. — i also want to talk to you about my brother, — lisa answered with the same nervousness. — he is sick... i need a miracle to cure him. — what? you want a miracle? — the pharmacist became even more irritated. — his name is oliver, and something bad is growing in his brain. my parents say that now only a miracle can save him. tell me, how much does a miracle cost? — we do not sell miracles here, my daughter. i am sorry, but i cannot help you, — the pharmacist said in a slightly soft voice. — look, i have money. if it is not enough, i will find the rest and bring it. just tell me the price of a miracle. a smartly dressed man who had been watching the conversation from the sidelines until now—the pharmacist’s brother—leaned down and asked lisa: — what kind of miracle does your brother need? — i don’t know,— lisa answered.— all i know is that he is really sick, and my parents say he needs an operation. since my parents don’t have the money for an operation, i decided to spend my own money. — how much money do you have?— the man asked. — one dollar and eleven cents,— lisa answered slowly.— that’s all i have, but i can add a little more if necessary. — one dollar and eleven cents... what a wonderful coincidence,— the man smiled.— that’s exactly the price of the miracle your little brother needs. the man took the money from the girl, took her hand, and said: — take me home. i want to see your brother, talk to your parents. let’s see if i have the miracle your brother needs. this handsome man was dr. carlton armstrong, a renowned neurosurgeon. the operation was free of charge, and soon oliver was back home. mom and dad were overjoyed. mom whispered to dad, “this is a real miracle. how much did the operation cost?” lisa’s lips parted, and she smiled with satisfaction. because she knew exactly how much this miracle had cost: one dollar and eleven cents—plus the little girl’s belief in the existence of a miracle that could heal her brother. moral: faith is a powerful force. sometimes, when a child’s pure faith meets the kindness of a human being, a miracle is born. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Where does misfortune come from?", "stem": "where-does-calamity-come-from", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "where does misfortune come from? faith and spirituality the dervish is asked: — where does misfortune come from? the dervish says: — check your eyes, have you looked at what is forbidden? — check your tongue and ears, have you listened to gossip and gossip? — check your hands, have you reached out to what is forbidden and added something forbidden to your livelihood? — check your feet, have you gone where you should not have gone? moral: the root of what happens to us can often be found in our own actions. check your eyes, your tongue, your hands, and your steps. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Why are you waiting for the end of the world?", "stem": "why-are-you-waiting-for-the-last-day-of-the-world", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "why are you waiting for the end of the world? faith and spirituality one day, a professor at a university in america addressed his students as follows while starting a class: — imagine for a moment that today is the last day of the world. tomorrow at this time, everything will be over. you have no chance of salvation. what would you do if that were the case? the students gave different answers: — i would pray, ask god to forgive my sins. — i would say goodbye to all my loved ones. — i would dedicate the rest of my time to my family. — i would visit my mother or father. — i would eat all my favorite foods for the last time. — i would sleep until i was full. — i would walk in the forest for the last time, enjoying the greenery. — i would watch the sunrise and sunset for the last time. — i would watch the stars in the evening. — i would prepare my favorite meal, invite all the people i love to dinner. — i would go to the place i wanted to go most in life, and wait for death there. — i would look for and find the people i have hurt, i would apologize to them so that they would forgive me, and so on. the teacher writes all the answers on the board, then laughingly says: — children, why are you waiting for the end of the world to do all this? moral: don't wait for the end of the world to do good deeds. do loving, forgiving, and valuing today, right now. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Work with your hands, hope in God", "stem": "your-hand-at-work-your-hope-in-god", "category": "Faith and spirituality", "slug": "iman-ve-meneviyyat", "hay": "work with your hands, hope in god faith and spirituality once upon a time, there was a dervish. he spent most of his days in solitude and worship. however, he did not like being in need of people, so he would devote part of his day to work: he would work a little, buy bread with the money he earned with his hands, and distribute the excess to the poor. one day, while he was still engaged in worship in the desert, his eyes fell on a fox. the fox's legs were crippled, and he could barely move forward. the dervish thought to himself: \"i wonder what this fox does for a living? wild animals live on hunting, and for hunting they need sharp feet. this poor thing has no feet. i wonder where god provides for him?\" while he was thinking this, he saw a lion from afar bringing a gazelle he had hunted in its tusks. the lion puts down its prey, tears it apart, eats it to its heart's content, then leaves the rest there and walks away. the crippled fox, who had been lying in wait for a while, crawls towards it, eats the meat and fills its stomach. the dervish is amazed at this. he praises allah, who provides sustenance to the fox through the lion's hand. then the following thought comes to his mind: \"since this fox earns its sustenance without doing anything, why shouldn't allah provide for me? i cut off the hours i would have set aside for worship and spend them on work. however, i can also worship my lord during those hours and earn rewards. from now on, i will not work. allah, who sent the crippled fox's food to the lion's feet, will also provide for me in some way.\" from that day on, the dervish abandons work and devotes himself to worship day and night. but no matter how much he waits, sustenance does not come. no one knocks on his door, no one brings him a piece of bread. the dervish melts away day by day, leaving only skin and bones. finally, one day when he was exhausted, he heard a voice from the unseen: — i gave you a healthy body, strong arms, and sharp legs. but you have compared yourself to a crippled fox. why do you compare yourself to a fox and not a lion? why do you look at someone else's surplus? get up, work like a lion, earn your own livelihood. let the helpless and the helpless rely on you. eat yourself and feed the needy. the dervish is ashamed of what he has done, repents, and returns to his previous life. he earns his livelihood again with the labor of his own hands, and gives up the idea of ​​being a burden to others once and for all. moral: a person who looks at the share of the needy despite having the strength is not trusting in allah - he is simply covering up his laziness with faith. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "A Father’s Footsteps", "stem": "a-fathers-footprints", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "a father’s footsteps family and upbringing a father said to his son: “son, watch your step carefully.” the boy replied: “you should watch your own steps, father. i am following right behind you.” moral: the most powerful form of guidance is personal example. children often imitate not what adults say, but the steps they take. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "A Festive Gift", "stem": "a-festive-gift", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "a festive gift family and upbringing “wise elder,” i asked, “what should i buy for the holiday?” he replied: “lift the spirits of a few people. bring smiles to the faces of a few children. and, if you can, earn the prayers of a few people in need.” moral: the most precious gift is measured not by money, but by kindness and attention. what makes a celebration meaningful is sometimes not what you receive, but what you give. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "A Mother’s Love", "stem": "a-mothers-love", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "a mother’s love family and upbringing erich fromm i am one year old—completely happy, because my mother is always beside me. i am two—my mother is with me only sometimes. i wonder what is happening. i am three—my mother is at work, and my nanny is with me. i am five—my mother is in a meeting, and my grandmother is with me. i am seven—my mother is at a seminar; i am at school with my teacher. i am thirteen—a teenager. my mother still has to work, and my friends are beside me. i am seventeen—my mother comes home exhausted. i am at school, in class, or at a café with friends. i am twenty—young and confident. my mother is not at home, but my friends are with me. i am twenty-three—i have started working. my mother is home now, while i am at work. i am thirty—and i wonder: when did my mother and i last spend real time together? something is wrong. i am thirty-five—married. my mother lives in her home, and i in mine. i am forty—my mother waits for me just as i once waited for her as a child. i am fifty—i have grown older, yet one wish never ages: to press my heart to hers, embrace her, and rest my head on her tired knees. i am fifty-five—another mother is always beside me now, the mother of my children. my own mother has passed away, and i live with the ache of missing her. in the rush of ordinary life, one of our gravest failures is not giving enough time to our children and those we love. children are the most precious characters in the extraordinary film of life unfolding before us. if we fail to notice them growing, those passing moments can never be recovered. life has no stop, play, forward, or rewind buttons that can show us its vanished scenes again. may we understand this before it is too late. love your child today. hold them, take their hand, and remain beside them. do not miss today’s episode, because tomorrow a new scene of childhood will already have begun. “a mother’s love is a selfless, protective, and warm refuge. precisely because it is unconditional, it cannot be obtained by force.” moral: a mother’s love is unconditional and irreplaceable. make time for those you love today, because time is the most precious thing we can never recover. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Fakir Baykurt's unforgettable mother's advice", "stem": "the-mothers-advice-fakir-baykurt-never-forgot", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "fakir baykurt's unforgettable mother's advice family and upbringing one day, fakir baykurt (turkish writer, 1929-1999) asked his mother for tea: \"mom, my heart wants tea, come and have some tea,\" he said. the mother did not let her son down and took him to topal hüseyin's nearby teahouse and called out, \"master, please bring a cup of tea for my child.\" after the tea arrived, fakir baykurt, who was so excited at that moment and did not know how to drink the tea, took a big sip of the hot tea and suddenly threw the cup on the ground as it was near his mouth. although the tea cup did not break because it fell to the ground, fakir baykurt thought that his mother would slap him. but that did not happen. his mother called topal hüseyin and asked him to bring another cup of tea. baykurt drank the second cup of tea this time in a slurping motion. for years, he asked his mother why she didn't slap him that day, but his mother left this question unanswered every time... fakir baykurt's mother answered this question years later at the village school where her son taught. fakir baykurt remembers that day when his mother, elif khanum, attended his son's lesson: — i got angry, excited, and roared during class! when the lesson was over, i asked my mother outside: \"mom, how was it, did you like my teaching?\" and my mother said: \"oh, what can i say, son, it wasn't bad.\" — mom, the inspectors come, they like it, and they give good grades. and you say \"it wasn't bad.\" how can this be? — i asked. and my mother said these words that can be a lesson to everyone: \"for years, you asked me why i didn't slap you. now i'm telling you, open your ears and listen! if i had been angry the day you poured tea on me, the lion inside would have raged. if i had beaten him, that lion would have died! then you wouldn't be a teacher like that. you should act like me, be calm. don't try to kill the lion inside these children with a stick.\" moral: don't kill the \"lion\" inside a child — his self-confidence — by beating him. true education is not given with fear, but with patience and love. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Father and son", "stem": "father-and-son", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "father and son family and upbringing the son had left home after a heated argument with his father. the father, who was heartbroken, was also angry and forbade his son's name to be mentioned in the house. the mother, however, would sit on her son's bed every night and go to sleep sniffing his pillow. — i miss my son. what can i do, let's go find him and bring him home, — she said, but the father did not agree. a long time had passed. one day, seeing that the mother was constantly in tears, the father felt sorry for her; he took a piece of paper with his son's address written on it from his pocket and handed it to the mother: — go, you can find him at this address, — he said. — but never tell him that i gave him the address. although the father said a few more words, the mother did not seem to hear him; all that was left in her mind was the address. she was overjoyed. she immediately got ready, set off and reached the address written on the paper. it was a repair shop. her son was working in overalls. the mother stared at her son with tearful eyes for a few minutes, then finally walked towards him. the son, who had been unaware of his family for a long time, thinking that everyone had forgotten about him, was at first surprised when he saw his mother, then hugged her tightly. he asked everyone except his father, asked how he was, and finally: — how is that man, is he still angry? — he asked his mother. the mother left this question unanswered: — come on, son, let's go home, — he said. — no, mother, i won't go. i can't live in the same house with that man again, — he said and started walking towards work. the mother, who had been watching her son for a while, called out to give him the sweets she had prepared. the son took the gift: — what's wrong, mother, don't insist, i won't come. i can't live in the same house with a man who hasn't even looked for me once, isn't interested in me, — he said. the mother, with her neck bent, said: — well, son, it's best that you know. i see that your decision is firm, you will not return home. but your father ordered you to stay away from the person you have been friends with for the past month - he will not do you any good. let him reconcile with his old friend, - he said. hearing this, the son froze in amazement. the mother returned home and rebuked the father: - man, if you knew where he was, why did you hide it from me? so, that's why you were so relaxed? the father, who always seemed nervous, broke down, tears flowed from his eyes: - he is my soul, my soul, - he said. - how long have you known? - the mother asked. - since he left. sometimes during the lunch break, i would go and watch from afar to see what he was eating and drinking. this was also the reason why i sometimes came home late: i would go and watch our son in the evenings to see what he was doing, who he was sitting with, - the father replied. while the elders were hugging each other and crying, there was a knock on the door. the mother wiped away her tears and went to open the door. the son had returned home. he kissed his parents' hands and hugged them. he had understood - the father he thought had forgotten him, torn him from his heart, had actually never taken his eyes off his son. moral: don't hide your love, show it. sometimes a timely step can prevent years of resentment. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "From the wise father's advice to his son", "stem": "from-a-wise-fathers-advice-to-his-son", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "from the wise father's advice to his son family and upbringing o my beloved son! every human being should have a mind that can help them to establish different relationships with others. this mind is the inexhaustible source of all happiness and joy. life cannot be satisfied; its short or long life does not diminish its value. fruit only falls beautifully from the branch when it is ripe. similarly, people have always preferred those who have seen the light and experienced. the son of man usually commits the wrongdoings he commits in his youth without thinking about it. but when he remembers them later, these sins pierce his heart like a dagger and torment him. those who follow the right and good path in their youth will be respected and honored in their old age in return. it is good to be strong and powerful. however, strength must be given to the command of the mind. the almighty god, the creator of the worlds, did not send the son of man to this world to live forever, but wanted him to taste death at the end of his life. know this well: god almighty has thoroughly tested and accustomed me to two things that have kept me healthy up to this age. one of them is to eat little, and the other is to walk around to digest what i eat. i also want you to know this: there are three types of people in this world: the first is those who have their minds and thoughts in order, and who can see and think more or less about the future; the second is those who are far from knowing whether their path is right or wrong, but who fall into this state not of their own will, but due to the influence of those around them. when they are advised, they accept and listen to words. the third is those who neither know anything themselves, nor listen to the advice given, but think they know everything. these are the most miserable of all. o son! if god almighty has created you from the people i have listed in the first place, i will be happy. if you are from the second, i advise you to listen to the advice given to you. definitely do not be from the third. they are in no good standing with god or man. moral: wisdom is the source of happiness, and strength is the servant of wisdom. he who listens to advice will be exalted; but he who thinks he knows everything will perish. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Grandfather and Grandson at the Market", "stem": "grandfather-and-grandson-at-the-market", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "grandfather and grandson at the market family and upbringing a grandfather comes to the supermarket with his grandson. they walk the aisles, choose groceries, and fill the basket. meanwhile the grandson keeps shouting, “buy me this, buy me that!” and turns the shop upside down. each time the grandfather says, “be quiet, arif — we’ll go home when we finish shopping.” the child reaches the limit of his frustration, takes a kinder surprise from the shelf, puts it under his foot, and crushes it. again the grandfather says, “be quiet, arif — we’ll go home when we finish shopping,” then, crestfallen, turns to a nearby worker: “i’m so sorry — i’ll pay for this too.” at last they pay at the checkout and leave. a woman who has watched the whole scene follows them, approaches the grandfather, and says: “i was impressed by how you behaved with arif. you are a very patient man. well done!” the old man thanks her and smiles: “the child’s name is rafiq — i am arif.” moral: being able to control yourself in a tense moment is a sign of true strength. keeping calm sometimes means mastering yourself, not others. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Hold my hand", "stem": "hold-my-hand", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "hold my hand family and upbringing a little girl and her father are crossing a bridge. the father, a little worried, asks his daughter to hold his hand: — my sweet daughter, hold my hand tightly so that you don't fall into the river. the little girl replies: — no, father, you hold my hand. the father, a little confused, asks curiously: — what's the difference? — it's a big difference, — the little girl replies. — if i hold your hand and something happens to me, i might let go of it. but if you hold my hand, i'm sure that no matter what happens, you won't let go of my hand. moral: the power of love is not letting go. instead of waiting for the hand of those who love you to hold theirs, hold theirs tightly. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "I'm tired, mom", "stem": "i-am-tired-mother", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "i'm tired, mom family and upbringing — i'm tired, mom... — what, son? — i'm tired of everything: of working, of friends, of traveling, of always being responsible for pleasing someone, i'm so tired. — put your head on my knee and relax, son, forget everything for a moment... give me your pain... — thank you, mom, it's good that you're here; with you i forget my age, my life. be there, mom. — mom is the giver of life, son, i'm always with you. — mom, we tire you out a lot too, forgive us. — you don't tire me out, son. my tiredness is my motherlessness. i don't have a \"mother's knee\" to rest my head on, son... don't get tired, appreciate what you have... moral: a mother's lap is the most comfortable refuge in the world. a mother always finds a place to put her tiredness - appreciate what she has. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Mother and son", "stem": "mother-and-son", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "mother and son family and upbringing the man put the suitcases he was carrying against the wall. then he stood up, took a deep breath, and said to his mother, “mom, this is a very beautiful place.” “of course, it is beautiful, my son,” the old woman replied. for a while, they were both silent, carefully examining the room. then the woman opened her small suitcase and took out her nightgown, slippers, a few towels, and a framed picture. she placed the picture on the table next to her bed. a boy in a school uniform, with a soldierly posture, was looking out of the picture. a child with full eyes and pursed lips... the man approached the window and opened the curtain. a pale daylight filled the room. it was freezing outside. he leaned his hands on the heater and watched the muddy cars passing by for a while. then, without even turning his head, he asked, “is this closet enough for you, mom?” the old woman was lost in thought, so she didn’t even hear her son. she was leaning slightly to one side on her bed, her eyes fixed on the boy in the worn frame, his eyes fixed on the boy with a frightened look. it was a picture they had taken on the first day of school. the woman suddenly went back years ago. the day before, she had spent hours explaining how wonderful school was, but she had barely managed to get her son into the classroom. he had been at school all day, and every break she had gone to him and said, “i’m here, son, i’m waiting for you.” but the boy had clung to his mother’s hand even tighter and cried, “don’t leave me, mom, i’m so scared.” the sun was setting. the man took his hands off the heater and leaned them against the window, sweating. “open your other suitcase too, let’s put your things in it, mom,” he said. “don’t delay, son, i’ll put them in myself. i don’t have much stuff anyway,” the old woman said. she reached out her trembling hands and took her son’s hand. she seemed to be about to say something, but then she stopped. she swallowed: “the children will be worried too.” the door slammed shut. the old woman was left alone in the room with the peeling paint and the soldier-like look in the picture with her son. after sitting on the bed for a while, she put on her slippers and went to the window with the curtains open. her son was getting into the car. the old woman was afraid. despite the fact that she had listened for hours the day before to how beautiful this place was, she was still afraid. her son's handprint was still on the window glass. she carefully rested her cheek against the mark on the glass. her eyes were full, her lips were pursed. “don't leave me, son, i'm so scared,” she whispered. the words seemed to melt on the sweaty glass and flowed towards her son, who was sitting outside in his car, waving at her. those who lived on the same floor as the old woman heard a sobbing sound that night. the only thing they were used to in the nursing home was the sound of sobbing that echoed through the corridors at night. moral: life is a circle: a child who holds onto his mother's hand one day may leave her the next. true loyalty to a parent is not physical care, but love and attention. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Only my mother cries", "stem": "only-my-mother-would-weep", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "only my mother cries family and upbringing only my mother cries, the rest are lies! moral: you can see a lot of love in this world, but there is no other love as unconditional and sincere as a mother's love. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Parenting", "stem": "raising-children", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "parenting family and upbringing in a family, there was a boy who did not listen to his parents. whatever they said, the boy did the opposite. the boy did poorly at school. he neither studied hard nor completed his homework. even though his mother got angry and beat him, it did not help. even though his father locked him in his room and punished him, he could not cope with the child. one day, when the mother, who was left helpless, took the child to school, she came across a “bear trainer” on the way. the trainer, surrounded by people around, was playing a tambourine, while the bear with a ring on its nose was jumping and playing. the woman stopped and thought. she said to herself: “i can’t raise this child, but look how this man raised his bear!” then she approached the bear trainer and asked: — i can’t raise my child, how do you raise this wild animal? the man smiled: — there is a way to everything. i also have my own way of raising him. the woman said: — please help me. the bear trainer looked at the boy and said: — there is no difficult task. entrust him to me for a week, i will make him obedient, — he said. the woman was happy and started taking the boy to the bear trainer every day. although only a few days had passed, the boy's behavior slowly began to improve. he no longer protested against his mother. after a week, the boy became exactly as the woman wanted. the woman was very happy. she expressed her approval to the bear trainer and prayed for him many times. the neighbors were very surprised by this. that disobedient, rebellious boy was gone, and in his place was a calm, peaceful, well-mannered boy. the teacher at school was also pleased with this change in the boy and congratulated the mother. however, the change did not last long. although he was not as ambitious and angry as before, this time the boy began to steal his classmates' things, do secret things and lie openly. the teacher called the boy's mother and said: “i don't know what you are going to do, but your boy has become very strange. he started stealing and lying.” the woman was very sad. although she went to look for the bear trainer in despair, she could not find him. the man had left. seeing the parents’ helpless state, her wise neighbors said: “don’t worry, i know a wise man with a big heart. if you want, go to him and explain the situation.” the woman agreed. she took her child and took him to this wise man. after the man got acquainted with the situation, he said: — give me this beautiful young man for a week, i will see what i can do. — the woman accepted the offer. he started taking the child to this wise man. after only two meetings, the man became very upset. he said to the child’s mother: — what have you done to this child? i see animal traits in him. — the woman was surprised: — i didn’t do anything, i just used to take him to the bear trainer because the child was very naughty. the man became very angry: — do you think an animal trainer can educate a child? animals are educated with fear, and people with conscience. animals are sometimes disciplined by beating, cursing, and punishing; if that doesn’t help, they are put in a dark room and left helpless. how can you entrust your child to an animal trainer? then he continued: — remember that education is not about going after a child, but about entering his heart and influencing him with compassion. take your child and go. be close to him, kiss and hug him, listen to him, do not withhold your love from him. the more love you show your child, the more he will soften, he will see that he has parents who love him, his trust and love for you will increase. — your child’s cure is neither the bear trainer nor me; only you — the parents, — the wise man concluded. moral: a child is educated not with fear, but with love. it is not a teacher or an outsider who educates a child—it is a loving parent. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Puppies for sale", "stem": "puppies-for-sale", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "puppies for sale family and upbringing a little boy entered the shop after seeing a sign in the window saying “we have puppies for sale.” “how much do you sell puppies for?” he asked. “between thirty and fifty dollars. each one has a different price,” said the shopkeeper. the little boy reached into his pocket, took out all the money he had, and placed it in front of the shopkeeper, then counted it all carefully. finally, in a disappointed voice, he said, “all i have is thirteen dollars and twenty-seven cents,” he said. then, taking a deep breath, he asked, “can i at least see the puppies?” the shopkeeper smiled and called to his lady in the back room to bring the puppies. a moment later, the lady came out of the room, followed by five fluffy, plump puppies. however, one of the puppies was walking with great difficulty. the boy looked at the puppy: “what’s wrong with him? why can’t he walk?” he asked. “our vet said that his tibia was congenitally deformed. he will never be able to walk comfortably, - the shopkeeper replied”. hearing this, the boy was excited: “i want to buy this puppy,” the shopkeeper said: “i don't think you want to buy it, my son. if you want, i'll give it to you.” the little boy was very angry. he looked the shopkeeper straight in the eye and said: “i don't want you to give him to me. that puppy is as valuable as any other. i can give you thirteen dollars and twenty-seven cents now, but i'll bring you five dollars every week until i get thirty dollars,” he said. the shopkeeper said: “you really don't want that puppy, my son. he won't be able to run and play with you like the others,” the little boy said: “but we have to give him a chance, he's as valuable as any puppy,” he rebelled. then he bent down and slowly pulled up the hem of his trousers and showed his leg, which was injured by polio, and the iron supports on it. then he turned to the man and said in a soft voice: — uncle, i can't run and play either. this little one and i need friends who will understand and support us. moral: every living thing needs a friend who will understand and support it. the one who is different is just as valuable as the others; we need to give him a chance. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Rotten Seed", "stem": "rotten-seed", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "rotten seed family and upbringing a father who called his little son, who was constantly suffering from illnesses after his wife died, despised him and gave him to an orphanage. years passed. one day, this man saw his grown son in front of a school. he could not believe his eyes. the father, who looked at his son's shining eyes and healthy body with regret, said with excitement: — i did not believe that you would recover, that you would become a man. you have become a strong warrior. how did this miracle happen? how did you get rid of your illnesses? — he asked his son. the boy looks at his father, who once despised him, with his moist eyes, and points to the old woman who has adopted him, who is approaching him from the opposite side, smiling and saying: — after you said, “rotten seeds do not germinate” and gave me to the orphanage, this woman took me in as her son and never left her compassionate hands on me, saying, “every seed watered with love will germinate.” moral: words either build or destroy. it is possible to elevate or destroy someone with a single word—every seed watered with love will germinate. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Spinach", "stem": "spinach", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "spinach family and upbringing we lived in a remote village. we didn’t even have a proper road to get around. my mother was addicted to spinach. after my father’s death, i suspected that she had lost her mind when i was a child... “you need to eat a lot, it gives strength to the body,” my mother would say, and for breakfast, lunch, and dinner, she would only feed us spinach, with a little cornbread on the side. i hated spinach already, i couldn’t stand to see this vegetable. one day i told my mother: “when i grow up and have a job and start a family, i will never buy spinach at home. i won’t cook spinach at home.” she promised that she was looking forward to those days, and that she would even play at my wedding, no matter how old she was. the day came, i moved from our village to the city to study in high school. i somehow managed to make ends meet with the small scholarship i received. once a month, when i came home, i didn't want to eat a spoonful of the spinach dish my mother put on the table. i only wanted to go back to the dormitory and eat delicious food. i studied. by the time i graduated from university, my master's degree, and my doctorate with government loans, my mother was already a bent old woman. when i came to the village and got married to the girl i was going to marry, my old mother both cried and played at my wedding, as she had promised years ago. at that time, i couldn't understand why my mother shed tears when she played. years passed. my mother passed away two years ago. one day, i found the notebook my mother had kept at that time in an old chest. on one page of the notebook, my mother had written: “i only had spinach seeds left. i constantly planted spinach and a little corn. i didn't have any on hand or in my pocket. i cooked this dish so that my children wouldn't go hungry. but i didn't rebel. ”spinach is very useful, so i give it to you three times a day,“ my heart ached when i said. this was all i could do for my children. i love them with all my heart.” i loved eating spinach at the age of fifty. my wife asks me in surprise why i buy spinach so often, she thinks i love spinach. but she doesn't know that every time i smell that food, i remember the sacrifices my mother made to raise me, how she played at my wedding while crying... moral: we often understand a mother's sacrifice in the face of impossibility years later. love is sometimes hidden in a plate of simple food. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Teeth falling out", "stem": "fallen-teeth", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "teeth falling out family and upbringing a king saw in a dream that all his teeth had fallen out, so he called a dream interpreter and asked him what it meant. the man replied: “may the king live, this means that all your relatives will die, and you will be the last to die.” the king was angry and dismissed the man, called another and asked him the same question, and received the answer: “may the king live, you will be the longest-lived in your family.” the king was very pleased with this answer and rewarded the man and sent him on his way. moral: the same truth can be expressed both harshly and delicately. words can both hurt and heal - how can one say it? stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The best gift", "stem": "the-most-beautiful-gift", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "the best gift family and upbringing for a father — honor, for a mother — respect, for a child — compassion, for a spouse — love, for a friend — loyalty, for society — service, for the deceased — prayer, for the world — humanity, for the hereafter — goodness in the world. moral: every relationship has its own best gift: honor for a father, respect for a mother, loyalty to a friend, service to humanity. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The nest builder", "stem": "the-builder-of-the-nest", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "the nest builder family and upbringing the female bird builds the nest. moral: it is the woman who builds the nest and keeps the family afloat. her hard work should be seen and appreciated. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The Value of Your Family", "stem": "the-value-of-your-family", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "the value of your family family and upbringing one evening, a son came to his father and said he wanted to ask him a question. the father agreed. the son got straight to the point and asked his father how much money he earned in an hour of work. the father was troubled by this question and wanted to know why his son was asking such a question. the son said he was just curious and waited for an answer. finally, the father gave in and said that he earned a hundred dollars an hour. hearing this, the son lowered his head, was silent for a moment, and then asked his father to borrow fifty dollars. this request angered the father; he refused, thinking that his son would spend the money on a meaningless toy or something unnecessary, and told him to go to his room and sleep. the son went to his room without protesting, quietly closing the door. the father tried to calm down by pacing the room. after a while, he calmed down and admitted to himself that it was wrong for his son to get angry without asking why he wanted the money. the father, tormented by a guilty conscience, went to his son's room, knocked lightly on the door, and asked if he was asleep. the son replied that he was still awake. the father entered the room and apologized for his harsh behavior. he admitted that he had taken the stress of the day out of his son, handed him fifty dollars, and told him to spend it as he pleased. the son thanked him joyfully, took out the money he had hidden under his pillow, counted it, and looked at his father. seeing this, the father became angry again and asked his son why he had asked for more money when he had money. the son explained that the money he had saved before was not enough to buy what he wanted, and now he had exactly one hundred dollars. then he asked his father if he could \"buy\" a watch for him - he asked him to come home from work early tomorrow and have lunch with him. these words deeply moved the father, and his eyes filled with tears. he hugged his little son and apologized to him. moral: the greatest need of children is the attention, love, and time spent with them by their parents. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Waiting for the teeth", "stem": "i-am-waiting-for-the-teeth", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "waiting for the teeth family and upbringing on a cold winter evening, an elderly couple walks into mcdonald's and sits down at a table. the man goes to the cashier and buys a hamburger, a large box of fries, and a large coca-cola. he cuts the hamburger in half and puts half in front of his wife, counts the fries one by one, and gives her half. after a while, he opens the coca-cola bottle, takes a sip first for himself, and then for his wife. the people around them watch them, saying, \"two people share a meal for one, poor things.\" after a while, they see that the hamburger and fries in front of the wife are still the same. the wife watches her husband eat with appetite, occasionally taking a sip of the coke... finally, one of the young employees loses patience and approaches the couple and asks the man: — uncle, excuse me, i've been watching you for a while: you're eating, and your wife isn't touching the food. if he doesn't like it, let me bring him something else... the old man: — thank you, young man, but we are happy with our situation... we have been married for sixty years and we share everything like this, — he says. not satisfied with this answer, the young man turns to the woman this time: — auntie, why don't you eat your hamburger and fries, what are you waiting for? the old woman points to her husband and replies: — teeth...! i'm waiting for teeth, baby. moral: no one should be left in need of the one they love. may god not leave anyone helpless, even in their own family. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Weeds need to be uprooted", "stem": "weeds-must-be-pulled-from-the-root", "category": "Family and upbringing", "slug": "aile-ve-terbiye", "hay": "weeds need to be uprooted family and upbringing when we were in school, we would spend our summer holidays in our garden with our family. my late father wanted to get us used to hard work. he would assign us garden chores on the days he was at work. he had planted a melon, and it was our duty to water it. since the water in our well was not suitable for drinking, we would carry drinking water from the well in our neighbor, uncle aghasalim's garden. we had learned to harrow the land. we also did weeding: we would turn the soil upside down with the help of a harrow, and crush and destroy the weeds. but they were not so easy to remove. one day, my father called us to him and explained: \"if you want to destroy a weed, hit it deeply with the harrow; the work is not finished until you pull out its roots.\" moral: if you do not uproot both the problem and the bad habit, the appearance of cleanliness is deceptive - after a while it will reappear. bakhtiyar sirajov"}, {"title": "A loved one is a person who gives his love to others", "stem": "the-beloved-is-one-who-gives-love-to-people", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "a loved one is a person who gives his love to others love and marriage many years ago in china, a girl named li-li got married and started living in the same house with her husband and mother-in-law. however, she soon realized that it was very difficult to make ends meet with her mother-in-law. the two have completely different personalities, which causes them to argue frequently. according to chinese traditions, this is not a pleasant situation and leads to discontent among those around her. a few months later, the endless quarrels between the daughter-in-law and the mother-in-law turn into hellish torment for both the young woman and her husband. believing that something must be done, the young woman runs to her father's old friend, a spice merchant, and tells him about her troubles. the old man prepares a medicine from herbs and instructs her to add a little bit of it to the food she cooks for her mother-in-law every day for three months. the poison will be given little by little so that it will not be known that the daughter-in-law killed her mother-in-law. the old man instructs the young woman to treat her mother-in-law very well and to cook the most delicious dishes for her so that no one, especially her husband, suspects. returning home with joy, li-li applied exactly what the old man had said. every day, she cooked the most delicious dishes and dripped “poison” into her mother-in-law’s bowl. she treated her very well so that no one would suspect anything. after a while, her mother-in-law had changed a lot, and she looked at her as her own daughter. feeling a heavy burden, the young woman went to the spice shop, feeling regretful and begging the old man for a medicine that would cleanse the blood of the poison she had given her mother-in-law. she no longer wanted the old woman to die. the old man looked at li-li standing in front of him and started laughing and said: “dear li-li, what i gave you was only vitamins. the real poison was in your own brain. as you treated her well, that poison melted and gave way to love; thus you became a real mother and child.” moral: the real poison is often in our own brains. as you treat others with love, hatred melts, and enemies turn into friends. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Advice for Those Who Marry", "stem": "advice-for-those-who-marry", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "advice for those who marry love and marriage wise elders offered the following counsel to a young couple beginning married life: “do not compare your marriage with anyone else’s. do not say, ‘look at what that person’s husband is like,’ or ‘look at what that person’s wife is like.’ do not seek perfection; none of us is flawless. do not investigate each other’s past—it is called the past for a reason. do not expect your spouse to read your mind. no one can. speak openly about your wishes and concerns, and try to resolve difficulties through conversation. accept that alongside your shared life, each of you has a private world. hedgehogs draw only as close as their spines allow; if they move too near, they hurt one another. respect each other’s personal boundaries. in marriage, be neither owner nor servant, but companions on the same road. do not promise, ‘everything will always be wonderful.’ say instead, ‘when life becomes difficult, i will be beside you.’ even if you have been upset with each other during the day, remember to say good night before sleeping. let tomorrow begin with reconciliation.” moral: a happy marriage rests not on comparison or the pursuit of perfection, but on open communication, mutual respect, and care for each other’s personal boundaries. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Don't let your hearts separate", "stem": "do-not-let-your-hearts-part", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "don't let your hearts separate love and marriage why do people shout when they argue? if the person is right next to you, why do you need to shout? can't you talk to him calmly? why do you shout when you get angry? when people are unhappy with each other and argue, their hearts move away from each other. to overcome this distance and hear each other, they have to shout. the more angry they are, the louder they shout. but what happens when people fall in love? they don't shout, but they speak softly. because their hearts are very close, and the distance between them is very small. what happens when their love grows stronger? they are silent, but they whisper to each other, and this love brings them even closer. in the end, even whispering becomes unnecessary for them - they just look at each other and understand everything without words. this happens only between two people who love each other. therefore, when you argue, don't let your hearts separate, don't say words that can increase the distance between you. because one day this distance may become so great that you cannot return. moral: screaming is the sound of the distance between hearts. do not say words in an argument that will increase that distance, otherwise the return will become difficult. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Early Marriage", "stem": "early-marriage", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "early marriage love and marriage i had just graduated from university and started working as a teacher at a school. one day, i was sitting in the teachers' room, reviewing the students' exam papers. i was correcting the wrong answers of the female students so that they could get a good grade in order to move up to the next grade. at that moment, a person entered the teachers' room and curiously asked why i was changing the girls' exam papers. i said, \"if these girls fail the exam, their fathers will take them out of school and marry them off at the age of twelve or thirteen. but if they move up to the next grade, their marriageable age will be at least fifteen or sixteen. during this time, even the laziest student will definitely learn something in class.\" about half an hour later, our principal called me over and asked what i was doing in the teachers' room. i said that i was reviewing the exam papers. — did you meet someone in the teachers' room? what did you say to him? — he asked. — did that person complain to you about me? — i asked. — not at all! the man came to me and said that he had come to inspect the school, but there was no need for it. — why? — i was surprised. — because you have a wise young teacher. please convey my thanks to him, — he said. moral: a teacher's small care can change the fate of a girl. early marriage deprives children of education; let's not turn a blind eye to this. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Friendship and love", "stem": "friendship-and-love", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "friendship and love love and marriage — write the quarrel on the leaves of the tree so that when autumn comes, the leaves dry up and fall, and the quarrel disappears. — write the anger on the clouds so that it rains, the clouds disperse, and the anger disappears. — write the hatred on the snow so that the sun rises, the snow melts, and the hatred disappears. — write the friendship and love on the hearts of newborn babies so that they grow up, and friendship and love spread throughout the world. moral: write the quarrel, anger, and hatred on temporary things so that they disappear; write the friendship and love on the hearts so that they remain forever and spread throughout the world. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "I kiss your eyes", "stem": "i-kiss-your-eyes", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "i kiss your eyes love and marriage do you know why it is said \"i kiss your eyes\"? it is the eyes that suffer the most from longing; therefore, they are the ones who deserve the most farewell. moral: the one who longs the most is the one who deserves the most farewell. love repays the one who works hard. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "I Love You", "stem": "i-love-you", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "i love you love and marriage they say a nightingale fell in love with a white rose… every night he would go and perch beside her and declare his love. but the white rose ignored his feelings and paid no heed to his devotion. one day the nightingale gathered all his courage, perched beside the white rose again, and said he loved her madly. but the rose snapped: “leave me alone — i don’t love you!” and hurt him. however much he insisted, the rose rejected him. the love was so strong that the nightingale, shaken by despair, begged the rose’s forgiveness, pulled a thorn from her, drove it into his own heart with all his strength, and dyed the pure white rose red with his blood! the rose understood, though late, that the nightingale had truly loved her. but what good was it? it was already too late. under the branches of the rose now dyed red, the nightingale closed his eyes forever. since that day, red roses have meant “i love you.” moral: true love is sometimes understood too late. value the one who loves you in the moment — fear having to say “too late.” stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Love from a distance", "stem": "loving-from-afar", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "love from a distance love and marriage two friends meet. the following conversation takes place between them: — i think i have a crush on a girl. — how wonderful! does she know? — no. — why don't you come closer and confess? — i would, but i'm afraid of disrupting the order. suddenly, right where i am, i start to fall in love with someone. we start going to the cinema, the theatre, walking around holding hands. this can lead to resentment, reconciliation, and questions like \"why did you look at that?\" \"why did you filter that?\" if there's going to be a breakup in the end, why is there a need for all this fuss? that's good, i love that girl from a distance. moral: sometimes people are afraid to express their love for fear of losing her. but unrequited love is also a loss. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Purple chepken", "stem": "the-purple-jacket", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "purple chepken love and marriage in ancient times, there was a custom in turkish villages: a purple chepken was added to the dowry of girls getting married, along with other items. the chepken was placed at the very bottom of the dowry chest, and it was hoped that the woman would never wear it. a purple chepken was a garment that could be a threat to men. because if a woman wore that chepken and went out onto the roof of the house, the chimney, the village square - a place where everyone could see it, it would mean \"i don't love my husband, my husband is hurting me, i got married against my will, i want to get a divorce, i am in a difficult situation, help me.\" in such a case, the village people would help the woman, but would condemn her husband. this was considered a great shame for the man. for this reason, men would try to solve their problems with their wives without resorting to violence, with love and respect, and, moreover, by making important concessions to the woman. otherwise, they knew that the woman would wear a purple turban. a man whose wife wore a purple turban would not only be forced to divorce, but would also not be able to easily remarry. because everyone knew that a man who made his wife wear a purple turban would never become a husband. no one would give a girl to a man who did not respect a woman and was violent, and it would be very difficult for him to remarry. it is said that this is why the number of women subjected to violence was almost non-existent. if a woman wore a purple turban, then the flowing waters would stop, the hands that milked cows, spun wool, and wove rugs would stop; old mothers and grandmothers would stop their work and come to the defense of the woman who was forced to wear a purple turban. at that time, if there were no weddings, no flutes, no other entertainment around, everyone would be silent. because for a married woman to wear a purple turban or fall into a difficult situation was considered death. the husband of a woman wearing a purple turban cannot leave the house, cannot go to the teahouse, and no one will look at his face. moral: violence against women is unacceptable. when society considers it a duty of conscience to protect women, there is no room for oppression. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Rose", "stem": "the-rose", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "rose love and marriage if you water a rose too much, it will rot; if you water it too little, it will wither; if you water it right, it will live. moral: in love and in upbringing, moderation is key. neither excessive pressure nor indifference — moderate care keeps it alive. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Take Care of Those You Love", "stem": "beware-of-those-you-love", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "take care of those you love love and marriage on a sunny day in the park, a woman told the man beside her that the boy in the red shirt on the swings was her son. the man smiled and pointed to the boy in blue: that was his son, jamil. after a while, he checked his watch and called: “jamil, it is time to go home.” “five more minutes, dad, please!” the father agreed. later he called again, but the boy asked for another five minutes. once more, the man said yes. the woman remarked admiringly: “you are a very patient father.” the man answered with a sorrowful smile: “this is not patience. last year my older son kamal was struck nearby by a drunk driver. i had never given him enough of my time. now i would give anything for five more minutes with him. i promised not to repeat that mistake with jamil”. “my son thinks he gains five more minutes of play. in truth, i am the one who gains — the happiness of watching him five minutes longer.” moral: every extra moment with those you love is a gift. before it is too late, give them five more minutes today. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The eraser in marriage", "stem": "what-breaks-a-marriage", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "the eraser in marriage love and marriage a story takes place between a father and his newly married young son. the father asks his son to bring a white piece of paper, a pen, and an eraser. — why? — the son asks. — you bring it, — says the father. the son brings a pen and paper, but he can’t find an eraser. — go and get an eraser, — says the father. the son is very surprised, but he goes and buys an eraser, brings it, and sits next to his father. — write, — says the father. — what should i write? — write whatever you want. the son writes a sentence. — erase it now, — says the father. the son erases it. — write another sentence. — for god’s sake, father, what do you want? — write something again. the son writes. his father again erases what he has written. after this writing and erasing has been repeated several times, the father asks: — is the paper still white? — yes, it is white, father. what does all this mean? the father puts his hand on his son's shoulder and says: — yes, my son, marriage is something that cannot be done without an eraser... you need to carry an eraser with you to erase some of the things you will see and dislike in your wife. and your wife should have such an eraser to erase the unpleasant things you will do. because if you don't do this, your marriage page will be black and white in a few days... if you expect mutual love and respect in your life, both of you will show it to each other. and you will never forget the eraser! moral: marriage is the art of forgiveness. for love and respect to continue, both parties must be able to \"erase\" each other's flaws. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The Mother-of-Pearl Flower", "stem": "the-mother-of-pearl-flower", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "the mother-of-pearl flower love and marriage the situation of an eighty-year-old couple who were being tried for divorce in the courtroom was painful for everyone… the judge, looking into the eyes of the stubborn, silent grandmother, sunken from crying, addressed the old woman in a full voice: — tell me, aunt, why do you want a divorce? — he asked. after taking a deep breath, the old woman began to speak in a choked voice: — this man has worn me out; for exactly fifty years he has made me tired of life…, — she said and fell silent. a long silence fell on the hall. the silence was broken by a journalist’s photo flash. who knows what headline the newspapers would give this event after fifty years of life together. many journalists were watching the trial. what would the woman say? everyone was listening to her. the old woman’s eyes filled with tears and she continued: — i had a mother-of-pearl flower that i loved very much… he doesn’t know… it was fifty years ago. i had planted that flower by taking a leaf from the flowers he had given me. we had no children, but i considered this flower my child. after a while, i saw that the flower had begun to wither. at that time, i promised that i would wake up every night before sunrise and water this flower. for fifty years this man never once got up at night and said, “let me water this flower.” until last night… last night i lay down exhausted and could not wake up… i spent fifty years with such a man. i gave my life, my hope, my everything. i saw nothing from him. i assure you, i can live my life better without him. the judge turned to the elder: — do you have anything to say, grandfather? — he asked. the old man leaned on his staff, turned to the judge with a guilty expression on his face, and began to speak: — i spent my military service as a gardener. i worked hard to make that garden grow beautifully and healthily. i met fatima there. i gave her bouquets of the most beautiful flowers. when we were newly married, she complained of neck pain, so i took her to the doctor. the doctor said, “if she sleeps too long without waking, the muscles in her neck will stiffen and her condition will worsen. she should break her sleep every night, wake up, and walk around.” the lady did not listen to the doctor’s words… i did not listen to her either… in those days our flower accidentally started to wither. i told her, “if you get up and water the flower at night, it will come back to life.” i asked her to promise, and she promised… i woke her up every night and watched her. i watched the woman i loved watering the flower she thought of as her child. every night, it was as if i was that flower… grandfather paused for a moment and continued: — every night, after she went to bed, i woke up to empty the water from the pot. mother-of-pearl does not like to be watered at night, your honor… and last night… what can i say, this old age… i could not wake up either… the flower could have gone thirsty, but my wife would have faced health complications… she blamed me for not waking her… i did not raise my voice; i remained silent… at that moment everyone in the hall, including the journalists, was in tears… moral: sometimes love is hidden not in words, but in the care that is silently borne for years. let us protect those we love, and stay away from breaking hearts. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The red dress", "stem": "the-red-dress", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "the red dress love and marriage the red dress was hanging in my mother's wardrobe. it seemed to shine next to the worn, faded clothes she had worn all her life. i went to touch my mother and the moment i saw it, i realized that she was living the last moments of her life. seeing the red dress, i said to her: — mom, what a beautiful dress! i have never seen you wear it before, — i said. — i have never worn it, — she said softly. — sit down next to me, my beautiful daughter, i want to teach you a lesson before i leave this world. i sat down on the edge of the bed. my mother took a deep breath. “i don’t have much time left,” she said and continued: — while i thought i had always taught you good things, i now realize that i have taught you very wrong things. — what is that word, mother? — listen to me. i have always believed that a good woman should not think of herself first. i should have thought of others before myself. i should have always fulfilled everyone's wishes, this, that. my desires were crushed under the desires of others. who knows, maybe one day my desires would come true too. but that day never came. my whole life was spent in sacrifices—for your father, for your brothers and sisters, for you. — that's right, mother, you did everything a mother could do. — oh, my daughter, what i did for you or for them didn't benefit me. do you understand? i never wanted anything for myself! your father is now in the next room, staring at the walls, annoyed. when the doctor told him that i was living my last days, he came to me and said: — you must not die, do you hear me? what will i do after you? — he said. — my daughter, i have always worked hard for you. seven days a week... i was always the first to wake up in the house and the last to go to bed. i always ate the burnt bread, the smallest pieces. i spent every penny i had saved on your clothes, on your books—even if they were often unnecessary. i have never gone shopping and bought myself anything nice. i just bought that red dress you saw last year. i had some money saved up to fix the washing machine. when i got home with the dress, my father was very upset: — where are you going to wear this dress? are you going to the opera or something? — he said. in fact, he was right. i never wore that dress except when i tried it on in the store. — oh, my daughter, i used to think that if a person does not think about himself in this world, he will be happy in the next. but i don’t believe that anymore. i think god wants us to fulfill our desires in this world and now. if a miracle could happen now and i could get out of this bed, i would be a very different person. these were my mother’s last words to me: — my daughter, — she said, — don’t follow my path. promise me. i promised my mother and she breathed her last. moral: a person who always forgets himself will one day find that he can't live for anything. love others, but give yourself the right to live too. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "True Love", "stem": "true-love", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "true love love and marriage there was an island where all the feelings lived. one day news spread that the island was sinking. the feelings began to prepare in haste to leave. love was in no hurry to leave. she waited until the last moment, as if sure a miracle would save her. when the island was almost gone, love finally decided to ask for help. at that moment wealth was sailing away in a very large boat. love asked: “wealth, can you take me with you?” wealth said: “no, i can’t. my boat is full of gold and silver — there is no room for you.” love then turned to arrogance, sailing in a beautiful sailboat, and asked for help: “arrogance, please help me.” arrogance replied: “i can’t help you, love. you’re too wet — you’ll ruin my sail.” sadness was nearby. love asked him too: “sadness, may i come with you?” sadness said: “oh, love, i am so sad that i need to be alone.” happiness was not far from love, yet so excited that she did not hear love’s call. suddenly a voice said: “love, come with me.” love felt so lucky and happy that it did not even occur to her to ask who was reaching out. when they reached the shore, the one who had helped love left her there and went on. on the shore, realizing how much she owed, love told a wise person what had happened and asked: “who was it that helped me?” the wise one answered: “it was time.” “time? but why did time help me?” love asked. the wise one smiled: “because only time knows how important and precious love is.” moral: only time knows the worth of true love. time keeps sincere love alive and lets the false fade into forgetfulness. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "What did a woman suffer in this world...", "stem": "what-women-have-endured-in-this-world", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "what did a woman suffer in this world... love and marriage what did a woman suffer in this world... even in fairy tales, they made her either a witch or a stepmother... what was not said about a woman in stories. she could never be herself; whereas in this world, her name should have been someone who gave life, nurtured, and loved unconditionally. woman... they told her that her hair was long and her mind was short. your hands are full of flour, stay put, don't interfere with anyone, they told her. oh woman, what did you suffer in this world. few people knew your worth. few people could see your love, your heart, your goodness. when you asked for your rights, you were evil, but when you were silent, you were pure. when you showed your love, some thought you were a master. sometimes, those you raised to the heavens with your love preferred to stay there and forgot about you. they didn't ask if you were okay, if you weren't okay, how were you. they didn't say if you were there or not. they forgot that you had a soul. they forgot that you were human. they made you sick, they crushed you, they ignored you... and you had no intention other than to beautify, help, and improve. your soul wandered in this world in need of love. they talked about heaven and made you experience hell. they looked into your eyes and denied even what they saw there. they did not hear the cry that came from within. she loved being a mother the most, she loved looking into her child's eyes with love. in this world, only her child's embrace felt sincere to her. moral: there can be no peace or love where a woman is not valued. it is the duty of society to see her work and heart. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "What is loyalty?", "stem": "what-is-loyalty", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "what is loyalty? love and marriage it is not forgetting, not turning your back on a difficult day, not closing the doors... not laughing when your friend is crying, loyalty is sharing every pain with him... it is not just a word... loyalty means being with your friend, balm for the wounded, water for the thirsty, and finally, loyalty is \"understanding\", not throwing away a rose because of its thorn. moral: loyalty is not abandoning on a difficult day, not throwing away a rose because of its thorn. it is not a word, but a deed. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Will a broken heart love again?", "stem": "can-a-broken-heart-love-again", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "will a broken heart love again? love and marriage someone asked the dervish: — will a broken heart love again? — yes, it does, — said the dervish. the man said to the dervish: — have you ever drunk water from a broken vessel? dervish: — tell me, have you ever stopped drinking water because the vessel is broken? moral: even if the heart is broken, it does not stop loving. just as one does not stop drinking water because a vessel is broken, so too does a person continue to love. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Woman and the mirror", "stem": "the-woman-and-the-mirror", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "woman and the mirror love and marriage a woman wakes up in the morning and looks in the mirror. she sees that she has three strands of hair left on her head. “hmm... — she says. — today i’ll braid my hair…” she braids her hair and has a great day... the next day she wakes up and looks in the mirror again... she sees that she has two strands of hair left on her head. “hmm... — she says. — today i’ll part my hair in two…” she does what she said and has another great day... the next day she wakes up and sees that she has only one strand of hair left on her head. “no problem, that’s fine... — she says. — today i’ll do a ponytail too...” she does so and has another great day... another day she looks in the mirror and sees that she has no hair left on her head. “oh my!… — she shouts happily. — how great, i don’t have any hair problems today!” moral: everything depends on the perspective. those who cannot create happiness within themselves cannot take it from others - they can walk even in the rain. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Yellow and Red Flowers", "stem": "yellow-and-red-flowers", "category": "Love and marriage", "slug": "sevgi-ve-evlilik", "hay": "yellow and red flowers love and marriage in earlier times, yellow flowers placed in a house window meant, “someone in this home is ill; please do not make noise as you pass.” red flowers conveyed a different message: “a young woman who has come of age lives here; please mind your words.” moral: a culture of considerate communication, expressed through subtle signs, teaches people to show respect without unnecessary words. a warning does not always have to be loud. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "A Compliment", "stem": "a-compliment", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "a compliment friendship and human relations maria lived with her mother in a small apartment in new york and worked as a secretary for a large company. her life was quiet, ordinary, and rather dull. at work, hardly anyone noticed her. one morning, on her way to the office, maria saw a newly opened hat shop and went inside out of curiosity. a woman and her young daughter were there, along with another customer looking for a hat. maria tried on several hats. one appealed to her more than all the others. the moment she put it on, the little girl exclaimed with delight: “mom, look how beautiful that woman looks in the hat!” the girl’s mother turned to maria and said: “madam, that hat suits you very well.” the other customer joined them: “it really does. you look wonderful in it!” maria stepped in front of the mirror. for the first time in her life, she liked her reflection so much. smiling, she went to the counter and paid for the hat. when maria left the shop, an entirely new world seemed to open before her. the flowers looked brighter than ever, and the fresh air smelled especially sweet. even the sounds of traffic and passing voices seemed to form a harmonious melody. maria felt beautiful and happy. at the office entrance, the security guard opened the door and wished her good morning. in the elevator, someone asked which floor she needed and pressed the button for her. throughout the day, her colleagues admired her and told her how lovely she looked, as though they were seeing her for the first time. her manager even invited her to lunch to ask for her opinion on a new project. when the magical workday ended, maria was still smiling. she thought about how suddenly her life had changed and was certain that she owed it all to her new hat. maria took a taxi home. her mother opened the door, stared at her in amazement, and said: “maria, you look wonderful! your eyes are shining just as they did when you were a child!” “yes, mom, it is all because of my new hat. i have had an amazing day!” “what hat?” her mother asked, puzzled. alarmed, maria touched her head. the hat was gone. she wondered whether she had left it in the taxi, at lunch, or in the office. then she replayed the scene in the shop: putting on the hat, hearing the compliments, walking to the counter, taking out her purse, and setting the hat down beside the register… suddenly she understood. she had forgotten the hat in the shop and walked out without it, her eyes still shining with joy. the hat had not made maria happy. it was the confidence and delight awakened by other people’s kind words. the world had not changed; maria’s way of seeing it had. moral: a single kind word can change how a person sees themselves—and sometimes how they see the entire world. happiness is not found in outward possessions, but within us and in the sincere attention we offer one another. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "A Glass of Milk", "stem": "a-glass-of-milk", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "a glass of milk friendship and human relations a poor boy who sold small goods from door to door to pay his tuition discovered one day that he had no money left and was desperately hungry. he decided to knock on the next door and ask for something to eat. a young woman answered. embarrassed, the boy could not bring himself to ask for food and requested only a glass of water. seeing that he was hungry, the woman brought him a large glass of milk instead. after drinking it slowly, the boy asked: “how much do i owe you?” “nothing,” the woman replied. “my mother taught us to do kindness without expecting anything in return.” the boy could say only: “thank you from the bottom of my heart.” as he walked away, he felt stronger not only physically, but also in spirit. his faith in god and in other people had been renewed. years passed. one day, the same woman became seriously ill. unable to determine the cause of her illness, the local doctors sent her to specialists in a large city. dr. howard kelly was called in for a consultation. when he heard which town the patient came from, he immediately went to her room. the moment he saw her, he recognized the woman who had given him a glass of milk years before and rekindled hope in his heart. dr. kelly personally took charge of her treatment and resolved to do everything he could to save her. after a long struggle, the illness was overcome and the woman recovered. when the time came for her discharge, dr. kelly requested the final bill. he wrote a few words along its edge and had it delivered to the patient’s room. the woman opened it anxiously. she feared that she would never be able to pay the amount and would spend the rest of her life repaying the debt. then she noticed a sentence written in the margin: “paid in full with one glass of milk.” signed: dr. howard kelly. moral: a selfless act of kindness is never lost. it is entered into an unseen account and may return to us one day—often when we least expect it. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "A Handful of Roasted Chickpeas", "stem": "a-handful-of-roasted-chickpeas", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "a handful of roasted chickpeas friendship and human relations a boy entered a nut shop with his mother and kept insisting, “i want some roasted chickpeas.” the shopkeeper saw that the mother did not intend to buy any and felt sorry for the child: “come here, son. take a handful for yourself.” “no, i will take them only if you give them to me,” the boy replied. the shopkeeper handed him a generous handful and asked: “why did you not take them yourself? why did you want me to give them to you?” the boy smiled: “because your hand is bigger than mine.” “what a clever child you are!” the shopkeeper said with a laugh. moral: sometimes who you ask matters more than what you ask for. when the giver’s hand is larger, the gift may be larger too. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "A Pair of Boots", "stem": "a-pair-of-boots", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "a pair of boots friendship and human relations one day, a schoolteacher asked her pupils to write a story on any subject and promised a pair of boots to the author of the best one. every child completed a story and handed it in. the teacher read each piece and liked them all. unable to choose the best, she decided to draw the winner by lot. each pupil wrote a name on a slip of paper, folded it, and placed it in a box on the teacher’s desk. after mixing the slips, she drew one and announced: “amina!” the classroom erupted in applause. amina pressed the boots to her chest and could not hold back tears of joy. that evening, the teacher returned home and tearfully told her husband what had happened. he was puzzled: “you found a wonderful solution. why are you crying?” the teacher replied: “because not one slip bore the writer’s own name. every child had written amina—the pupil from the poorest family in the class. they all wanted her to receive the boots.” moral: the finest victory is to value another person’s joy above one’s own. true compassion is revealed in the willingness to surrender an opportunity for someone else. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Being lonely", "stem": "to-be-alone", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "being lonely friendship and human relations i went to visit an acquaintance. i entered his house, i went into his room. he was sitting silently with his head bowed. he looked sad. — are you sad? — i asked. he raised his head, looked into my eyes and: — no, i'm lonely, — he replied. moral: the biggest pain is to feel lonely even among people. seeing the loneliness of someone next to you is true sensitivity. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Discussion and Conflict", "stem": "discussion-and-conflict", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "discussion and conflict friendship and human relations as every day, javid bey and his team gathered in the meeting room to discuss the status of the project they were working on. everyone briefly reported the successful results of the previous day as well as the difficulties they had encountered. it was kamal’s turn. like the others, he reported on the work he had done, but — as if hoping for help from his colleagues — he spoke in more detail about the problem he faced. as soon as he finished, nadir accused him in front of everyone: “you are to blame; because of you our work has stopped and we can do nothing.” the room froze. kamal was deeply embarrassed and did not know what to do. as project manager, javid bey immediately intervened so the talk would not turn into a conflict, and said to the team: “friends, if you like, let’s end the discussion for today and settle this matter another time.” everyone dispersed. javid bey knew exactly what to do: meet one-to-one and talk as friends. he invited nadir to the company café under the pretext of a cup of tea; after some small talk he finally came to the point and said calmly, without losing composure: “my son, your behaviour toward kamal today was very wrong. it is unacceptable to accuse someone without knowing the truth of the matter — and in front of others. put yourself in his place. he expected help from us and was met with blame. i am sure kamal hoped we would sit together, think it through, and find a solution as a team.” nadir lowered his head in shame. javid bey’s counsel had worked; it was a good lesson for nadir — he understood he had done wrong. later, whenever the moment arose, he would recall that conversation and say he was always grateful to javid bey. importantly, no one else in the company ever learned of that talk. moral: conflict asks who is right; discussion asks what is right. if you want the truth, discuss — do not quarrel. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Everyone Is the Same Here", "stem": "everyone-is-the-same-here", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "everyone is the same here friendship and human relations a newly appointed young head of the district municipality decided to walk around and learn the situation. after walking the area alone, he entered a teahouse on a side street and ordered tea. just then a twelve- or thirteen-year-old child said: — uncle, shall i polish your shoes? though his shoes were already polished, not wishing to hurt the child’s heart, he said: — all right, come polish them. if you do it well, i’ll give you twice what you ask. — then the child replied: — i polish them the same way for everyone. the official said: — what do you mean? the child answered: — our teacher told us: “children, whatever you do, treat everyone the same — do not discriminate.” with this money i will buy medicine for my sick mother. if i serve you differently, that medicine will not heal her! the young official had learned one of the most important lessons of his life. he was deeply moved and gave the child the largest note in his pocket, and his card as well. he promised to look after this fatherless boy who both studied and cared for his sick mother. he also went to see the teacher who had instilled that honesty in the child, and thanked him separately. so as not to forget what he had heard from the shoe-shine boy, and to apply it always in his work, he had the sentence “everyone is the same here” written on the back of the nameplate on his desk. though it was hard, he always tried to put it into practice. moral: treat a person the same because they are human, not because of their rank. society is changed by those who choose what is right in small moments. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Forgiveness", "stem": "to-forgive", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "forgiveness friendship and human relations forgiving someone does not mean loving them; it does not mean having to talk to them, continuing the relationship; it does not mean accepting that they are innocent, right. forgiving someone means getting out of the prison of disappointment, resentment, hatred, and no longer feeling the pain of the wound inflicted on you. forgive and be free. forgive, but never forget, otherwise you will be hurt again. moral: forgiving is not about justifying the other person - it is about freeing yourself from the prison of hatred and resentment. forgive and be relieved, but do not forget the lesson. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Getting to know and getting to know", "stem": "to-meet-and-to-know", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "getting to know and getting to know friendship and human relations for some, we can only say this: we met - we were happy, we got to know - we were sad. moral: acquaintance is happy, but getting to know is not always pleasant. seeing a person as they are sometimes brings disappointment. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Go away, but come again", "stem": "go-but-come-again", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "go away, but come again friendship and human relations an ottoman custom: when a guest came to the house, the host would place their shoes in front of the door with the toes pointing towards the house. this way, the guest would walk around as if he were walking; this meant \"go away, but come again\". moral: a subtle gesture can convey great kindness. the value given to a person is often seen in small moments. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Gossip", "stem": "gossip", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "gossip friendship and human relations a bitter, impolite man was summoned to the village elder, accused of spreading gossip. — it was a joke, just a joke, — he denied the accusation. — others misunderstood my words, i am not guilty! but the victim of the gossip stood firm: — you have tarnished my clean name. i demand justice. the culprit retreated as he was pressed: — i take back everything i said, i ask for forgiveness. the elder listened silently. it crossed his mind: “this man has not yet realized the gravity of what he has done. time will pass, and he will repeat the same sin again.” then he said to the man: — go and bring a feather pillow to the market square. cut it, scatter the feathers around. then collect all the feathers one by one and bring them to me. if you cannot do this, there is no forgiveness. the man reluctantly accepted the elder's condition. “the old man has lost his mind,” he thought, and then he formed and cut the pillow. the feathers scattered in a single blow and flew in all directions. the man ran to the right and left, chasing the feathers; but no matter how hard he tried, in the end only a few feathers remained in his palm. he lowered his head and returned to the elder: — i could not collect the rest. then he sighed deeply: — just like the words i spread. i cannot take them back. moral: a word that is spread is like a feather thrown to the wind - it cannot be collected again. therefore, stay away from gossip. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "How to overcome insults", "stem": "how-to-ward-off-insults", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "how to overcome insults friendship and human relations (an instructive story from the life of the samurai) there was a wise and wise samurai. he taught his students the secrets of wisdom and martial arts. one day, during the next lesson, a warrior known for his rudeness and cruelty entered the class. this warrior would insult his opponents and get them angry, and then, taking advantage of the mistakes made by his angry opponent one after another, he would defeat him. this time, he used the same tactic, saying a few insults to the samurai to provoke him into a fight. the samurai ignored the insults and continued his lesson. after repeating his insults several times, the warrior, who could not get any response from the samurai, left the class in a state of anger. the students, who were watching this scene carefully, asked their teacher: — master, why did you tolerate these insults? why didn’t you challenge him to a fight? the wise samurai answers: — if someone brings you a gift and you do not accept it, then to whom does the gift belong? — to the previous owner, — the disciples answer. — envy, hatred, and insults should be viewed in the same way. if you do not accept them, such “gifts” remain with those who tried to address them to you. moral: an insult that you do not accept returns to its owner — just like a gift that is returned. do not return evil for evil, but with moderation. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Humanity", "stem": "humanity", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "humanity friendship and human relations he studied for years, worked hard, and finally the day of the exam that would determine his fate arrived... his father, kazim, was a rather demanding man, always closely interested in his son's studies and thought that he would definitely succeed in this exam... kamal entered the building and found the class where he would take the exam. although he was excited when the question and answer sheets were distributed, he was very relieved when the exam began... he answered the questions easily, and he was completely sure of the correctness of the answers... there was half an hour left before the exam ended. he was so confident in himself that... suddenly, he fixed his eyes intently on the classroom window. without thinking about what he saw, he got up and ran towards the door... — go back! otherwise, your exam will be canceled, a year will be wasted, my son, — the teacher who was taking the exam called out to him. but he did not stop, he ran out the back door of the building... his father, who came home a few hours later, first scolded kamal, and then even slapped him in the face. kazim was very upset. even though he asked why he had left the class and why he had left the exam unfinished, kamal kept quiet because he knew that his father would be even more angry... if he had said so, he would not have understood... kazim scolded and reprimanded his son all that year. although kamal was hurt by this situation, he did not listen, he kept his head down and focused on his studies. the following year, he took the exam again and was accepted into the medical faculty... he became a very good doctor. despite living in the city for many years, he asked for a voluntary assignment to a remote village hospital. after working there for a year, his family came to visit him at his house... the exam issue between him and his father had long been forgotten... on a snowy and cold evening, kamal's home phone rang. the caller said that there was a patient in one of the nearby villages. when he picked up his bag and set off, his father kazim did not want to leave his son alone on the mountain roads, so he decided to accompany him. they walked for about an hour under a heavy blizzard and reached that village. they found the house and knocked on the door. an old man opened the door, said that his wife was sick, and invited the doctor in with his father... kamal examined the patient and prescribed the necessary treatment. the old woman thanked the doctor and said in a trembling voice: \"you have helped us in this cold, my son. what would this world be like if there were no good people like you? being a human is not easy... listen.\" \"years ago, i went to the city. suddenly, in the middle of the road, i felt bad, i was in severe pain. there was no one around, the roads were empty. by the way, there was an exam that day. less than five minutes later, a young man approached me in excitement. he took me to the hospital in half an hour in that heat. i was pregnant, and i was trembling with fear that something would happen to my child. the doctor in the admissions department said that if i had been ten minutes late, i could have died... that day my son was born. i learned the name of the boy who brought me to the hospital - he said it was kamal. he said, \"i was in the exam, i saw you when i looked out the window, and when i realized that you were alone, i left the classroom and came to your aid. even though i lost a year because i gave up the exam, i did not regret it at all. i named my son kamal. i raised him with the story of that kind boy, i always advised him to be a person like kamal. now he is a teacher... he tries to raise his students as human beings first and foremost... oh, i wish i could find that boy and get halal from him... anyone can be a doctor, teacher, engineer. the most difficult thing in this world is to be a human being... what a wonderful person that young man was. he did not leave me alone in the middle of the road.\" hearing this, dr. kamal said: \"it is halal, mother, don't worry...\". although his father kazim was a handsome man, he did not want to show his tears. however, when he left the house, tears wet his cheeks. even after he set off, kazim's tears did not stop. he said in a trembling voice, \"kamal, my son,\" but he couldn't continue because he was sobbing. moral: it's possible to have a profession, but the real challenge is to be a human being. a kindness done in a young person's time becomes a blessing that lasts for generations. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "I want a friend", "stem": "i-want-a-friend", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "i want a friend friendship and human relations i want neither wealth nor possessions, i want a true friend. which possession will show mercy to its owner when he dies? i want a friend who will show mercy to me. moral: neither wealth nor possessions are the true wealth of a person, but a loyal friend. a friend who will show mercy to his owner after death is more valuable than any possession. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "I was ashamed, by God", "stem": "i-was-so-embarrassed", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "i was ashamed, by god friendship and human relations it was during the covid-19 pandemic. your sword, as they say, was cutting both the branch and the front. after a long break, the following conversation takes place between two people who meet: — hello, seagull, what's up? it's been a long time since you've seen each other. where have you been? — no! i had covid. — what? what is covid? — what do you mean, what is it? you don't know what covid is? — by god, i have no idea. — so you don't know. so you're saying you've never had covid? — no, by god, i've never had it. — shame on you. but don't you know that in this day and age, it's considered a shame not to have covid? — no, by god, that's not possible, are you right? — may there never be a dawn! — thank you very much for opening my eyes. then let me go and get covid too, or i'll be a disgrace to the community. i was ashamed, by god. moral: it is ridiculous to follow everything the crowd says is \"shameful.\" wisdom lies not in the gossip of the crowd, but in one's own thoughts. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "If the road is not tiring, it is because of your husband", "stem": "if-the-road-does-not-tire-you-it-is-because-of-your-companion", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "if the road is not tiring, it is because of your husband friendship and human relations my mother used to say: \"i married your father when i was very young. i could neither cook a proper meal nor pick up a needle and embroider. one day, when my mother-in-law—your grandmother—was getting ready to go to the neighbor's house, she spread out a blanket in front of me that she had washed and dried, and said, 'you can finish sewing this before i get back.' i started sewing, but i couldn't do it. the needle wouldn't go through the blanket. i started to sew. on the one hand, the needle was digging into my hand, and my eyes were watering from the pain, and on the other hand, my tears wouldn't stop because i didn't know what to say to my mother-in-law. at that time, my father came home for lunch. seeing me crying, he picked up the needle, finished sewing the blanket in a short time, and put it aside. then he ate his meal and went to work. he didn't say anything to my mother-in-law. when the woman returned home, she saw that the blanket was completely ready and was very pleased.\" it's not for nothing that they say: \"if the road doesn't tire you, it's because of your companion.\" moral: it's not the road that tires you or lightens your load, but the person next to you. a true companion makes even the hardest task easier. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Interfering in the lives of others", "stem": "meddling-in-others-lives", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "interfering in the lives of others friendship and human relations may god never leave anyone so unemployed that they interfere in the lives of others! moral: unsolicited advice is not a help, but a burden. interfering in the lives of others under the guise of \"kindness\" often brings harm. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Know your friend", "stem": "know-your-friend", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "know your friend friendship and human relations if you want to know your friend, see him in trouble, in hardship, in strife; touch his advantage once, and then see him again. moral: a friend is known in trouble, in hardship, and in the test of advantage. everyone is a friend in good times; a true friend is revealed in adversity. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Mercy", "stem": "compassion", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "mercy friendship and human relations it was a cold winter night. i was returning home from work. i saw a man in front of me who looked like a drunk. he was leaning back and forth, weakly clinging to a pole next to him. i approached him. he was over thirty. he was very clean and neatly dressed. some of the passers-by were talking loudly about the dangers of alcohol, while others were laughing mockingly. — are you okay, do you need anything? — i asked the man. in a voice that sounded like a groan coming from his lips: — i am sick!… — he said. i held him by the waist with my arm so that he wouldn’t fall and led him to the taxi stand. we started waiting for a taxi. the snow that had stopped in the evening was falling again; there were no signs of life except for stray dogs racing each other on the slowly icy roads. i had given up hope of finding a taxi when a taxi stopped next to us. i explained the situation to the driver and told him that we were in a hurry. we put the patient in the back seat and headed to the hospital. thankfully, the doctors did everything they could right away, administered medication; we waited at the man's bedside. the doctor on duty said that we had at least saved the patient from freezing. the young man, who could not speak yet, just looked at us and smiled. the driver was with me... our patient came to his senses after a while. we put him back in the car and took him home. his wife, knowing that he often fell into diabetic comas, had gone out into the street with her son in a terrible panic. when they saw us, they ran to us excitedly. the family members hugged each other tightly, their eyes full of joy. there was no trace of our fatigue that had lasted for hours; our eyes were filled with the happiness of that family that did not know how to thank us. when we said goodbye, i asked the driver what i owed him. he shook his head: — you are not in debt, on the contrary, you have a debt, my friend! — he said. — you have made me a partner in such a good thing. i am indebted to you for giving this wonderful feeling to a person who has probably forgotten what it is to cry for twenty years. i did not feel the cold of the night as i hugged the driver and left, and decided to walk home. who knows? maybe another person would come across me on my way who needed my help? moral: humanity is built on compassion. compassion is not crying with the crying person, but finding a way to stop the tears. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "People who are in need of you", "stem": "people-who-need-something-from-you", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "people who are in need of you friendship and human relations instead of feeling offended by people who \"only seek you out when they are in need,\" you will feel much more at ease if you think that they are in trouble, that they may need your help, and that is why they are seeking you out. moral: instead of feeling offended by people who only seek you out when they are in need, you will feel much more at ease. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Permission or Apology", "stem": "permission-or-apology", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "permission or apology friendship and human relations there is a saying attributed to rear admiral grace hopper that says, “it’s better to ask for permission than to ask for it.” this saying is used in the sense of “ask for permission first to do something — do it, and then apologize if you’re not happy.” sometimes waiting for permission is a missed opportunity. do what you know is right, and apologize later if necessary. imagine that a company has a clear problem that is slowing down the work process. for example, every week the same information is manually transferred to several different spreadsheets, which takes hours. an employee realizes that this can be solved with simple automation. if he first tries to get permission at length, the issue can be passed from one manager to another, waiting for weeks for a response, and the opportunity can be lost. however, the employee, realizing that the risk is small, sets up a separate test system without damaging the existing data, automates the process, and shows the result. if the system is successful, everyone benefits from it. if someone is unhappy that a procedure was not agreed upon in advance, the employee can say, “i was just trying to speed things up. if i broke a rule, i apologize.” moral: the point here is not to “ignore the rules.” the point is that in situations where the outcome is reversible, the risk is low, and the benefit is clear, expecting too much permission can kill the initiative. sometimes it is better to act responsibly and show results than to expect endless approval. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Relationship with people", "stem": "how-we-treat-people", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "relationship with people friendship and human relations let your relationship with people be like your relationship with fire: don't go too far away lest you freeze; don't come too close lest you burn. saadi shirazi moral: keep your distance in your relationship with people: neither go too far away lest you freeze, nor too close lest you burn. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Ridicule is unacceptable", "stem": "mockery-is-unacceptable", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "ridicule is unacceptable friendship and human relations we should teach our children from a young age that it is unacceptable to ridicule or humiliate other people for their differences or appearance. moral: it is humiliating to ridicule someone else's differences or appearance. we should teach this to our children from a young age. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Spend time with people", "stem": "spend-your-time-with-people", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "spend time with people friendship and human relations my father had to transfer money, so i had to spend an hour with him at the bank. finally, i couldn't wait any longer and said to him: — dad, let's do your banking online. what do you think? — what will this give me, son? — my father looked at me in surprise. — you won't have to waste an hour at the bank to transfer money anymore. the internet will even allow you to do your shopping online. everything will be very easy and convenient, — i quickly replied. i was very excited about the idea of ​​getting my father involved in the world of the internet. my father asked: — if i do what you say, then i won't have to leave the house. is that right? — that's right, dad. i told him that even the groceries he would buy could be delivered to his door, that amazon delivers everything to the buyer's home. my father's answer left me speechless: — my son, in the hour i spent at the bank, i saw four of my friends, and i talked to bank employees who have known me for a long time. you know, i'm lonely... i need to see people. i like to come to the bank dressed up. i have enough time, and i always enjoy live communication. two years ago, when i was sick, the salesman from the nearby store where i always bought fruit came to see me. he was very upset that i was sick, sat by my bed and sympathized with my condition. some time ago, when your mother fell during her morning walk, the grocer in our neighborhood picked her up, put her in his car, and quickly drove her to our house. because he knew where we lived. if everything goes online, won't i be deprived of these human feelings? after all, why should i only communicate with a computer so that everything can be delivered to my house? i want to get to know the person i'm communicating with instead of an \"electronic salesman.\" i want a live connection, a relationship. can amazon do that too? technology is not life. spend your time with people, not devices, son. moral: technology provides convenience, but it does not replace life. the real value is in live communication, in the warmth between people. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The Friendship of Horses", "stem": "the-friendship-of-horses", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "the friendship of horses friendship and human relations there are two horses on a farm near my house. from a distance, they look nothing like the others. but when you get closer, you see something very interesting: one of the horses is blind. the owner has built a safe and comfortable stable for him. this alone is quite an amazing step. as you approach the horses, you will hear a bell. this sound comes from the other small, healthy horse, which has a bell on its bridle, and the blind horse can hear where that horse is and follow it. if you watch these two friends carefully, you will see that the horse with the bell always checks where the blind horse is; the blind horse, in turn, listens to the sound of the bell and slowly follows the healthy horse. every evening, as the horse with the bell returns to the stable, he stops and looks back to make sure that his blind friend is close enough to hear the bell. moral: the lord does not leave us alone in difficult times; when we need help, it sends it through the hands of a human being. sometimes it falls to us to be that helper. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The sound of the doorbell...", "stem": "the-sound-of-the-doorbell", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "the sound of the doorbell... friendship and human relations i was laid off last winter. to make ends meet, i had to work as a newspaper carrier temporarily. since the mailbox of one of the houses i delivered newspapers to was sealed, i had to knock on the door. an old man opened the door. — uncle, why did you seal the mailbox? — i asked. — i sealed it on purpose, — he replied. then he smiled strangely and continued: — i ask you, every day when you bring the newspaper, please knock on the door or ring the bell and give it to me personally. — of course, but it’s inconvenient for both of us, a waste of time, — i was surprised. — no problem, i’m at home every day, — he said and added: — my wife has passed away, my son is abroad, and i live here alone. who knows how long i have left to live? at that moment, i saw the old man’s misty, moist eyes. — have you subscribed to read the newspaper? — i asked. — i never read the newspaper... i only subscribed to the sound of the doorbell. then he clasped his hands together and said: — young man, please do me a favor! here is my son's phone number. if one day you don't hear from me when i knock on your door, call my son and let him know. moral: for some people, one ring of the doorbell, one greeting, is worth the whole world. let's not forget to knock on the door of the lonely. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Those who will not be friends", "stem": "those-you-should-not-befriend", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "those who will not be friends friendship and human relations — do not be friends with an arrogant person! he does not know the state, the mood, the heart; you will be upset. — do not be friends with someone who thinks he is very smart! he talks a lot, talks nonsense, and talks little; you will be upset. — do not be friends with a coward! he does not know courage, the heart does not know, and friends do not know; you will be upset. moral: only regret remains from friendship with an arrogant, self-satisfied and cowardly person. choose your friend according to your heart. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Welcome, sir", "stem": "welcome-my-bey", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "welcome, sir friendship and human relations when my late father hafiz ali was in çanakkale, i was still in my mother's womb, seven months old. i never saw or recognized my father. there was not even a single photograph of him. those were very difficult days. mobilization, occupation, liberation, poverty, hardship... our childhood was spent constantly running around in search of bread, filled with pain. whenever my mother wanted to go somewhere, she would always come to me and say: — son, i'm going to the market. if your father comes, let me know right away! — i'm going to khalangil. if your father comes, let me know right away! — i'm going to the neighbor's. if your father comes, let me know right away! — she would say. my mother waited for my father on the road with her eyes wide open. i grew up, became a business owner, and opened a shop. as always, when my mother went somewhere, she would come to the shop, tell me where she was going, and say: \"if your father comes, let me know, huh!\" he would add. years passed. my mother grew old. despite this, she would still come to me, remaining true to her custom and leaning on her stick: \"if your father comes, call me, huh!\" she would say. one day, she became ill and fell into bed. on her deathbed, she said goodbye to us: \"you took good care of me, grant me your rights.\" she slowly turned to me and said: \"if your father comes, tell him that my mother has always waited for you.\" suddenly, she raised her head, looked towards the door, smiled and said: \"welcome, sir, welcome!\" and gave up her spirit. * * * from the memoirs of cevdet algalp, an old shoemaker living in balıkesir. written by: researcher and writer, geography teacher at bursa çınar anatolian high school mustafa doğru. * * * march 18 is the day of the çanakkale victory. if çanakkale had fallen in 1915, fictitious states would have been established in anatolia, the peoples living here would have been turned into enemies and exterminated, and the country would have become a colony of the british, french and italians. * * * may the souls of all martyrs, including our azerbaijani soldiers who were martyred in çanakkale, rest in peace! may no mother lose sight of the road! moral: a mother's lifelong loyalty and longing are the purest expression of love. the memory of those who died for the homeland and mothers who lost their sight of the road is sacred. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Why do people shout when they argue?", "stem": "why-people-shout-when-they-argue", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "why do people shout when they argue? friendship and human relations one day during class, a teacher asked his students: — why do people shout when they argue? — because they can’t control their temper, — one student answered. — after all, the person you’re arguing with is right next to you, so why shout? can’t you just talk to him calmly? if you’re angry, why should you shout? — the teacher deepened his question. each of the students gave an answer, but none of them satisfied the teacher. finally, he said: — when people are unhappy with each other, their hearts move away from each other. they have to shout to hear each other from this distance. the louder they shout, the further their hearts are from each other. after a moment of silence, the teacher continued: — but what happens when people love each other? they don’t shout, on the contrary, they speak softly. because their hearts are very close to each other, the distance between them is small. but what happens when there is stronger love? then they don't even speak softly, they whisper and get a little closer to each other. as their hearts get closer, they don't even need to whisper — they just look at each other and understand everything without words... my dear students, when you argue, don't let your hearts drift apart, don't say words that could increase the distance between you. because one day this distance may grow so much that you won't be able to find your way back. moral: screaming is the sound of hearts drifting apart. a whisper is enough in love — don't increase the distance between you. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Your friends", "stem": "your-friends", "category": "Friendship and human relations", "slug": "dostluq-ve-insan-munasibetleri", "hay": "your friends friendship and human relations one day, a young man decides to get married. after spending a week preparing for his wedding, one evening he says to his father, “dad, i have a request for you. i can’t do it all by myself. please take this list and call all my friends on it and invite them to my wedding.” “i’m sorry, son!” his father replies. on the day of the wedding, the boy approaches his father angrily: “dad, i asked you to call all my friends!” — son, i did as you asked. — i had fifty people on my list, but i only see fifteen here, — the boy said, expressing his concern again. — son, i called all fifty people one by one. i told each of them that i was calling on your behalf, that you had a problem right now, that you needed the help of your friends, and i asked them to be here at this time, at this place. don’t worry, son. your true friends are here with you today! moral: a true friend is one who is there for you when you are in trouble. it is not the many acquaintances that are valuable, but the few friends who stand the test. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "A Generous Man", "stem": "a-generous-man", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "a generous man morals and character a generous man was once asked: “do you expect the poor to be grateful to you for everything you share with them?” he replied: “i am like a ladle in a cook’s hand. although the food is served with the ladle, it is the cook—not the ladle—who gives it.” moral: those who do good should not consider themselves the true owners of what they give. they are only instruments; the true giver is god. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "A Mature Person", "stem": "a-mature-person", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "a mature person morals and character a time comes when you value the sincerity of old friends more deeply, stop chasing meaningless acquaintances, and choose distance from ignorance and relationships that harm your peace of mind. that is when you realize that you have grown inwardly mature. moral: maturity is the ability to step away from harmful relationships and protect one’s inner peace. knowing whom—and what—to keep at a distance is also a form of wisdom. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "A Meaningless Question", "stem": "a-meaningless-question", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "a meaningless question morals and character one day, a graduate of the university of california, berkeley, visited a rabbi in new york and asked for help studying the talmud. “do you know aramaic? no? what about hebrew? were you at least taught the torah as a child?” the young man answered no to every question. “but i defended a dissertation on socratic logic! i would like to study the talmud to fill some gaps in my education.” “the talmud is one of the greatest works of human thought, but you are not yet ready to study it. still, since you insist, i will give you a logic question. if you answer correctly, i will teach you. two people climb down the same chimney. one emerges with a clean face and the other with a face covered in soot. which one will go and wash?” “you call that a logic question?” the berkeley graduate asked confidently. “the person with the dirty face, of course!” “wrong. the person with the clean face will see the other’s sooty face, assume that his own face is dirty too, and go to wash. the person covered in soot will see a clean face in front of him and suppose that he has remained clean.” “a very clever answer! could you give me another chance?” “all right. two people climb down the same chimney. one has a dirty face and the other a clean one. which will go and wash?” “but we have just discussed that. the person with the clean face will go.” “wrong again. both will wash. the person with the clean face will see his companion’s dirty face and think that he is dirty too. the other will notice that his companion looked at him and then went to wash, so he will realize that his own face must also be dirty.” “i truly had not considered that. may i have one more question?” “very well. i will ask for the last time: two people climb down the same chimney. one has a clean face and the other a dirty one. which will go and wash?” “now it is clear: both of them will go!” “no. neither will go. the person with the dirty face will see a clean face before him and assume that he is clean as well. the person with the clean face will notice that his companion is not going to wash and conclude that there is no need to do so.” growing desperate, the young man begged the rabbi for one more question. the rabbi began: “two people climb down the same chimney. one has a dirty face and the other a clean one…” “neither of them will wash!” the young man answered hastily. “wrong again,” the rabbi said calmly. “as a specialist in socratic logic, think for yourself: how could two people climb down the same chimney together and only one of them emerge with soot on his face? can you not see that the question itself is meaningless? if you spend your life searching for answers to meaningless questions, neither your answers nor your life will acquire meaning.” moral: an answer to a meaningless question cannot lead to truth. a well-formed question is already half the answer. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Attitude Toward a Problem", "stem": "attitude-toward-a-problem", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "attitude toward a problem morals and character is there a problem in your life? if not, there is no reason to worry. if there is, can you solve it? if you can, there is no reason to worry. if you cannot, there is still no reason to worry. moral: either there is no problem, or there is a solution. persistent worry is useless both for what can be changed and for what cannot. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Avoid These Questions", "stem": "avoid-these-questions", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "avoid these questions morals and character do not ask an unemployed person, “are you still without work?”, an employee, “how much do you earn?”, a childless person, “you still have no children?”, or a single person, “when are you getting married?” nor should we embarrass someone by asking, “you did not know that?” what seems like ordinary curiosity to us may touch another person’s deepest wound. moral: some questions wound the heart. every painful question left unasked may lift a burden from someone’s shoulders. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Be Sure of Your Purpose", "stem": "be-sure-of-your-purpose", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "be sure of your purpose morals and character two men were struggling to move a heavy chest through a doorway. after a long effort, they stopped, exhausted and drenched in sweat. one said: “what a heavy chest! we still could not get it inside.” the other asked in surprise: “weren’t we supposed to take it outside?” moral: clarify the goal before beginning the work. otherwise, you may labor for hours while directing all your strength the wrong way. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Being cool-headed", "stem": "to-be-cool-headed", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "being cool-headed morals and character try to assess the situation you find yourself in calmly, without panicking, so that you can make the best decision and turn the situation to your advantage. moral: the best decision is made with a calm mind, not in a panic. cool-headedness can turn even a difficult situation into a good one. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Being Human", "stem": "being-human", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "being human morals and character “it is easy to become a scholar, but difficult to become a true human being,” we often hear. what, then, does being human mean? it means living with virtue, wisdom, civility, justice, honor, and dignity. being courteous, perceptive, respectful, and humble—showing regard for others without praising oneself. thinking before acting, maintaining balance, remaining consistent, and standing upright in adversity. keeping one’s word; refusing to worship money or position; neither stealing nor taking what belongs to another. rejecting lies and slander, never betraying others for personal gain, and defending what is right. remaining free of hypocrisy and hatred, refusing to turn people against one another, and knowing how to love. moral: true worth lies not in knowledge alone, but in humanity. a heart capable of respect also makes room for love and justice. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Being self-confident", "stem": "to-be-self-confident", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "being self-confident morals and character value yourself! do not allow weakness! add strength to your strength! do not lose hope! be knowledgeable! be determined! be of good intentions! protect your dignity! be faithful! moral: value yourself, do not lose hope, protect your dignity. self-confidence is a person's strongest support. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Don't do these", "stem": "do-not-do-these-things", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "don't do these morals and character don't talk about your wealth in front of the poor, your health in front of the sick, your happiness in front of the distressed, your freedom in front of the prisoner, your strength in front of the weak, your children in front of the childless, or your parents in front of the orphan. moral: don't remind the other person of something they don't have. they don't need reminders, they need understanding. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Don't open your mouth", "stem": "dont-open-your-mouth", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "don't open your mouth morals and character if you're not going to open your mouth to say something nice, don't open it at all. moral: the power of words lies in using them appropriately and for good. if you don't have anything worth saying, silence speaks louder. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Don't say \"I didn't deliver, I couldn't do it\"", "stem": "dont-say-i-couldnt-deliver-or-i-couldnt-manage", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "don't say \"i didn't deliver, i couldn't do it\" morals and character days replace each other. some end their days happy, some sad. but no matter what, don't say \"i didn't deliver, i couldn't do it\" for the things you couldn't do today. most importantly, don't despair. the next day, as soon as the sun rises, the whole day is yours. moral: every day is a new page. you can't change yesterday, but how you write today is in your hands. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Education or character", "stem": "education-or-character", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "education or character morals and character the king asks his minister: — minister, is education important or character? the minister answers without thinking: — character, my king. the king sends a proclamation to the country, orders to find the best animal trainer and bring him to him. they find such a person and bring him. the king asks the man: — how long can it take you to train a cat to serve the assembly with a bowl in its paw? — six months, my king. six months pass. the man is called to the palace with the cat he has trained. the king asks: — did you teach it? — i did, my king. the entire palace gathers. the cat begins to serve them with a bowl in its paw. when the cat approaches the minister, the king asks: — minister, is education important or character? before the minister can answer the king, he takes out a mouse he has been holding ready in his pocket and drops it on the ground. seeing the mouse, the cat drops its paw and runs after it. the vizier answers the king: — character, my king. moral: education does not change character; when the opportunity arises, the true nature is revealed. knowledge is good, but character is more important. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Everything is in our own hands", "stem": "everything-is-in-our-own-hands", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "everything is in our own hands morals and character (from indian folklore) a young boy gets the idea of ​​“playing a game” with an old monk. he catches a bird. he takes the bird in his hand and hides it behind his back, and asks the monk: — o wise old man, tell me, is the bird in my hand alive or dead? but he thinks to himself: if the old man says “it is alive”, i will strangle the bird and kill it; if not, “it is dead”, i will open my hand and let the bird fly away, and thus scare it away. the wise old man thinks for a while and says: — my dear son, the answer to your question is in your own hands. the boy stops and lowers his head. the old man continues: — war and peace, punishment and pardon, good and evil - all this is in your own hands. the path of a person’s life is determined not by his mind, but by his intentions. remember that every person is capable of doing both good and evil. but there is always a choice, and this choice is in our own hands. moral: both good and evil are human choices. our destiny is determined not by our minds, but by our intentions. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Explaining a problem", "stem": "explaining-ones-sorrow", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "explaining a problem morals and character the person who explains their problem simply is a problem solver; the person who explains it beautifully is a \"literary person\", the person who explains it with a \"lover\", and the person who covers it with a smile is a \"sage\". moral: people carry their problems in different ways: some talk, some write, some keep quiet and smile. the deepest problems are often hidden in the deepest silence. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Giving up", "stem": "to-give-up", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "giving up morals and character \"it is a beautiful quality to give up!\" from those who do not know the value of things, those who trample on sincerity, those who change for profit, from arrogant and boastful people, those who are envious, gossipers, those who make a habit of lying, those who do not know what decency is, those who nullify their values, those who turn a blind eye to ignorance, those who despise their homeland, deny their nation, those who forget their native language, those who are deprived of maternal love, paternal honor, and filial love... \"it is a beautiful quality to give up!\" from those who think they are irreplaceable...! moral: giving up on some people is not weakness, but self-respect. walking away from those who do not know their worth is a beautiful quality. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "He wouldn't give so little", "stem": "he-would-not-have-given-so-little", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "he wouldn't give so little morals and character a king gives a gold coin to a beloved child he meets, but the child doesn't take the money. when the king asks why he didn't take it, the child says: — when i go home, my mother and father ask, \"where did you steal this money?\" — you say that the king gave it to you, — says the king. — then they won't believe it at all. they say that if the king had given it to him, he wouldn't have given so little, — replies the child. the king likes this answer very much and he gives the child a bag of gold. moral: great things begin in the family, in proper upbringing. a child who grows up honestly becomes an honest person tomorrow. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Honey or dry bread", "stem": "honey-or-dry-bread", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "honey or dry bread morals and character it is better to sit with a scholar and eat dry bread than to sit with an ignorant man and eat honey. moral: the person you sit with will either elevate you or degrade you. the simple bread of a scholar is better than the sweetness of an ignorant man. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "How can I get rid of my thoughts?", "stem": "how-can-i-escape-my-thoughts", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "how can i get rid of my thoughts? morals and character one day, a man came to visit one of the scholars of his time and asked: — how can i get rid of the painful thoughts that i am a prisoner of? instead of answering him, the scholar stood up, hugged a nearby pole and began to shout with all his might: — separate me from this pole, save my life from it!… —. the man, looking at him in surprise, asked: — why are you doing this? i thought you were a wise man and came to ask for help. but i see that you do not look like a wise man. you yourself held the pole, it did not hold you. let go of it, and your life will be saved! the scholar let go of the pole and said to the man: — you yourself answered your own question. it is not you who holds thoughts, but you! let them go so that your life will be saved. moral: you yourself hold the thoughts that oppress you; it is in your hands to let them go. change your perspective, and your burden will lighten. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "I am now aware", "stem": "i-am-aware-now", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "i am now aware morals and character from someone who has fallen hard in life: i no longer try to make anyone love me. i am aware that those who don't like me can always find an excuse. i now know my worth and i am aware that i don't have to prove myself to anyone. i am now aware that i don't want to give my life to people who are complacent, arrogant, and conceited. i am now aware that those who want something have a purpose, and those who don't want something always have a \"but\". i no longer share my secrets with someone who gossips, because i am aware that a gossiper cannot keep a secret. i no longer try to convince or lead stubborn people, because i am aware that it is impossible to turn a stubborn person from his or her path. i am now aware that i want people who lie, make empty promises and don't keep their promises out of my life. i am no longer able to forgive, to be cruel and harsh. i am aware that i should value people who are only able to apologize when they make a mistake. i am now aware that i am happier with the person i am \"myself\" with. moral: true maturity is not about changing others, but about knowing who to let into your life and who to stay away from. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "I was busy cleaning", "stem": "i-got-busy-with-cleaning", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "i was busy cleaning morals and character today i was busy cleaning... and in my whole body... i cleaned all my muscles, nerves, veins, even my blood... i threw away all the garbage that had accumulated deep down... first of all, i threw away the disappointments... i didn't even look at what they were when i threw them away. you should have seen this... how much space they occupied inside me... in their place, i carefully placed forgiveness. i diligently sowed the seeds of forgiveness on every disappointment... i threw out my jealousy... how jealous i am... it was very easy. i was happy... it was as if i had found something i had lost... thank god there was no grudge or hatred in my heart... i don't know how i cleaned it... it was my turn to be afraid... at first i was even afraid to take it out... how i got used to living with them... i couldn't understand how i could get used to so much pain and anxiety... every day new fears, caused by new worries, were accumulating inside me... how much i had postponed my happiness and hopes... if i had given them this attention at that moment, if i had thought about them every day and added a hope, if i had given them without receiving, if i had loved them without expecting, if i had realized the love and strength inside me first of all, i wouldn't have needed such cleanings... i had difficulty cleaning my fears... they were intertwined like chains and almost locked together... but i expelled them without regretting that i had once kept them alive inside me like a nightmare. i know that if i got angry with my fears, if i shouted at them, they would come back and attack me. today i was busy cleaning... i planted joy, i planted trust, i planted love... i planted giving without receiving, loving without being loved, sharing... i planted madness, naturalness, forgiveness inside me... i planted love in every cell of my body... i planted enthusiasm, excitement, silence... all the best ideas come to me in silence... i planted acceptance... not bowing down, but accepting as it is... moral: true cleanliness is in the heart: throwing away disappointment, fear, and resentment and planting love, forgiveness, and hope in their place. the one who cleans his inner world finds peace. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "I Would Not Trade It for Anything", "stem": "i-would-not-trade-it-for-anything", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "i would not trade it for anything morals and character a day, a beautiful day, a sunny day — i would not trade for anything. for its sake i do not love war, for its sake i do not love oppression, for its sake i do not love lies. i know they cannot live in the sun, i know they cannot live side by side with love — neither injustice, nor fear, nor hunger… necati cumalı moral: on a beautiful, sunny day there is no place for strife, oppression, or lies. in the light, injustice, fear, and hunger cannot live. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "It was not your right", "stem": "that-was-not-your-right", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "it was not your right morals and character in the battle of çanakkale, only the wounded who could survive were given morphine injections. one of the seriously wounded brought to dr. tarık nusret was his own son. seeing the impossibility of saving his son's life, the father did not give him an injection. two hours later, hugging the lifeless body of his martyred son, the father whispered in deep shock: \"forgive me, my son, it was not your right.\" moral: sometimes the greatest justice is when a person goes against his own interests. a father who knows the truth will not give his son such an unfair share. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Lying", "stem": "telling-lies", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "lying morals and character — are you lying? — yes. — what kind of lie is that? — i only tell that lie to myself. that is, every day i tell myself that “you are stronger than everyone, more educated than everyone, more capable than everyone,” etc. — maybe this is true? — no, such a claim cannot be true. because there are people stronger than me, and there are people more capable than me. — then why do you tell yourself this lie? — believe it or not, this lie gives me great motivation. it encourages me to be stronger, more educated, and to work harder on myself. moral: sometimes the kind words you tell yourself — “you can do it” — become the force that drives you forward. a person is as strong as he believes in himself. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "May your face always smile", "stem": "may-your-face-always-smile", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "may your face always smile morals and character a person who sells vinegar with a smile will have more customers than a person who sells honey with a frown. moral: a smiling person is more loved than a frowning one. a smile is the cheapest but most powerful charm. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Never complain about any day you live", "stem": "do-not-complain-about-any-day-you-have-lived", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "never complain about any day you live morals and character never complain about any day you live: good days make you happy, bad days give you experience, the worst days teach you a lesson, the best days give you memories. moral: every day has its share: good days make you happy, bad days give you experience, and the worst days give you a lesson. never complain about any day. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "No one listens to a person who complains constantly", "stem": "no-one-listens-to-constant-complainers", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "no one listens to a person who complains constantly morals and character a wise man taught a group of people who complained about the same problems over and over again. he told a funny joke; everyone laughed heartily. he told the same joke a second time; only a few people laughed. when he told the same joke a third time, no one even moved their lips. complaining about the same problems over and over again instead of finding a solution is similar. no one listens to a person who complains constantly. moral: repeated complaints, like repeated jokes, quickly become boring. find a solution to the problem; no one will listen to you if you whine over and over again. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Not every pain is told to people", "stem": "not-every-sorrow-is-told-to-people", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "not every pain is told to people morals and character not every pain is told to people. the day will come, they will hit you with that pain. moral: don't tell every secret to everyone. one day they may hit you with that pain - choose who you tell. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Other People's Opinions", "stem": "other-peoples-opinions", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "other people's opinions morals and character one day, the human being lost the comma (,). what he said got mixed up. another day, he lost the period (.). his thoughts got long and he couldn't separate them. another day, he lost the exclamation mark (!). he lost his joy, his anger, all his emotions. then he lost the question mark (?). he forgot to ask questions, he had to accept everything as it was, without questioning. another day, he lost the colon (:). the ability to explain and explain disappeared. when he reached the end of his life, he had only one quotation mark left - and it contained only other people's opinions. moral: a person who loses his own values ​​and roots one by one is filled with foreign ideas. if you don't protect your spiritual roots, you will eventually cease to be yourself. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Our qualities can be our enemies", "stem": "our-qualities-can-become-our-enemies", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "our qualities can be our enemies morals and character the enemy of a peacock is its own tail... it is its sweet tongue that traps a nightingale. it is its fancy fur that traps a fox... some of the qualities we see and love in ourselves can actually be our weakness and disaster... moral: sometimes the trait we boast about is our weak point. the peacock's tail betrays the nightingale, the tongue - do not rely too much on your superiority. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Phrases to avoid", "stem": "expressions-you-should-stay-away-from", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "phrases to avoid morals and character avoid these phrases, especially in the family, and never use them against your spouse: “don’t talk nonsense!” this phrase makes your spouse feel that their thoughts and feelings are worthless, misunderstood, and belittled. instead, if you gently say, “i don’t think like you, i don’t completely agree with you on this issue,” you will show that you take their opinion into account and maintain respect between you. “don’t exaggerate!” this phrase may sound like a sign that your spouse’s view of events is wrong; he or she will feel criticized. instead, by asking, “can you explain a little more clearly why you are worried and what you are feeling?” you will take a step towards eliminating the misunderstanding that has arisen. “look at his or her husband (wife)! he or she does this, and you do that?” this phrase is very dangerous because when you say it, you are bringing third parties into your family relationship. comparing your spouse to someone else will make them distrust you. they will think that you are thinking the same thing, and they will feel hurt and unloved. instead of making comparisons and analogies, clearly state your expectations. “you don’t understand!” this is an insult—you are putting your spouse down. if you think that they won’t understand you on this topic or don’t want to share your opinion, it is more appropriate to say, “i don’t want to talk about this, don’t be offended, let’s talk about something else” or “this probably doesn’t interest you.” “don’t go there, don’t talk to her, don’t do this, don’t wear that, don’t talk like that, bring this, take that!” your spouse is not your subordinate. it is better to respectfully discuss with them what you don’t want or don’t like and to avoid conflict. instead of using impassioned statements, use requests (please, please, etc.). moral: words can hurt the person we love the most. in the family, we should use the language of respect and requests, not orders and insults. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Strength and Pain", "stem": "strength-and-sorrow", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "strength and pain morals and character my god! give me either strength for the pain i suffer, or pain as much as i can bear. muhammad fuzuli moral: a person should either ask for strength to bear his pain, or pain according to his strength. do not fall under a burden that exceeds his strength. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The Flower of Honesty", "stem": "the-flower-of-honesty", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "the flower of honesty morals and character a prince was preparing to ascend the throne. but according to the rules, he had to get married first. in order to choose a suitable candidate, he had the most beautiful girls in the country summoned to his presence. the daughter of one of the servants in the palace was also in love with the prince. she also told her mother that she wanted to go to the prince's presence. although her mother tried to dissuade her daughter from this idea, the girl did not change her mind and insisted on going to the presence. the expected day came. the young beautiful girls who gathered at the palace wore their most elegant clothes, decorated themselves, and did their best to impress. the prince gave each of the girls a seed, instructed them to plant it in a pot and return to him in six months. he said that he would choose the one who grows the most beautiful flower and marry her. the girls took the seed and returned home excitedly. the girl in the palace also planted the seed she had received in the pot. despite her diligent care, not a single sprout grows in the soil. she tries every way, consults with gardeners, but still nothing. when six months are up, the girl is forced to come to the palace with an empty pot. unlike her, the other girls come with pots full of beautiful flowers. the time of the selection comes. the prince enters the hall, walks among the girls, and looks at the pots one by one. finally, he announces in a loud voice that he has chosen the servant's daughter. everyone is stunned. the other girls protest this decision: how can a girl who comes with an empty pot be chosen as a candidate for the bride? the prince explains the reason for his decision as follows: — this young lady grew and brought me the most valuable flower. the name of that flower is the flower of honesty. all the seeds that i distributed to you were fake, it was impossible for them to bloom. moral: the most valuable decoration is honesty. a person is exalted not by his appearance, but by having a true word and a clear conscience. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The Goose to Be Taken", "stem": "the-goose-to-be-plucked", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "the goose to be taken morals and character on a very cold winter day, the king decided to leave the palace in disguise and take his chief vizier with him. after walking for a while, they came across an old man working on the edge of a stream. the man was dipping the skins in his hands in water and beating them. the king greeted the old man: — assalamu alaykum, o piri-fani. — assalamu alaykum, o sardari-jahan, — and the old man accepted the greeting. the king asked: — what did you do at six? — unless you add six to six, it doesn't make thirty-two, — he replied. the king asked again: — did you never get up at night? — we got up, my king... but it was good for the people... the king laughed and asked his last question: — if i send a goose, would you go? — of course, i will go, and without shouting, — the elder replies. the king and the chief vizier leave the man and continue on their way. the king asks the chief vizier: — do you understand what we are talking about? — no, my king, — the chief vizier replies in a distressed manner. the king gets angry: — if you do not understand what we are talking about by this evening, i will have your neck cut off. after the frightened chief vizier takes the king to the palace, he rushes back to the edge of the valley and sees that the man is still working there. — what did you talk about with the king? — the chief vizier addresses the elder. the man looks at the chief vizier from head to toe beautifully: — forgive me, i will not tell the weather. give me a hundred gold coins, and i will tell you, — he says. the chief vizier gives the hundred gold coins. — you greeted the king as “sardari-jahan”. how did you know that he was a king? — i am a tanner. no one but the king could wear the fur he is wearing. the vizier scratches his head: — what did he mean by “if you don’t add six to six, it won’t be enough for thirty-two”? the man also takes a hundred gold coins to answer this question: — king, didn’t you work during the six months of heat, and now you are working on this winter day? i said: if we don’t work not only during the six months of heat, but also in the six months of winter, we won’t be able to find bread. the chief vizier continues: — what did he mean by “didn’t you ever get up at night?” the man takes another hundred gold coins: — king, don’t you have any children? he asked. yes, but they are all girls; they got married, they are destined for someone else, i said. the chief vizier lowers his head: — and he said, “if i send a goose”… what did he mean? the man laughs and says to the chief vizier: — you find her too, — he says. moral: a wise word is revealed to those who understand it. not everyone understands what they hear — some answers a person must find for themselves. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The strongest shield", "stem": "the-strongest-shield", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "the strongest shield morals and character the strongest shield in life is distance. moral: sometimes the strongest way to protect yourself is to keep a healthy distance. too much closeness to everything and everyone burns you. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The three best things", "stem": "the-three-best-things", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "the three best things morals and character father: — son, we should strive for these three things in life: to eat the best food, to sleep in the best bed, and to live in the best house. son: — father, but we are poor, how can we do this? father: — if you eat when you are hungry, this will be your best food. if you sleep tired after working hard, this will be your best sleep. if you give people a place in your heart, this will be your best home. moral: the best food is found in hunger, the best sleep after hard work, and the best home is found in hearts. happiness is not in opportunity, but in perspective. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Think, Speak, Act Positively", "stem": "think-speak-and-act-positively", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "think, speak, act positively morals and character think positively, because your thoughts become your words. speak positively, because your words become your actions. act positively, because your actions become your habits. develop positive habits, because your habits become your values. develop positive values, because your values ​​become your destiny. moral: thoughts become words, words become actions, actions become habits, and habits become destiny. the beginning of a positive destiny is positive thinking. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Try to think like this", "stem": "try-to-think-this-way", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "try to think like this morals and character don’t say, “i’m not good at this job.” say, “maybe there’s something i don’t know? i’m sure if i refresh my knowledge and work on it, i can do it.” don’t say, “i’ve given up on this job.” say, “maybe i can solve this problem in a different way? i’ll try, maybe it will work.” don’t say, “this job is too hard.” say, “it’s hard, but not impossible; it just takes a little extra time and effort.” don’t say, “i’m satisfied with the work i’m doing, i can’t do better.” say, “i can work on myself, improve, and achieve better results.” don’t say, “i made a mistake, i wish i hadn’t.” say, “it’s not the end of the world; mistakes help me learn.” don’t say, “he’s so smart, i can’t be like him.” say, “i can learn how he did it and achieve the same success.” moral: changing your mindset is the first step to changing your life. saying “i can't” closes the door, saying “i can learn” opens up new possibilities. failure is not the end, it is an opportunity for growth. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Turning difficulties into opportunities", "stem": "turning-hardship-into-opportunity", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "turning difficulties into opportunities morals and character sometimes in life, things go very smoothly, and sometimes we face unexpected difficulties. the important thing is to be able to turn the difficulties we encounter into opportunities. moral: the same difficulty breaks one and strengthens another. it is up to a person to turn difficulties into tools, not weapons. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Who Handles Honey Licks His Finger", "stem": "who-handles-honey-licks-his-finger", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "who handles honey licks his finger morals and character a beekeeper filled containers with the honey he had collected and brought them to a grocer to sell. they struck a bargain on the price and began to weigh the honey. because the grocer had only a one-kilo weight, he broke off pieces of honeycomb by hand and weighed the honey kilo by kilo. but before placing each piece on the scale, he never failed to lick his honey-smeared fingers. once, twice, three times… the beekeeper saw that some of the honey never reached the scale — it stayed on the grocer’s fingers and went to his tongue. he frowned and showed his displeasure. the grocer paid no attention and kept weighing, licking his fingers as he went. at last the beekeeper’s patience ran out: — uncle, this won’t do — you’ve licked away half the honey! still licking his honeyed fingers, the grocer smiled quietly and replied: — brother, i mean no harm. who handles honey licks his finger. moral: even a small share in a job usually brings some gain from it. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Words for the mouths, not dust for the feet", "stem": "dont-be-words-for-mouths-dust-for-feet", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "words for the mouths, not dust for the feet morals and character words for the mouths, not dust for the feet! moral: preserve your dignity: live in such a way that you neither fall into disrepute nor become as worthless as the dust underfoot. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "You can't go down a well with his rope", "stem": "you-cannot-descend-a-well-on-his-rope", "category": "Morals and character", "slug": "exlaq-ve-xarakter", "hay": "you can't go down a well with his rope morals and character once upon a time, there was a master named ali who was busy making rope from flax fibers. since he didn't do his job well, the ropes he made often turned out rotten. he was even called \"master ali, the rotten rope.\" master ali's ropes often broke and caused many accidents. one day, a sheep fell into a deep well. an acquaintance, intending to go down the well to get his sheep out, asked the sheep's owner for a rope, but he didn't like the rope he was given: — i know this rope. this is master ali's work. you can't go down the well with his rope, he complained. — don't be unfair. why, you can go down the well with master ali's rope; but i can't say whether you can get out of there with the same rope, i can't say,\" the sheep's owner replied with a smile. moral: a person gains or loses trust in his name by the quality of his work. once trust is shaken, it is very difficult to restore it. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Communication", "stem": "communication", "category": "Word, silence, and communication", "slug": "soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet", "hay": "communication word, silence, and communication if you often interact with the elderly and children, you will see experience in one and innocence in the other. moral: experience is learned from the elderly, innocence from the child. every age has a value to give to a person. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Language", "stem": "the-tongue", "category": "Word, silence, and communication", "slug": "soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet", "hay": "language word, silence, and communication language is a flower, it grows in the mouth, if you don't water it and protect it, it will wither and disappear. moral: if the language is not protected, it will wither and disappear like a flower that is not watered. keeping your mother tongue alive means using it lovingly every day. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Silence", "stem": "silence", "category": "Word, silence, and communication", "slug": "soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet", "hay": "silence word, silence, and communication the answer to some questions is silence. either it's not the time for an answer, or the interlocutor doesn't deserve an answer! * * * darvish jalal ruhavi moral: the most correct answer to some questions is silence — either it's not the time, or it doesn't deserve the answer in front of you. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Silent corridor", "stem": "the-silent-corridor", "category": "Word, silence, and communication", "slug": "soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet", "hay": "silent corridor word, silence, and communication there were two organizations working together — one was the sponsor, the other was the project development agency. the project team worked tirelessly for months, carefully polishing the documents, and one day, when everything was ready, they sent the materials to the sponsor and began to wait for a response. days passed, weeks passed. the result — silence. the uncertainty within the team grew, spreading like a shadow along the corridor: “have the documents arrived? have they been reviewed?” no one had any clear information. and requests to the sponsor only brought vague, vague answers. a few months later, the team decided to go directly to the sponsor’s office to clarify the matter. there, the truth finally emerged: “yes, the documents arrived. but we did not accept them. because the submission form was incorrect and the official procedure was not followed.” the team froze in place. “why didn’t you tell us that?” they asked. “we could have fixed everything in a short time.”... frustrated, the employees returned to the office and complained to their management: “if we had been actively monitoring the process, checking in on the status regularly, and communicating clearly, this misunderstanding would never have happened. we wasted time, wasted energy, and lost trust—all because the hallway between us was silent.” moral: when communication is unclear, even the best work can go unnoticed. when there is no consistent follow-up, even the strongest partnership can falter. in collaboration, the greatest enemy is often not failure—it is silence. bakhtiyar sirajov"}, {"title": "Speaking the right words", "stem": "saying-the-word-in-its-place", "category": "Word, silence, and communication", "slug": "soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet", "hay": "speaking the right words word, silence, and communication he who knows how to speak the right words will never have to apologize. moral: he who speaks the right words will never have to apologize later. the right word at the right time is precious. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Tell what you know", "stem": "say-what-you-know", "category": "Word, silence, and communication", "slug": "soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet", "hay": "tell what you know word, silence, and communication tell what you know, but don't teach wisdom. listen carefully to what is said, but don't assume everything is true. just as being silent does not mean that you know nothing, talking too much does not mean that you know much. it is foolish to belittle a person, and to extol him excessively is cowardice. moral: speak correctly, listen correctly, and you will be able to remain silent. neither humiliate nor exalt a person - see him as he is. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The Conversation of the Candles", "stem": "the-candles-conversation", "category": "Word, silence, and communication", "slug": "soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet", "hay": "the conversation of the candles word, silence, and communication four candles were burning in a room. the atmosphere was so quiet that you could hear the candles talking to each other. the first candle said: “i am faith! but no one is helping me to burn. i think i will soon go out.” and so it was. its flame began to fade rapidly, and finally faith went out completely. the second candle said: “i am faith! what a pity, i am no longer needed by anyone. that is why there is no point in me burning anymore.” as soon as he finished speaking, a light breeze blew out faith completely. the turn came for the third candle. he said in a sad voice: “i am love! i have no strength left to burn anymore. people have forgotten me, my value has disappeared. even my closest people have forgotten to love.” love could not stand it any longer and went out. at that moment, a child entered the room and saw that all three candles were not burning. “why don't you burn? weren't you supposed to burn forever?” he began to cry. then the fourth candle spoke: “don't be afraid, as long as i burn, we can light the other candles again. i am hope!” the child was happy and with shining eyes, he took the hope candle in his hand and lit the other candles one by one. moral: when faith, loyalty and love go out, it is hope that lights them up again. if we keep the light of hope alive, the other values ​​will also come alive. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The Knower and the Unknower", "stem": "the-one-who-knows-and-the-one-who-does-not", "category": "Word, silence, and communication", "slug": "soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet", "hay": "the knower and the unknower word, silence, and communication (chinese proverb) the one who does not know and knows that he does not know — yearns for knowledge. teach him! the one who knows and does not know that he knows — is asleep. wake him up! the one who does not know and does not know that he does not know — is ignorant. stay away from him! the one who knows and knows that he knows — is a leader. follow him! moral: teach the one who yearns to learn, awaken the one who is heedless, stay away from the ignorant who thinks he knows everything, and follow the one who really knows. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The Shepherd's Word", "stem": "shepherds-word", "category": "Word, silence, and communication", "slug": "soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet", "hay": "the shepherd's word word, silence, and communication once upon a time, in a village, there lived a poor but honest shepherd named hasan. one day, the village chief called him and said: — hey hasan, i have heard that you are very familiar with the pastures of the mountains. i will entrust you with the herd this summer. if my animals return healthy and fat in the fall, i will give you a cow. hasan agreed. he grazed his herd all summer, rain or shine, and guarded it without blinking an eye at night. when fall came, the herd returned to the village fatter and healthier than before. hasan came to the chief, hoping to receive the promised cow. the chief smiled mockingly and said: — i had no intention of giving you a cow, that word was just said to encourage you to work. if you are already poor, what will you do with a cow? hasan's eyes filled with tears. but he answered calmly and with dignity: — you are right, chief, i am poor. but i have a conscience and i am the master of my word. you may be rich, but if your word is worthless, then you are truly poor. these words spread from mouth to mouth in the village. the master's name fell out of respect, and people began to avoid doing business with him. but he understood, albeit late: the value of a person is determined not by his wealth or position, but by his loyalty to his word. moral: a person's true wealth is not his wealth, but his word, conscience, and trust. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "What is a word?", "stem": "what-is-a-word", "category": "Word, silence, and communication", "slug": "soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet", "hay": "what is a word? word, silence, and communication a word is a wound; a word is a pearl. moral: a word can be both a wounding thorn and a precious pearl. what makes the difference is how you use it. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Words and silence", "stem": "word-and-silence", "category": "Word, silence, and communication", "slug": "soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet", "hay": "words and silence word, silence, and communication sometimes a person says more by keeping quiet. because words are not just sounds — they are a mirror of intention. if a word will break a heart, if a sentence will extinguish hope — it is not worth saying. if the words you are going to say will not warm a heart, if they will not cause a smile — be silent. remember... not every word blooms. some are like thorns — they hurt the one who touches them. silence is wisdom. sometimes it is the most valuable answer. if a word is born from the heart, it turns into wisdom; if it comes out of the mouth without thought, it causes harm, wounds. silence. because silence also speaks — sometimes more deeply, sometimes more honestly. moral: not every word blooms; some hurt like thorns. silence is wiser than words that will not bring good. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "You need to call the shepherd", "stem": "the-shepherd-must-be-called", "category": "Word, silence, and communication", "slug": "soz-sukut-ve-unsiyyet", "hay": "you need to call the shepherd word, silence, and communication a student who wants to complete his education after many years turns to his master to receive his blessing. his master: — yes, but i have one condition. i will ask you a question to see if you have properly learned the knowledge i have given you over the years. if you answer the question correctly, i will know that my efforts have not been in vain, and i will give you my blessing. the student confidently says: — come on, my master, ask. his master: — my son, imagine that you are going to your village and on the way a pack of rabid dogs belonging to a shepherd attacks you. you are in danger. what will you do to save your life? — i will bend down and pick up a few stones from the ground and throw them at the dogs, and i will chase them away. — but, my son, if you throw stones at one, you throw stones at two — you will not be able to handle them all. they will join together and tear you apart. — then i will run away. — be sure that they will run away from you quickly and catch you. so what? the student realizes that he is helpless and asks his master for help. his master: — my son, there is only one way out of this situation: you must call the shepherd and ask him to stop his dogs. the dogs will freeze in place as soon as they hear the shepherd's voice. moral: not every problem can be solved by force. sometimes taking a step back and looking at it differently is the shortest way out. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "A Different Path", "stem": "a-different-path", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "a different path wisdom and life lessons one day, a cow returning to its shed had to cross an untouched forest. unable to choose a sensible route, the animal wandered uphill and downhill, creating a long, twisting, and difficult trail. the next day, a dog came to the same place. to reach the other side of the forest, it followed the cow’s trail. then came a flock of sheep. seeing a ready-made path, the shepherd led the flock along it. before long, people began using the trail as well. they complained about its difficulty as they turned left and right, ducked beneath branches, and twisted their way through the bushes. yet no one thought to look for a better route. through constant use, the trail gradually became a road. unaware that the other side of the forest could be reached in only half an hour, people and heavily burdened animals spent three hours following the cow’s winding path. years passed. the small road first became the town’s main route and eventually its most important street. although it was the least efficient route imaginable, everyone merely complained about the traffic. watching it all, the old and wise forest pitied the people who blindly followed the familiar road without ever asking whether it was the right one. moral: the path everyone follows is not always the best one. sometimes those who step beyond habit and search for a new route reach their destination more quickly and easily. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "A Human Being Emerged", "stem": "a-human-emerged", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "a human being emerged wisdom and life lessons i joined thought with the heart—and love emerged. i gathered love through labor—and loyalty emerged. i divided bread according to conscience—and justice emerged. i brought love, labor, conscience, loyalty, and justice together—and a human being emerged. — yaşar kemal moral: when love, labor, conscience, loyalty, and justice come together, a true human being is formed. humanity is the union of these values. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "A Life Lesson", "stem": "a-life-lesson", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "a life lesson wisdom and life lessons while teaching his cub about life, a father wolf pointed to some sheep: “their meat is delicious.” then he pointed to the shepherd: “but his staff can be very bitter. be careful.” seeing the dog beside the shepherd, the cub exclaimed happily: “father, look! that one resembles us.” the father replied: “run the moment you see it, my son. we have suffered most from those who look like us but are not truly one of us.” moral: sometimes the greatest harm comes from those who resemble us while concealing different intentions. recognizing hypocrisy in time is the first step toward protecting oneself. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "A More Effective Way", "stem": "a-more-effective-way", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "a more effective way wisdom and life lessons a hunter walking through a forest came upon an unusual scene. a woodcutter had been hacking at a fallen tree for a long time with enormous effort. his face was drenched in sweat and his whole body was tense. the hunter approached and asked: “why is the work going so slowly and painfully? your axe is completely blunt. why not sharpen it?” the woodcutter stared at him in surprise: “what are you talking about? where would i find time to sharpen my axe? i still have twenty more trees to cut!” moral: hard work is valuable, but unexamined effort can be wasteful. sometimes a short pause to sharpen the tool makes all the work that follows faster and easier. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "A Windmill Turning in Still Air", "stem": "a-windmill-turning-in-still-air", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "a windmill turning in still air wisdom and life lessons if a windmill turns when the air is still, someone must be blowing on its sails. eskimo proverb moral: no outcome arises without a cause. the force behind an event may be invisible, yet its influence becomes clear in the result. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "At the Bookstore", "stem": "at-the-bookstore", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "at the bookstore wisdom and life lessons after a long search, a medical student found the book he needed at the bilge bookstore in bursa. when he saw the price on the back, his smile vanished: he could not afford it. hiding his disappointment, he leafed through the pages, found the section assigned by his professor, and—seeing that the owners were busy with customers—read it quickly before leaving. for the next month, the student returned every other day and studied among the shelves. one week before his final examinations, he came again, but the book was gone. he searched every shelf; it seemed to have vanished. as the pale-faced young man was about to leave, the owners stopped him: “please sit down. we have something to tell you.” one bookseller reached beneath the counter and produced the book: “a customer recently asked its price and appeared ready to buy it. i removed it from the shelf and kept it for you. you may continue reading it here.” after completing his studies and becoming a doctor, the young man never forgot the two booksellers. he never charged patients who could not afford treatment. moral: a small act of kindness can leave a lifelong mark on the heart. those who receive kindness often carry it forward to others. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "At the Doctor’s Appointment", "stem": "at-the-doctors-appointment", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "at the doctor’s appointment wisdom and life lessons i was waiting for my turn at a doctor’s office. having missed breakfast at home, i bought a sandwich and apple juice from the café and began to eat. a boy sitting near the window was watching me closely. his expression seemed to say, “i am hungry too.” feeling sorry for him, i returned to the café and bought him a sandwich and juice. the boy ate eagerly, while i felt pleased with what i believed was a good deed. at that moment, his mother emerged from the registration room. seeing the scene, she hurried over in alarm: “what have you done? the doctor told me to bring him fasting. i took time off work, and we traveled a long way. now we will have to begin everything again tomorrow.” the woman could not hold back her tears. i stood frozen: while believing i was helping, i had undone an entire family’s effort. moral: judgment based only on emotion and assumption can lead us to the wrong decision. asking about the situation before helping is itself part of compassion. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Blind people with eyes", "stem": "the-blind-who-have-eyes", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "blind people with eyes wisdom and life lessons the world is full of blind people who think they see because they have eyes. moral: seeing is not done with the eyes, but with understanding. a person who looks but does not see, or hears but does not understand, is truly blind. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Blind Well", "stem": "the-blind-well", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "blind well wisdom and life lessons one day, a villager's donkey falls into a blind well (a well whose water has dried up). hours pass; finally, the owner and his neighbors, hearing the donkey's painful cries, rush to the rescue. he says, he says, and finally they come to the conclusion that it will be impossible to get it out of the well, and they see the only solution in filling the well with earth. each takes a shovel in his hand and begins to carry earth from the surrounding area and pour it into the well. the poor animal shakes off the earth that has fallen on it and throws it under its feet, and thanks to this earth it gradually rises, finally managing to climb to the mouth of the well. the people are very surprised when they see the donkey come out of the well safely. moral: shake off the earth that has fallen on it and throw it under its feet, and rise. the way out of helplessness is not in whining, but in shaking and struggling. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Diderot effect", "stem": "the-diderot-effect", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "diderot effect wisdom and life lessons one of the 18th century french writers, the enlightener denis diderot, falls into a huge debt swamp. his miserable situation reaches the ears of the russian empress catherine. the empress makes diderot a kind offer to get out of this swamp: she buys his library for money and gives it back to him. moreover, she pays diderot 25 years of salary in advance for working in that library. of course, paying this advance at such an unexpected moment meant making a fortune for diderot. now diderot was free from all debts and relieved. one day, a friend gives him a red velvet robe (luxurious outerwear). it all starts from there... denis puts on the robe. while working enthusiastically at his desk, he suddenly thinks that this beautiful velvet robe and the table do not match. intoxicated by the cash in his safe, he immediately goes to change the table and buys a magnificent one. the dress and the table now match each other... but then he sees that the old carpet on the floor does not suit either the dress or the table. he runs out and buys a carpet that suits his money and himself. still, there was a feeling of unease inside him. the armchairs, cupboards, chairs, pictures on the wall, and the decorations in the room no longer match each other, they seem old... everything is starting to sink in... time comes, time goes... diderot changed everything in the house, from the needle to the thread. it did not take diderot long to realize the situation. he realized that the reason for his return to the starting point was his own inexhaustible greed. as a result, the author wrote his famous work on this subject, \"i regret parting with my old dress.\" then he wrote a short sentence that will go down in history: \"while i was the master of my old dress, i became the slave of the new one.\" moral: after a person acquires a new thing, he feels the need to change the rest - this is called the \"diderot effect.\" there is no end to greed; it is better to be the master of the old than the slave of the new. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Dreaming", "stem": "to-dream", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "dreaming wisdom and life lessons god does not make you dream of something that he will not grant. moral: every dream that comes to your heart has a wisdom. god does not put a desire in your heart that he will not grant - do not lose hope. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Free time", "stem": "spare-time", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "free time wisdom and life lessons some people talk to you when they have free time, while others take their free time to talk to you. moral: time is the only wealth that never comes back. appreciate the person who willingly spends it for you. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "From the memories of a woman from Istanbul", "stem": "from-the-memoirs-of-a-lady-from-istanbul", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "from the memories of a woman from istanbul wisdom and life lessons it was 1919. istanbul was completely under british occupation. i had just finished high school. i was a beautiful girl, and ambassadors came to our house. one day, a young man who introduced himself as a lawyer came to our house with his ambassadors. i saw this handsome, tall boy and liked him. we got engaged. i loved my fiancé... i wanted to build a happy home, so i wove small towels by lamplight until morning and prepared my dowry. one day, a rumor spread in the neighborhood. they said, “ayesha's fiancé was not a lawyer at all, he was crying all day long by carrying a coffin in front of the mosque.” i was devastated. my father and i went to the mosque, watched from a distance, and saw that he was really carrying a coffin. i was shocked. we returned the engagement, and we broke up. five years passed. i was married, i had a child. our country was already free. one day i met him in the city. my son was with me. he stopped when he saw me. he said hesitantly, “if you have time, i would like to offer you a cup of tea.” i agreed. we went into an office. it was a law firm; his name was written on the sign on the door. his assistants were working inside. “are you really a lawyer?” i asked. “yes,” he said. “but if you are a lawyer, why were you carrying a coffin in front of the mosque?” he bowed his head and said, “forgive me.” “istanbul was under occupation. we were surrounded by british soldiers everywhere, they were checking everything and everyone very carefully. and we were sending coffins full of weapons to anatolia, to help the national forces, under the guise of funerals. it was a vital task for the country. i couldn’t tell you that…” moral: don’t judge by appearance; behind every behavior there may be a truth that you don’t know. our freedom is owed to the sacrifice of the selfless. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Hayat’s Life Story", "stem": "hayats-life-story", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "hayat’s life story wisdom and life lessons when my mother left our home, my father, and us, i was six and my brother was eight. even at that age i understood why she left my father, but i could not understand why she left us. would a mother abandon her children? my father used to beat my mother. he beat me and my brother too. i came to believe that all men beat their wives and children… only after my mother left did i learn that a home without a mother is nothing but four walls. just as no one said in the mornings, “wash your hands and face and come — breakfast is ready,” i learned that no one asked “are you hungry?” even if you went days without food. of all i learned, this burned me most: when there is no mother’s scent, children grow up in an instant, whatever their age. i grew up at six. about ten days after my mother left, the police came to our house. they said she had taken her own life. in her hand she had been clutching an envelope. on it was written: “to be given to my daughter and my son.” i could not read then — how was i supposed to read it? my brother read it. i cried as i listened. my brother cried too. when my mother left us, she had gone to her father’s house. her father flew into a rage. apparently a woman was supposed to stay in her own home; even if her mouth were full of blood, she was supposed to say, “i drank red sherbet.” my mother begged my grandfather: “let me stay with you for a month; then i will find a way, take my children, and begin a new life.” oh, no, no, no! a woman living alone? she would cheapen my grandfather’s honour for a penny; he would not be able to show his face; better if his daughter were dead. that evening my mother, without thinking, put the clothesline around her neck. my grandmother told me this years later on her deathbed, when i was nineteen. when my father heard of my mother’s death, he was not upset at all. he said he would put us in an orphanage. from then on we would live there; that would be our home. brother and sister might be parted, forget each other, and years later fail to know each other — so we were both to keep mother’s letter. but before i could ask how the two of us would keep one letter, my brother cut it in half with scissors. he gave me the beginnings of the sentences. since i had the openings, once i learned to read i would have to guess the rest. my brother had already memorized the letter anyway. when my aunt heard we were to be sent to an orphanage, she came. she told my father: “a girl is not given to an orphanage. i will take hayat and raise her myself.” living with my aunt, i understood why: a girl meant an unpaid servant ordered about the house. not once did my aunt call me by my name. my name became “come-here”: “come-here, fetch water… come-here, wash these dishes…” once a month my brother visited me at my aunt’s. he said he was fine in the orphanage. i said i was fine too — i did not want to upset him. did he also say “i’m fine” so i would not worry? i never dared ask. i had started school. i was the first in the class to learn to read. how could i not — i was going to read my mother’s letter. the part i still had began: “hayat, my beautiful daughter, i…” every night in bed i tried to finish that sentence: “hayat, my beautiful daughter, i love you so much.” “hayat, my beautiful daughter, i miss you terribly.” “hayat, my beautiful daughter, i am waiting for you.” according to the words i added, i built dreams. in my dreams i was very happy. does anyone ever dream of bitterness? i finished middle school. my aunt said i would not go to school anymore. but i wanted to study — to stand on my own feet and live under one roof with my brother. that summer the wife of uncle hasan, the neighbourhood grocer with three children, died of cancer. i was heartbroken. what would the three children do — how would they find a mother’s scent? why did mothers die? would those three forget their names as i had, and become “come-here” too? while i was grieving for uncle hasan’s children, it came out that i was betrothed to him. it turned out i had been sold for eight bracelets and three thousand liras. because i was too young for a civil marriage, forty days later our religious marriage was performed. i was fifteen. i was uncle hasan’s wife. i had three children — two, five, and six. and my aunt insisted we must have a child of our own… i was supposed to have a child so my place would be secure and uncle hasan would not look at other women. the first slap from uncle hasan came because i called him “uncle hasan.” does a woman call her husband “uncle”? when i lost my mother at six, i learned to keep silent. so i did not ask, “does a man call a fifteen-year-old girl his wife?” — i stayed quiet. when uncle hasan hit me, all three children clung to their father’s legs: “don’t hit sister hayat — she plays with us, she tells us stories,” they begged. i was those children’s older sister. what i called stories were my dreams. when uncle hasan left the house, i stood before the mirror — i would learn to say “hasan.” every time i said “hasan,” i saw in the glass his thinning hair, the glasses slipped down his nose, his swollen belly. every “hasan” made me ashamed, as if i had sinned, and i could not call him that. while i practised, uncle hasan’s six-year-old son came: “sister hayat, mother used to call father ‘bey’ — you say ‘bey’ too,” he encouraged me. “bey” — of course, “bey” would be better. i bent and kissed dark-eyed sami, who listened to my dreams as if they were fairy tales. i called five-year-old elif and two-year-old zahra and began to tell them a story. that day i began: “once upon a time there was a six-year-old girl named nagil. nagil had lost her mother. she searched everywhere; when she could not find her, she went to the land of dreams. there nagil was so happy that she never returned to the real world.” in my tale nagil was always happy, always smiling. every day i told the children that story. we were very happy. uncle hasan was good too. i now called him “bey.” from time to time he grew angry, but i understood why: instead of a woman he had bought, for eight bracelets and three thousand liras, a storytelling sister. my brother came to visit. each time he wanted to give me his half of mother’s letter. i would not take it. i was afraid that if i read the other half, i would fall from the land of dreams into a cruel real world and be unhappy. all my dreams were built on that other half. by forty, the children who had listened to my stories had grown. sami had become a doctor, taken a post in another city, and left our house. when he felt low he would call: “sister hayat, tell me a story.” elif had become a teacher and married. i had a granddaughter. they badly wanted to name her hayat; i would not allow it. elif said, “then let her name be nagil.” so my granddaughter’s name was nagil. my youngest, zahra, became a veterinarian. “sister hayat, whenever an animal is restless i tell it a story, and it calms down,” she would say. zahra married too, and i had a grandson. when i turned forty-five, uncle hasan — my bey — passed away. i grieved deeply. his last words were “forgive me — make it lawful.” with all my heart i forgave him. he was a good man. but my claim — against those who see no wrong in marrying off fifteen-year-old girls, and those who support them; against the blind minds who call it dishonour when a daughter they know is beaten every day seeks divorce, and those who support them; against those who show no mercy to children left helpless and hopeless like wounded birds, and those who support them — i do not forgive! moral: blind custom and a false idea of “honour” can destroy a woman’s life, a child’s life. those who look away from early marriage, violence, and neglect are partners in that oppression. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "If I don’t do it these days, the world won’t fall apart", "stem": "if-i-dont-do-it-these-days-the-world-wont-collapse", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "if i don’t do it these days, the world won’t fall apart wisdom and life lessons when i asked one of our employees what stage of the implementation of a task entrusted to him was, his answer deeply disappointed me: “if i don’t do it these days, the world won’t fall apart…” i thought for a moment: yes, the world won’t fall apart. but what about the trust between us? won’t that fall apart too? wouldn’t this relationship harm our cooperation, mutual respect, and the common work we do? the issue wasn’t that the task was delayed by a few days. the issue was a person’s attitude towards the commitment they took on. because responsibility is not only about doing the job, but also about keeping your word, justifying the trust of the other party, and ensuring that people can rely on you. i think it’s not right to approach any commitment you take on in such an irresponsible way. what was even more regrettable was the way this approach was expressed. to me, such an answer sounds at least rude and shows that the other party's time, effort, and trust are not sufficiently valued. i must admit that these words broke my heart. at that moment, i realized that our cooperation could not continue as before. because the foundation of any professional relationship is trust. when trust is shaken, cooperation is also seriously tested. what disappointed me most was that my colleague realized the wrongness of what he said, neither apologized for it nor fulfilled the task. as a result, my trust in him was shaken, so i was forced to provide a solution to the problem in another way. this incident once again showed me that the most valuable resource in organizations is time, not money, nor technology. the most valuable resource is the trust that people have in each other. it can take years to earn trust, but sometimes it can be lost in just one sentence. moral: trust is the most valuable asset of organizations. it takes years to earn it, and losing it is like a sentence - those who keep their word protect this wealth. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "If the door in front of you doesn't open, it's not your door", "stem": "if-the-door-before-you-wont-open-it-is-not-your-door", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "if the door in front of you doesn't open, it's not your door wisdom and life lessons this story takes place between a company manager and his employee. one day, jamal comes to his boss, salim bey, and tells him that he has heard that there will be a promotion at work, and that he has expectations of his own. although salim bey says that he is satisfied with jamal's work, he tells him that he is fired, saying, \"you are not a good fit for us.\" jamal has to leave the company in disappointment. two years later, they meet again. after greeting each other kindly and checking in on each other, jamal tells his former boss that he has started a new company and that his business is going well. salim bey says that he is very happy. jamal expresses his gratitude to salim bey as follows: \"actually, i would like to thank you for not wanting me to stay in that company for which i was not a good fit, but for releasing me. this has meant a new opportunity for me, it has encouraged me to do a job that i really like, and i am very happy with my situation now.\" moral: every closed door can actually be a sign of a better path. sometimes, the deprivation of \"good\" leads to \"better.\" stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "It is futile", "stem": "it-is-in-vain", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "it is futile wisdom and life lessons it is futile to advise a fool, to argue with an ignorant person, to befriend a hypocrite. moral: choosing who you spend time with is actually choosing how you will live your life. arguing with an ignorant person, befriending a hypocrite is a wasted life. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "It Won’t Go to Waste", "stem": "it-wont-go-to-waste", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "it won’t go to waste wisdom and life lessons a kindness done from the heart, a deed kneaded with love, a word spoken in sincerity, conduct wrapped in courtesy, a smile coloured with affection, a step taken for truth and justice, respect shown to moral values — never goes to waste; besides lighting the path of those around you, it also helps your own life become bright, sound, and beautiful. moral: kindness, words, and deeds from the heart never vanish; they light others’ way and brighten your life. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Lecturer and mehter", "stem": "the-lecturer-and-the-groom", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "lecturer and mehter wisdom and life lessons a lecturer is invited to a village to educate the population. the man enters the hall and sees that only one person is sitting inside. after greeting, he addresses this man: “no one else has come. what do you say, should we go to the lecture or not?” the only “listener” says: — teacher, i am a mehter, i have twenty horses. if all the horses run away and only one remains, i will still feed it, give it water and feed. the lecturer begins his speech and speaks at length. after finishing, he asks the mehter: — how was it, did you like my lecture? the mehter replies: “i am a horse breeder, i did not understand anything from what you said. but i can only say that i will feed the water and feed of twenty horses to one horse and not make it break.” moral: speaking well is not about talking too much, but about the idea being understood and assimilated by the listener. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Life is short", "stem": "life-is-short", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "life is short wisdom and life lessons life is short. spend it with people who make you happy and feel loved. moral: life is short; spend it with people who make you happy and feel loved. don't waste your time on the wrong people. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Liver", "stem": "liver", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "liver wisdom and life lessons where do the expressions using the word “liver” come from—“jan-jiyar” meaning a very close, sincere, and kind friend; “jiyarguşam” meaning a very beloved child; “agqıyar” meaning a coward, a coward, a helpless person; “jiyarparam” meaning a very beloved person, etc.—originate? ancient people were impressed by the warm and bloody appearance of the liver, so they considered warm blood to be life, and its absence to be death. for this reason, the liver has long been considered the center of life. for example, the babylonians believed that the human soul resided in the liver. since the liver was thought to primarily represent emotions in some cultures, the liver came before the heart in idioms and writings. in ancient greece, virtue and related qualities such as courage and determination were attributed to the liver. if a person had a healthy liver, he was considered virtuous—he was perceived as someone who followed his principles, overcoming all obstacles. moral: language does not arise by chance; behind every word there is a history, a belief. our words are the memory of our past. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Living in the past", "stem": "living-in-the-past", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "living in the past wisdom and life lessons living in the past is like walking backwards. because you don't look ahead, you will always stumble and fall, unable to change what's behind you or open the doors ahead. moral: walking backwards is stumbling. you can't undo the past — turn your face, look forward. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Newton's 2nd Law", "stem": "newtons-second-law", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "newton's 2nd law wisdom and life lessons a physics teacher walks into the staff room after class and complains to his colleagues: \"how stupid these students are! i explained newton's 2nd law to them three times, and finally i understood it myself, but they still didn't understand.\" moral: he who wants to teach someone else must first fully understand it himself. explaining without understanding leads neither the teacher nor the learner to the same conclusion. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Nothing Is Ever Truly Lost", "stem": "nothing-is-ever-truly-lost", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "nothing is ever truly lost wisdom and life lessons there is no such thing as \"losing\". there are only successes and lessons learned from life. moral: there is no such thing as \"losing\" - you either win or you learn. every failure is actually a lesson. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Pawn and king", "stem": "the-pawn-and-the-king", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "pawn and king wisdom and life lessons don't look down on a pawn! one day, it will become a king. don't trust it just because it is a king! one day, it will become a match. moral: don't look down on anyone because they are small, don't brag about your position. a pawn can become a king, and a king can become a checkmate. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Planting millet in the bottom", "stem": "sowing-millet-at-the-bottom", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "planting millet in the bottom wisdom and life lessons one day, the king went on a walk with his courtiers. in a village on the way, he saw an old man planting seedlings in his field. he called out to the elder from a distance: — father, why are you busy planting these seedlings? mashallah, you have not lived your life; you will probably not be able to eat the fruit of these seedlings. the elder replied to the king: — it is not necessary for us to eat the fruit of these seedlings, my son. just as we ate the fruit of the seedlings planted by those before us, those after us will also eat the fruit of the seedlings we plant. this answer pleased the king and he ordered the elder to be given a bag of gold. the elder pointed to the bag full of gold and said: — did you see, my son? the seedlings we planted have already borne fruit. this answer pleased the king and he ordered the elder to be given another bag of gold. the old peasant was not an ordinary man - he was a wise and wise man: — my son, the saplings that everyone else plants bear fruit once a year, but the saplings we planted have already borne fruit twice. the ruler, who liked this answer, ordered the nobleman to be given another bag of gold. but this time the vizier intervened and tried to dissuade the ruler from his thoughts: — oh, my sultan! we must leave here as soon as possible. if this nobleman leaves like this, instead of planting saplings in his field, he will plant millet in the state treasury. moral: a person does not live only for himself. we ate what those who came before us planted; we must plant for those who come after us. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Properties of water", "stem": "the-properties-of-water", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "properties of water wisdom and life lessons look at the properties of water: — if it makes friends with a tree, it keeps it alive; — if it comes into contact with fire, it extinguishes it; — if it encounters impurity, it purifies it; — if it mixes with flour, it turns it into dough, making it ready for baking; — if it unites with the sun, a rainbow is formed; — if it flows, it brings life; — if it stagnates, it turns into a swamp and begins to smell. the human heart is also like water: it is very lively in communication, but lifeless in solitude. moral: the human heart is like water: it comes to life in communication, stagnates and withers in solitude. it is other people who keep a person alive. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Teacher, Hello — Do You Remember Me?", "stem": "teacher-hello-do-you-remember-me", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "teacher, hello — do you remember me? wisdom and life lessons — teacher, hello! do you remember me? you were once my teacher at school. — yes, i remember. you were my pupil in primary school. i’m glad to see you again after so many years. what do you do? — i’m very glad to see you too. i have become a teacher like you. — wonderful. why did you decide to become a teacher? — because you once inspired me as a teacher; you were an example in life. so i decided to become a teacher too — to help children grow and to inspire them to succeed. — i’m glad you think so and chose teaching. but you said i inspired you and was an example. what exactly do you mean? — when you taught us, one boy in the class had been given a fine watch by his parents. my parents could not afford one for me. i liked the watch very much, and one day at the long break i stole it from his pocket. after the break he told you it had been stolen. you asked the class to return it. when no one spoke, you closed the door and said you would search all the boys’ pockets. i thought to myself that this would be the most shameful moment of my life. the boys lined up along the wall; you told everyone to cover their eyes with their hands. one by one you checked every pocket. but after you took the watch from my pocket, you went on checking the others, and in the end you told no one who had stolen it. all year you said not a word of it to me or to my parents. you simply returned the watch to its owner. that day you did not let my dignity be crushed; you kept me from being branded a “thief” or a “liar” for a childish act, and you forgave me. that event was a great lesson for me. i understood that a true teacher is not only one who gives knowledge, but one who instills right values — who does not merely punish a mistake, but teaches learning from it, and who binds the pupil to life. at that very moment i decided that one day i too would be a teacher like you. the teacher listened and was astonished. the young man asked: — don’t you remember this? have you forgotten that you took that watch from my pocket? — i remember the incident; i simply did not know the watch had come from your pocket. — how could you not know? you found it in my pocket yourself! — because when i checked the pupils’ pockets, i had closed my eyes too… moral: to protect a person’s dignity without shaming them — by closing your eyes — is true teaching. a person whose dignity is kept learns from the mistake and rises. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The dead are the survivors", "stem": "the-dead-remain-in-life", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "the dead are the survivors wisdom and life lessons when one of two people who love each other with all their heart dies, the real \"dead\" is the one who survives. moral: when one of two people who love each other with all their heart leaves, the real \"dead\" is the one who is left behind and yearns. love is even deeper in separation. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The most beautiful places in the world", "stem": "the-most-beautiful-places-in-the-world", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "the most beautiful places in the world wisdom and life lessons the most beautiful places you can be in the world are in the thoughts, prayers and hearts of your loved ones. moral: the most beautiful place a person can be is in the hearts and prayers of their loved ones. the true place is not an address, but love. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The road to the cotton field", "stem": "the-road-to-the-cotton-field", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "the road to the cotton field wisdom and life lessons it was a time when we didn't know how to read or write properly. a teacher came to our village. he didn't just say that a teacher had come, but someone who would become a brother and father to us. when he learned about the miserable condition of our school and us students, and that we had to work in the cotton field after graduating from school, his eyes filled with tears. there was a road in our village. aydin teacher asked us where it went. we said that that road went to the cotton field. there was another road that extended from the village. he also asked where it went. since we had never left our village in our lives and thought that the world consisted only of our village, we could not answer this question. that day, he also asked us what we wanted to be when we grew up. we all remained silent because we didn't know what to answer... our teacher taught us how to read and write in a short time. days passed, and we were already writing and reading comfortably. one day, aydin teacher brought us many letters. he said that these letters came from doctors, engineers, scientists, and people with various professions. according to him, those people would become our pen pals. those people whom we had never met for years wrote letters to us. in their letters, they talked about their professions, the meaning of life, and the importance of science and education. until we received those letters, we had never even dreamed of studying science or having a profession other than cotton growing. because we thought that there was always only one path in front of us - the path leading to the cotton field. thanks to our pen pals, our lives changed. we learned where the other path led. thanks to the great effort of teacher aydin, we achieved great success years after graduating. now some of us are preparing to become doctors, some are engineers, and some are teachers. years later, another letter came to us: “my children... always have dreams. they will take you where you want to go. all you have to do is make an effort, work hard, and be determined to achieve your goals. i congratulate you all from the bottom of my heart. i knew you would succeed and succeed”. “your pen pal, teacher aydin.” we were all in shock. after all, it was our native teacher aydin who wrote the letters that changed our fate for years. moral: a teacher not only showed the way to children, but also created new paths for them. for a person who can dream, the world no longer consists of just one path. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The secret to living well and living long", "stem": "the-secret-of-living-well-and-longevity", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "the secret to living well and living long wisdom and life lessons half your food, double your walks, triple your laughter, and set no limits on your love. tibetan proverb. moral: eat little, walk a lot, laugh a lot, love endlessly. the secret to a long and happy life lies in simplicity and love. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "There is no need to be smart when everyone is crazy", "stem": "no-need-to-be-sane-when-everyone-is-mad", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "there is no need to be smart when everyone is crazy wisdom and life lessons one day, a wise man receives news from above: \"o man, from tomorrow the taste of water will change, and those who drink it will go crazy.\" the man quickly runs home, fills the jugs and bowls he can get his hands on with water, and sits down. the next morning, he sees that everyone is really starting to go crazy, and he only drinks from the water he has collected. this goes on for a week or two. the man can't stand it anymore and goes outside. as soon as he says a few words to those he meets, everyone mocks him: \"man, are you crazy? go to a madhouse!\" the poor man returns home, breaks the bowls and jugs, drinks from the ordinary fountain, the water that everyone drinks, and goes to bed. moral: the crowd may condemn you for knowing the truth. protect your mind, but don't tire yourself out by trying to prove your worth to the public every moment. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "This boat is also empty", "stem": "this-boat-is-empty-too", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "this boat is also empty wisdom and life lessons my master used to say: when i was young, i often went to the lake, i used to dream and think in solitude. i had a small boat. i would sail in that boat for hours, finding peace in the world of thoughts. one day, early in the morning, when the sun broke, i was sitting in my boat as usual, deep in thought. suddenly, someone's boat crashed into my boat and shook my entire dream world. i became very angry. i opened my eyes with the intention of cursing the owner of that boat and saw that the boat that crashed into me was empty. there was no one in the boat to whom i could vent my anger. so i closed my eyes and plunged into the world of beautiful dreams again. when the sun rose, i felt that my soul had regained complete peace. the empty boat was a lesson for me. since then, whenever someone tries to hurt me, i simply say to myself: — this boat is also empty. moral: if a person wants to maintain his peace, he should not look for evil intentions behind every incident. sometimes what worries us is just a coincidence; it is our choice whether to magnify it and turn it into anger. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Three Questions", "stem": "three-questions", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "three questions wisdom and life lessons the king called the vizier to him and said: — i am asking you three questions. if you do not find the answer by tomorrow, you will be expelled from the palace. the first: what does god eat? the second: what does god cover? the third: what does god do? no matter how much the vizier racked his brains, he could not find the answers to the questions. in the evening, he returned home in a state of dismay. he had a clever and shrewd servant. the servant saw that his master was in a bad mood and asked the reason, and the vizier told him what had happened to him. the servant said: — i know the answers to all three questions. but i will tell you two now and one tomorrow. know that what god eats is the sorrows and sorrows of his servants. what god covers is the faults of his servants. the next day, the vizier went to the king and told him these two answers. the king said: — that is true. but did you find these answers yourself, or did someone teach you? the vizier tells the truth: — my wise and clever servant taught me. hearing this, the king orders the vizier to take off his robe and give it to his servant. thus, the vizier takes off his robe and puts it on his servant, while he himself puts on his old clothes. the vizier, stunned, asks the servant: — and what is the third answer? what does god do? the servant smiles and says: — don't you know yet? god makes the vizier a servant and the servant a vizier in an instant! moral: a vizier today can become a servant tomorrow, so no one should be confident in his seat! stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Three Scenes", "stem": "three-landscapes", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "three scenes wisdom and life lessons a young man on a journey encounters three strange scenes on his way. the first strange scene he encounters is this: several people were sowing wheat in a field. the wheat grains sprout and grow as soon as they hit the ground, and at that moment they produce a harvest, but instead of harvesting the harvest, the farmers burn the field. the second strange scene was this: a man tries to lift a large stone from the ground, but he cannot. he can lift it only after placing another on top of that large stone. when he places a third stone, he lifts it more easily. no matter how much the young man looks, he cannot understand anything and continues on his way. the third strange scene he encounters on the road is this: a man is riding on the back of a sheep, and several men are climbing on its shoulders. the sheep runs away quickly; no matter how many men run after them, they cannot catch up. no matter how much the young man ponders what he sees, he cannot understand any meaning and arrives at a city. he sees a radiant old man near the entrance gate and greets him. the old man accepts the greeting, stops him and starts a conversation. the boy tells what he saw along the way, and the old man explains them as follows: — do you know who those farmers, the wheat field and the people who burned it are? those who first do good, and then talk about the good they have done here and there, belittling its value. — the meaning of the scene of the man lifting the stone is this: committing a sin for the first time is very difficult for a person. but if a person does not repent after committing a sin, subsequent sins are very easy for him. a sin without repentance is an open door for another sin. — as for the sheep and the people on its back, do not forget that the sheep is an animal of paradise and carries those who ride on its back to paradise. the first to ride on a sheep are scholars. those who hang on the shoulders of scholars are believers from all walks of life. those who run after them are unbelievers and irreligious. moral: goodness that is talked about and its value is reduced to nothing, sin without repentance, and following a true leader—each of them is a hidden lesson in life. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Three statues", "stem": "three-statues", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "three statues wisdom and life lessons there were good friends between the rulers of two neighboring countries. one day, one of the rulers decided to send the other a birthday present. he invited the best sculptor of his country to his presence and instructed him to make three statues of gold, one inch high, exactly like each other. but there would be a difference between these statues that only the ruler and the sculptor knew. the statues were ready and were sent to the neighboring ruler on his birthday. the letter accompanying the gift said: “i congratulate you on your birthday with these three golden statues. at first glance, the statues may seem exactly the same. but one of them is more valuable than the other two. when you find that statue, let me know.” the ruler, who received the gift, first weighed the statues on a scale. it turned out that they weighed the same, down to the gram. other investigations were also carried out, but without results. the ruler summoned all the people in his country who knew art to his palace. everyone carefully examines the statues, but cannot find a difference between them. days pass, and no solution is found. finally, one day, the ruler is informed that there is someone who can solve this problem. he is immediately found and brought to the palace. the man carefully examines the statues and asks for a thin wire. he puts the wire in the ear of the first statue - the wire comes out of the other ear. then he puts the wire in the ear of the second statue - the wire comes out of its mouth. finally, he puts the wire in the ear of the third statue, but the wire gets stuck inside. thus, the difference between the statues is revealed. the ruler writes a reply to his neighbor who sent the statues and sends it: “these three statues symbolize three different types of people. what he hears:” — he takes it from one ear and gives it from the other; — he immediately lets it out of his mouth; — he is able to keep it in his heart. the most valuable person is the one who can keep what he hears as a secret in his heart and only tells it when it is appropriate and at the right time. thank you very much for this valuable gift. moral: the most valuable person is the one who can keep what he hears as a secret in his heart and only speaks when it is appropriate. keeping your word is also a form of wisdom. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "To be full or to be lost", "stem": "to-be-full-or-to-be-gone", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "to be full or to be lost wisdom and life lessons we are full by sharing, we are lost by dividing. yunus emre moral: we become stronger when we are divided, but we are lost when we are divided. to preserve unity, we must keep our knowledge and spirituality healthy. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "To seek", "stem": "searching", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "to seek wisdom and life lessons to seek something where it is not is not to seek, but to deceive oneself — like seeking loyalty in disloyalty, literary shamelessness, and mercy in dishonesty. moral: to seek something that is not where it is not is to deceive oneself. do not expect everything from everyone — know a person as much as he or she can give. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Two Cups of Water", "stem": "two-bowls-of-water", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "two cups of water wisdom and life lessons in ancient times, there was a ruler who was famous for his wealth. this ruler would take a part of his wealth with him wherever he went and boast about it. the ruler had a wise teacher whom he trusted the most. one day, the ruler asked him: “you are a wise man. tell me, what do you think of my rule and wealth?” the wise man looked into the ruler’s eyes and said: “suppose, my ruler, you are in a very hot, endless desert. would you give half of your wealth to someone who gives you a cup of water so that you don’t die?” “of course i will,” the ruler replied. the wise man said: “suppose, after a while, your thirst increased a little. would you give the other half of your wealth for the next cup of water?” the ruler thought for a moment and said: “of course i would give it to you so that you don’t die.” the wise man smiled and said: “my lord, do not boast too much, for the value of your wealth is only two pots of water.” moral: the value of wealth depends on the circumstances: in the desert, all your wealth is worth only two pots of water. do not boast about your wealth - its value is fleeting. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "What do I need?", "stem": "what-do-i-need-it-for", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "what do i need? wisdom and life lessons sultan suleiman the magnificent (1494–1566; ascended the throne in 1520 and ruled until the end of his life) raised the ottoman state to the highest possible level of his time, but from time to time he wondered: “i wonder if one day the sons of osman will also face a decline, will it collapse?” as in many matters, he decided to reveal this anxious thought to his foster brother, the famous scholar yahya efendi. he wrote and sent the following letter to yahya efendi, whose discovery and miracle he believed in: “you are knowledgeable about divine secrets. please clarify these for us. in what case does a state collapse? what will be the fate of the sons of osman? can a day come when this state will go bankrupt?” yahya efendi, who read this letter from the powerful sultan of the time, suleiman the magnificent, responded very briefly: “what do i need, my sultan!” the sultan, who read this answer in amazement in the topkapi palace, could not make sense of it, and his anxiety increased even more. because a person like yahya efendi would not give such a simple answer to a serious issue. “i wonder if there is a meaning in this answer that we do not know?” the sultan thought and stood up and went to yahya efendi’s court in beşiktaş. this time, the sultan repeated his request in a reproachful manner, saying: “brother, what can i do, answer my letter. take the matter seriously.” yahya efendi: “my sultan, is it possible not to take your request seriously? i have thought about your request carefully and expressed my opinion clearly.” “okay, but i did not understand anything from your answer. you simply said what i need. this seems to mean that you are not involving me in these matters.” yahya efendi engraved these instructive words on the pages of history: “my sultan! if oppression spreads in a state, if injustice rises to the throne and those who hear it say, 'what do i need?' and then the shepherds, not wolves, eat the sheep, those who know the truth remain silent and hide it, if the cries of the poor, the needy, the destitute, and the destitute rise to the heavens and no one hears it except the stones, then the end of the state is in sight. in such cases, the treasury of the state is emptied, the trust and respect of the people are shaken. the sense of obedience that is the means of order disappears, and the sense of respect for the people disappears. collapse and bankruptcy are thus inevitable...” the old sultan, who began to cry while listening to what was being said, bowed his head and confirmed these things. then he left, thanking god that there was a scholar in his country who could give him such advice. moral: a society that sees oppression and says, “what do i need?” falls into decline. turning a blind eye to injustice is also complicity in injustice. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "What does it mean to be late?", "stem": "what-it-means-to-be-late", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "what does it mean to be late? wisdom and life lessons being late means not being able to do something, an action on time. especially in our relationships and behavior with the people around us. expressing our love to someone, receiving our share of love from someone, forgiving those who have broken our hearts, apologizing for those we have hurt is valuable and beautiful when it is in time. we are human beings. each of us can make a mistake or act wrongly at any time. the important thing is not to delay in admitting and correcting the mistake we made, apologizing, and making amends. life is short. let us not delay so that we can live a life full of justice and happiness, where there are no \"wishes\" and many \"good things\". moral: expressing love, forgiving, and apologizing on time is beautiful. life is short - let us not postpone the affairs of the heart. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "What is important in life?", "stem": "what-matters-in-life", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "what is important in life? wisdom and life lessons the teacher enters the classroom, greets the students, and takes a glass jar out of her bag and places it on the table: — we all have only one life, and we can achieve everything we want in this life — if we use our time wisely. — then she fills the jar with golf balls and asks the students: — do you think the jar is full? — full, — everyone answers in unison. the teacher takes a handful of large pebbles from her bag, adds them to the jar, and asks again: — now, is the jar full? — yes, — the students answer. the teacher pours some more sand into the jar and asks again: — is the jar full? the students confirm that the jar is full this time. having finished her demonstration, the teacher explains to the students the meaning of what she has seen: — imagine that this jar is a symbol of life: the golf balls represent the things you value most in life — your family, friends, and loved ones; the large pebbles represent other important factors—your wealth, possessions, and occupation; and the sand represents other things you may need but are not so important. if i filled the jar with sand first, there would be no room for golf balls or pebbles. life is like that. if we fill it with small, unimportant things and spend all our time and energy on them, we will not have time for the most important things. moral: fill the jar of life with the most valuable things first—family, love, and health. if you get caught up in the little things, there will be no room for the important things. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "What we learn in life", "stem": "what-we-learn-in-life", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "what we learn in life wisdom and life lessons in this life, you constantly learn: from whom to be patient, from whom to love, from whom to be loyal, faithful, and moral; from whom to know what hypocrisy, deceit, malice, and betrayal are. moral: life teaches us something from every person: from whom to be loyal, from whom to be treason. both are lessons. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Where does this road lead?", "stem": "where-does-this-road-go", "category": "Wisdom and life lessons", "slug": "hikmet-ve-heyat-dersleri", "hay": "where does this road lead? wisdom and life lessons a young man walking along the road meets an old man sitting by the side of the road and asks: — uncle, where does this road lead? — nowhere, my son. — how do you mean? a road that leads nowhere? — by god, i have been here for fifty years; this road is still where it was. it is the people who come and go. moral: the road is still where it is, the people who come and go. know not where the road leads, but where you are going. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "An Organization Without an Action Plan", "stem": "an-organization-without-an-action-plan", "category": "Work, livelihood, and wealth", "slug": "emek-ruzi-ve-servet", "hay": "an organization without an action plan work, livelihood, and wealth an organization without an action plan resembles a ship whose destination is known but whose nautical chart, route, and crew responsibilities have not been defined. such a ship may occasionally reach port through good fortune, a capable captain, or favorable weather. that outcome, however, would be the product of circumstance rather than systematic management. lasting success is achieved not by chance, but through thoughtful planning and disciplined execution. over time, an organization without a plan also begins to lose its members. people are sustained not only by ideas, but by the hope of seeing those ideas put into practice. when hope fades, people leave; when people leave, the organization gradually weakens and eventually ceases to function. moral: a goal provides direction; a plan defines the path toward it. an organization may advance by chance without a plan, but lasting success requires deliberate and consistent action. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "At the Fish Shop", "stem": "at-the-fish-shop", "category": "Work, livelihood, and wealth", "slug": "emek-ruzi-ve-servet", "hay": "at the fish shop work, livelihood, and wealth after work, a man was walking toward the bus stop when he passed a fish shop. remembering how much his wife and daughter loved fish, he went inside to surprise them with dinner. he asked the seller for one kilogram, then changed the order to two. at that moment, an elderly woman in a worn coat approached timidly: “son, would you buy some fish for me too?” without hesitation, the man told the seller: “make it four kilograms—two for me and two for her.” he then asked whether she had bread. when she said no, he hurried to the shop across the street, bought four loaves, and gave her a share. “is there anything else you need?” the woman took an empty medicine bottle from her pocket. they were eye drops she had last been able to buy two months earlier; without them, her sight was worsening. the man went to the pharmacy. the fish seller, who had watched everything, offered the woman a chair. returning with the medicine, the man was heartened by the seller’s compassion as well. he gave the woman the medicine and then all the money left in his pocket. blessing him, she made her way home. as the man headed toward the bus stop, he remembered that he had not paid for the fish. his pockets were empty. the seller simply smiled: “go, brother. it is freely given.” with no bus fare, the man walked home. he called his daughter, told her he had bought fish, and promised to arrive soon. as he walked through the rain, he thanked god for the chance to help; the tears on his cheeks mingled with the rainwater. moral: do not wait for great means before doing good; kindness depends not on wealth, but on a generous heart. a person rich in spirit finds a way to help even while possessing little. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Baklava", "stem": "baklava", "category": "Work, livelihood, and wealth", "slug": "emek-ruzi-ve-servet", "hay": "baklava work, livelihood, and wealth when i was a child, our family had very little money. one day, my father brought home some baklava, and we enjoyed it with tea that evening. the next day was a holiday. my mother packed one piece of baklava, a few olives, and some bread for me to take to school. my classmates had many different foods. a boy i disliked saw my modest meal and laughed: “did you bring that baklava for the mice?” when the whole class laughed, i ran home in tears and shouted at my mother: “why did you give me only one piece? everyone else had so much food—you humiliated me!” my mother answered sadly: “my child, that was the only piece that belonged to me.” her words shattered me. she had saved her sole piece of baklava for me instead of eating it herself. i was too ashamed even to embrace her. ever since, i have asked forgiveness whenever i remember my behavior; baklava became the one sweet i could never eat again. moral: mocking another person’s poverty is among the gravest wrongs. never forget that a mother may surrender her own share—even her final bite—for her child. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Chief Accountant and CEO", "stem": "the-chief-accountant-and-the-ceo", "category": "Work, livelihood, and wealth", "slug": "emek-ruzi-ve-servet", "hay": "chief accountant and ceo work, livelihood, and wealth the chief accountant says to the ceo: — suppose we spent time and money on the development of our employees, but despite this they left our company. what do you think would happen? ceo: — suppose we spent all the time and money we had on them, and they chose not to leave the company. what would happen then? moral: an investment in a person is not a loss. he who considers not spending to be saving is choosing tomorrow's big loss over today's small comfort. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Do you have money?", "stem": "do-you-have-spending-money", "category": "Work, livelihood, and wealth", "slug": "emek-ruzi-ve-servet", "hay": "do you have money? work, livelihood, and wealth value your parents while they are alive. never ask, \"do you have money?\" go and meet all their needs within your means. no one can take their place. moral: value your parents while they are alive, meet their needs with deeds, not words. no one or nothing can take their place. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Everyone Has Work to Do", "stem": "everyone-has-work-to-do", "category": "Work, livelihood, and wealth", "slug": "emek-ruzi-ve-servet", "hay": "everyone has work to do work, livelihood, and wealth i am very lucky to have wise teachers. one of them was my literature teacher, the esteemed baladadash. he would say that if someone asked you for something or assigned you a task, even if for a trivial reason, you should never leave it unanswered. everyone has work to do. a person bothers you not because he is idle, but because he needs you. never ignore an appeal to you; do not keep that person waiting by staying silent. if you will not be able to help, say so openly. leaving people without an answer destroys their trust in you. moral: it is a very painful feeling to live knowing you are regarded as unreliable and untrustworthy. bakhtiyar sirajov"}, {"title": "Former Minister at Seminar", "stem": "the-former-minister-at-the-seminar", "category": "Work, livelihood, and wealth", "slug": "emek-ruzi-ve-servet", "hay": "former minister at seminar work, livelihood, and wealth a former minister was invited to a seminar. when it was his turn, he stood up on the podium with a plastic cup in his hand and began to speak. but it was clear that his mind was elsewhere. suddenly, he raised the plastic cup, looked at it, and took a deep breath. he turned to the hall and said: — do you know what i think? last year, i spoke on this very podium. the only difference was that i was a minister then. when i came here, i had a business class ticket, a limousine and escort cars were waiting for me at the airport. they took me to a hotel. i rested in a luxury suite, and the next day i came here in the same limousine. a special welcome was arranged. while i was waiting for my speech, they offered me coffee in an expensive porcelain cup. then they seated me in a specially designated place. but today i am not speaking to you as a minister. i flew here yesterday with a ticket i bought with my own money. no one met me at the airport. i took a taxi to the hotel, and no one accompanied me to my room. when i came here this morning, they checked my id against the list and let me in. then i sat down in the first place i found in the hall. i wanted coffee, so they showed me the coffee machine in the hallway. so i went out and filled this plastic cup that you see here. the seminar participants had already started laughing. the former minister continued: “i think that last year’s coffee in an expensive cup was not for me, but for my position.” the hall erupted in applause. as soon as the applause died down, he said: — i want to share with you this important lesson that i learned: all praise, all services rendered are only for your rank, role, or position. none of them belong to you. instead of the expensive cup that you used while you were in office, when you leave office, you will be given a plastic cup — that is, what you deserve. moral: the respect shown to you is often for your position, not for yourself. therefore, do not brag about your position — your true value will be seen when you leave. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Garden and gardener", "stem": "the-garden-and-the-gardener", "category": "Work, livelihood, and wealth", "slug": "emek-ruzi-ve-servet", "hay": "garden and gardener work, livelihood, and wealth the teacher addresses his students: — imagine that a gardener is taking care of the garden. did you imagine it? — we did, — the students answer. the teacher says in a serious voice: — remember what i am going to say now: it is not true to say that the garden grew thanks to the gardener, but it is also not true to say that the garden grew by itself. moral: it is neither true to deny the hard work of others nor to belittle one's own. a person grows neither entirely by himself nor entirely thanks to someone else. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Grocery store and supermarket", "stem": "the-grocery-shop-and-the-supermarket", "category": "Work, livelihood, and wealth", "slug": "emek-ruzi-ve-servet", "hay": "grocery store and supermarket work, livelihood, and wealth an elderly woman had a grocery store in our neighborhood. one day, a supermarket opened opposite her store. both began to write and hang the prices of groceries on the sign in front of their shops. when the supermarket posted “butter — 70 dollars,” the woman posted “we have butter — 60 dollars.” the next day, the supermarket posted “butter — 50 dollars”; the aunt immediately updated her sign: “we have butter — 40 dollars.” one of the residents of the neighborhood, concerned about this situation, turned to the woman: — aunt, it’s better if you don’t compete with this supermarket. they have wide means, and if the price drops they won’t suffer, but you will go bankrupt. the woman replied without losing her composure: — i don’t sell butter at all… let people at least buy it cheaply. moral: a person who lives by the question “whom did i help?” rather than “what did i gain?” is different. the one who gives without expecting anything in return is actually the one who gains the most. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Henry Ford's Choice", "stem": "henry-fords-choice", "category": "Work, livelihood, and wealth", "slug": "emek-ruzi-ve-servet", "hay": "henry ford's choice work, livelihood, and wealth it is said that one day henry ford, the founder of the ford motor company, invited two young friends who wanted to work for his company to dinner. both young men graduated from the same university and were very talented and educated specialists. the dinner was over, the party ended. as they left the restaurant, ford told one of the young men that he had hired him, and wished the other good luck and said that he could not give him a job. the disappointed young man gathered all his courage and asked ford: — mr. ford, we have not talked about cars or engineering skills all evening, we have only touched on general topics. but you offered my friend a job, and deprived me of this opportunity. why did you make such a decision? ford replied: — look, young man, there are two reasons for this. first: i have observed the behavior of both of you throughout the meal. i noticed that your friend added salt after tasting the food, while you added salt first and only tasted it afterwards. i value those people who decide to make changes only after experimenting and measuring. — the second reason is this: your friend was very polite to the waiters, and you ignored the waiters and only showed politeness to me. and people should be treated the same, regardless of their position. moral: a person's character is shown in his small actions: in his impulsive decisions and in his attitude towards the weak. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "His Hand Is at Work", "stem": "his-hand-is-at-work", "category": "Work, livelihood, and wealth", "slug": "emek-ruzi-ve-servet", "hay": "his hand is at work work, livelihood, and wealth a man bought ten eggs for ten kopecks and sold them for nine. when acquaintances asked the purpose of this strange trade, he replied: — never mind the loss; seeing that i trade, everyone takes me for a merchant — everyone sees that my hand is at work. moral: wasting time on empty work for the sake of appearances is the real loss. looking busy does not bring real benefit. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Hot bread", "stem": "hot-bread", "category": "Work, livelihood, and wealth", "slug": "emek-ruzi-ve-servet", "hay": "hot bread work, livelihood, and wealth the smell of hot, fresh bread spread throughout the street. the old man approached the baker. he needed to buy hot bread for his grandson. the child had been asking for fresh bread for days. people were standing in line to buy bread. he took his place in line. there weren't many people, and his turn came quickly. he took out some change from his pocket. if he had a lot of money, he would have bought two loaves of bread. he handed the money to the baker. but the baker didn't take money from the grandfather and gave him a package. — take it, uncle, and enjoy it. there were three loaves of bread in the package. — son, i was going to buy one loaf. — take all three, uncle, and eat them later. the grandfather slowly approached the baker and whispered in his ear: — son, i only have money to buy one loaf of bread. the baker smiled: — uncle, this is our share. today is friday; we get three hot loaves of bread every friday. enjoy it. — may god be pleased with you, my son. — amen! he had just left the bakery when someone called out to the grandfather: — uncle, oh uncle! the grandfather turned and looked: — here, my son. — uncle, this is my mother's zakat; i would be very happy if you accept it. the young man put an envelope in the old man's pocket. — may god accept it, my son. may god be pleased with your mother. as they passed by the market, a woman called out to him: — uncle... wait a minute, please wait. the old man stopped and looked in the direction from which the voice came. a young woman handed him two full bags: — uncle, this ramadan we are giving food to everyone who passes by, please accept it. what can i do, don't let us down, take these bags. — may god accept it, my daughter. — amen, uncle! the old man reached home with difficulty, exhausted. — welcome, father. — where is your mother, my daughter? — in the kitchen. she said i will cook eggs, orkhan loves kaygana (a local egg scramble) very much. — take these and give them to your mother, my back is broken. — grandpa, did you buy hot bread? — the grandson said. — yes, i bought a lot. — long live grandpa! — dad, you didn’t have money in the morning, not even to drink tea. — god gave it to you, my daughter. his wife entered the room: — how much did you buy, man? — these are your share, wife. the man told what had happened one by one. the adhan was called. they opened the iftar with kaygana (a local egg scramble). the hot bread was perfect. there was butter and cheese in the bag. it went very well with the tea. — yes, it was very good, thank god. may god accept it. the halva was also very tasty. when his grandson got up from the table and said: — i'm going to go to my room and pray a little more, — the grandfather was surprised: — do it before you go to bed, my son, it's still early. — i did it at this time yesterday. i asked god for warm cheese and bread. look, today it came. now i'll go and pray that he will send you money to fix your teeth. your teeth are rotten, and you chew hard with these teeth. his mother smiled: — i wish your late father could see these days. — my father comes to me in my dreams every night anyway and sees these things. in fact, he told me to pray. the old man suddenly remembered his jacket. he went to the hanger, put his hand in his pocket and took out the envelope. there was a large amount of money in the envelope. his eyes filled with tears. — what happened, man, why are your eyes wet? — my lord has sent everything that our grandson has in his heart, wife. just look at this money — we can both get our teeth fixed before the holiday. thank god! moral: a sincere child's prayer, the kindness of a few kind-hearted people can solve a family's problems. in a society that is able to give, no one goes hungry. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Loan book", "stem": "the-credit-ledger", "category": "Work, livelihood, and wealth", "slug": "emek-ruzi-ve-servet", "hay": "loan book work, livelihood, and wealth a plain-clothed stranger enters a neighborhood shop. after looking at the seller and the counter for a while, he says: — brother, if i ask you for a loan of bread, tea, or eggs, will you give it to me? — there is a man who has been carrying bread for free for three months. to be honest, i don't believe he will pay this fee. but what can i do, he takes care of three orphans, let alone leave their children hungry. god is great, he will provide for us too. i don't want the fee for the bread i gave you. take it and go, you will be happy. — a smile spreads across the stranger's face and he says in his heart: \"i think i found what i was looking for.\" — how much debt do you have in that loan book? the seller asks, somewhat surprised and somewhat annoyed: — what are you doing? the man who came continued the conversation calmly, somewhat reassuringly: — look, brother, i beg you, don't get angry. i'm not such a vain person that i'd come here and spoil your nerves, take up your time. i'll buy that notebook of yours. please, count all the debts in it right now and tell me. the seller looked at the man in front of him, and then at the overflowing notebook. seeing that the man in front of him was stubborn, he didn't resist; he added up all the debts recorded in the notebook and told the man the amount received. the man who came took a wad of money from his jacket's breast pocket, put that amount in front of the seller and said: — brother, may god be pleased with you. if you trust this community so much, if you don't leave an orphan hungry out of consideration for god, then you are a good person. i have paid everyone's debts. let this be my share of ramadan to this community. now give me that notebook. i will take him and bury him, and i believe that list will be my help on the day of judgment. — the man took the notebook and left the store. when the shopkeeper counted the money in front of him and put it in the cash register, he still couldn't fully understand what was happening. it was as if he had been hit by frost. he looked out the small window of the shop. there was no stranger in sight. everyone was hurrying home because it was almost time for iftar. at that moment, the seven-year-old daughter of suraiya, whose husband had died of tuberculosis, appeared at the door and said shyly: — uncle ahmed, my mother says to give me one loaf of bread and three eggs for iftar. we will give you the money when my uncle comes to us next month. — the shopkeeper seemed to wake up from a dream at the girl's words. as usual, she reached under the counter to take out the notebook. she paused for a moment, smiled, and said: — come, my daughter, take the eggs, the bread, and the butter. tell your mother that that uncle came and went... moral: a kindness done silently is the highest kindness. forgiving a person's debt and not leaving an orphan hungry is the true richness of the heart. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Mercedes", "stem": "mercedes", "category": "Work, livelihood, and wealth", "slug": "emek-ruzi-ve-servet", "hay": "mercedes work, livelihood, and wealth mercedes was not the surname of its founder or the name of a large automobile company. it was the name of the daughter of a father who was passionate about cars. the father believed that the name of his daughter would bring him success. and so it happened... the car he named \"mercedes\" won consecutive victories in the races it participated in. thanks to these successes, countless mercedes cars have been produced since 1902. the father's love for his daughter turned the mercedes name into the brand of one of the largest automobile manufacturers in the world. mrs. mercedes, who had two unsuccessful marriages, lived in vienna. in 1929, at the age of thirty-nine, she died of cancer and was buried in vienna. moral: a name given with love, a work done with love can change the world. a father's love immortalized a name. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Pay rent for water", "stem": "pay-rent-for-the-water", "category": "Work, livelihood, and wealth", "slug": "emek-ruzi-ve-servet", "hay": "pay rent for water work, livelihood, and wealth a man buys a well from a villager. when he wants to draw water from the well, the seller does not allow him: “i sold you the well, not the water in the well,” he says. an argument ensues. he says, so does the judge—in the end, they have to go to the judge. the judge says to the seller: “since the water is yours, you cannot keep it in someone else’s well! either take your water and go, or pay rent to the owner of the well for keeping it there.” moral: he who wants to take his rights by crooked means falls into his own trap. justice weighs the rights of both parties. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Scarf seller", "stem": "the-scarf-seller", "category": "Work, livelihood, and wealth", "slug": "emek-ruzi-ve-servet", "hay": "scarf seller work, livelihood, and wealth the freezing cold of the night had enveloped the city. the ground was icy and slippery. my breath felt as if it would freeze. i was trying to walk very carefully so as not to fall. i approached the bus door, said “thank goodness,” and managed to get there safely. at that moment, a child approached me with the scarves he was trying to sell. i had no strength to pay him any attention; my only concern was to get on the bus as soon as possible. the child: — will you buy one, sister? — he said, holding out a scarf. to get rid of him, i said: — no, i don’t want one. — take it, sister, take it, you’ll need it, — he said. to escape the situation, i said: — i don’t have any money. — never mind, sister, you take it — i don’t want money; let it be from me, — he said. — that won’t do; i don’t want it, i already have a scarf, — i said, and threw myself onto the bus. at that moment i was thinking only of myself; i did not care about the child at all. i hurried toward my seat. before sitting down, i put my hand into my coat pocket to take out my phone. my hand touched a package. i took it out and looked. i was very surprised to see a package with a scarf inside. so the child had slipped the package into my pocket without my noticing. i immediately threw myself out of the bus door onto the street. the child selling scarves was still in the same place, trying to sell the scarf in his hand to people boarding the bus. i approached this child who paid no mind to the cold — his nose bright red, his pitch-black eyes shining like stars in the night, his innocent smile showing his white teeth, about twelve or thirteen years old, wearing a thin coat, without gloves, trying to earn money in the night cold with bare hands. i put my hand on his shoulder and showed him the scarf: — what is this? the child smiled and said: — it’s nothing, sister; you’ll need it — let it be from me. — if you do that, how will you earn money? aren’t you sorry for yourself in this cold? — i said. — it’s all right, sister, — he said with loving eyes. i held out the money. — no, sister, i don’t want it; let it be from me, — he said again. yet if he had looked at me and said, “sister, you said you had no money — where did you get this?”, i would have sunk into the ground from shame at that moment. but he was a straightforward, innocent, pure child, with an untainted heart, who considered everyone as pure as himself. — take this, — i said, looking firmly into his face. seeing my determination, he took the money and with his other hand began to take change from his pocket to give me back. — no, i don’t want it; keep the change, — i said. — sister, this is too much; that won’t do, — he said, and hurriedly tried to separate the money; some of it fell to the ground. — it’s all right; may you be well — keep it from me, — i said with a smile. embarrassed, with that smile that melts the ice, the child said: — thank you, sister; may god be pleased with you. “thank you too! thank you too, child of my poor country — big-hearted and content!” i said in my heart, and went and sat down. outside, ice-cold air still covered this child’s small, beloved city — a city in which there are still children who remain pure in their innocence, whose trust in people has not been damaged, who turn winter into spring with their laughter, and who try to earn something to support their families. we set off. in the darkness of the night, that city and that boy selling scarves were left behind. with a bitterness that had settled into my heart, i thought of the souls cast out of their homes in this cold. various emotions i could not name rose inside me; something knotting in my throat took my breath away, and tears kept looking for a chance to fall from my eyes. our bus continued on its way, winding through the snow-covered mountains. moral: sometimes the most helpless person shows the greatest generosity. the pure heart of a child turns material poverty into wealth. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The Weight of Butter", "stem": "the-weight-of-the-oil", "category": "Work, livelihood, and wealth", "slug": "emek-ruzi-ve-servet", "hay": "the weight of butter work, livelihood, and wealth a baker regularly bought a certain amount of butter from a peasant. one day he noticed that the weight of the butter he received was decreasing day by day. he accused the peasant of cheating him and complained to the judge. when the peasant was summoned before the judge, he replied: “actually, i don’t have any scales. i put the butter i want to sell in one hand and the one-kilo loaf i buy from the baker every day in the other, and i measure the butter against that.” moral: as you treat others, you often receive the same measure in return. the bread you give comes back to you as butter. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "We Are Rich", "stem": "we-are-rich", "category": "Work, livelihood, and wealth", "slug": "emek-ruzi-ve-servet", "hay": "we are rich work, livelihood, and wealth near the end of the fourth century, emperor nintoku of japan climbed a high tower one day and surveyed his country. seeing not even a wisp of smoke rising, he realized his people were in poverty and that no one could cook at home. he at once issued a decree ordering the people to work only for themselves for three years. he also sent the palace workers home. working for themselves and paying no tax, the people came to abundance after three years. three years later nintoku climbed the tower again, saw smoke rising on every side, and was sure that hearths were burning across the land. joyfully he said to the lady beside him: — now we are rich! but the empress pointed to the palace, neglected for three years — roof leaking, flowers withered — and asked: — do you call this wealth? nintoku’s answer has been remembered by the japanese for centuries: — our people’s poverty is our poverty, and their wealth is our wealth. moral: true wealth is not in the treasury, but in the people’s well-being. a leader’s greatness lies not in the post, but in the meaning given to that post. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Why am I poor?", "stem": "why-am-i-poor", "category": "Work, livelihood, and wealth", "slug": "emek-ruzi-ve-servet", "hay": "why am i poor? work, livelihood, and wealth once a poor man met buddha and asked him: — buddha, why am i poor? — because you don’t give anything from your wealth to anyone. — how can i do that? i have nothing to give to others! — you have five riches that you could share with others, but you don’t. — please explain those five riches to me. — okay. 1. you could give others a smile with your face, but you don’t. 2. you could look at others with compassion and love with your eyes, but you don’t. 3. you could say good words to others with your tongue, but you don’t. 4. you could wish others happiness with your heart, but you don’t. 5. you could do good deeds for others with your hands, but you don’t. moral: there is always something to give: a smile, a kind word, a compassionate look, a helping hand. a person is as rich as they share. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "A bell to hang from the cat's neck", "stem": "the-bell-to-hang-on-the-cats-neck", "category": "Justice and society", "slug": "edalet-ve-cemiyyet", "hay": "a bell to hang from the cat's neck justice and society a group of mice gathered together and looked for ways to get rid of a cat that had become a nuisance to them. many ideas were put forward, but none were accepted. finally, a young mouse suggested hanging a bell from the cat's neck. the essence of the plan was this: as the cat with the bell hanging from its neck approached the mice, they would hear the bell and run away, thus saving their lives. this proposal was met with great enthusiasm and applause by all the mice. at this moment, the old mouse, who was silently watching what was happening in a corner, spoke up: — this is a very clever proposal, and if it were implemented, i have no doubt that it would be successful. but one question makes me think: well, tell me, who would hang the bell from the cat's neck? moral: it is easy to make a plan, but difficult to carry it out. even the brightest idea that is not implemented is worthless. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "A woman whose house was robbed", "stem": "the-woman-whose-house-was-robbed", "category": "Justice and society", "slug": "edalet-ve-cemiyyet", "hay": "a woman whose house was robbed justice and society in ancient times, the house of an old woman living in a city was robbed. the woman came to the king in a state of dismay to complain. the king listened to her and angrily asked: “then why did you fall into such a deep sleep that you did not even notice that a thief had entered your house?” the woman replied: “thank god, king, we thought you were awake, so we slept peacefully in our house.” moral: the peace of the people comes from the trust they have in the state. if the people know that their leader is awake, they will sleep peacefully in their own homes. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Homeland and land", "stem": "homeland-and-land", "category": "Justice and society", "slug": "edalet-ve-cemiyyet", "hay": "homeland and land justice and society if you add water to the land, you get mud, if you add blood, you get a homeland. moral: what turns the land into a homeland is the blood shed for it. homeland is not just a place, it is a sacredness for which sacrifices are made. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Nazim Hikmet in Bursa Prison", "stem": "nazim-hikmet-in-bursa-prison", "category": "Justice and society", "slug": "edalet-ve-cemiyyet", "hay": "nazim hikmet in bursa prison justice and society turkish poet nazim hikmet was serving a sentence in bursa prison. one day, an inspector from the ministry of justice came to inspect the prison. he asked the prison chief to call nazim hikmet — he wanted to meet with him. nazim was called. he entered the room... they had a short conversation. but the ministry employee did not offer the poet a seat. after the conversation, he said: \"you can go.\" nazim hikmet turned around and asked the inspector as he was leaving the door: — do you know omar khayyam? — of course. is it okay not to know him? — and can you tell me who the king was during omar khayyam's time? the inspector was surprised by this unexpected question and could not answer. nazim hikmet said: — you see, you remembered the artist, but not the king. the time will come when they will neither remember the minister of justice nor you. but they will always remember my name. saying this, he leaves. the inspector realizes his mistake. even though he calls him, the poet does not return... moral: as time passes, those in positions of authority are forgotten, but those who create true value are remembered. positions are temporary, deeds are permanent. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Sharing justice", "stem": "sharing-justice", "category": "Justice and society", "slug": "edalet-ve-cemiyyet", "hay": "sharing justice justice and society either no one should cry, or these always laughing people should not laugh; or no one should have this disease called money, or the moneyed nation should learn to share; or no one should say what they love, or they should give the definition of this true love; or these holidays should not be experienced at all, or there should be no one who does not eat meat during the holidays. moral: humanity is created when joy, bread, and love are shared. if one side laughs and the other side cries, there is no justice there. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The just verdict of the Frankfurt judge", "stem": "the-just-verdict-of-the-frankfurt-judge", "category": "Justice and society", "slug": "edalet-ve-cemiyyet", "hay": "the just verdict of the frankfurt judge justice and society this incident was recorded in the city of frankfurt in 1506. a merchant lost his bag with 800 guilders on the road. by chance, a carpenter passing by found the bag. the carpenter, who was extremely religious, did not tell anyone that he had found the bag, thinking that it was impossible not to notice that so much money was lost, so the owner would definitely look for it. one day, the carpenter went to church to worship. after the service, the monk announced to the congregation that a merchant who had come to frankfurt had lost his 800 guilders and promised a reward of 100 guilders to whoever found it. hearing this, the carpenter took the money he had found, went to church, and handed the bag over to the monk. the merchant was informed that his money had been found; he went to church and took the bag, but refused to pay the carpenter the 100 guilders he had promised—he gave him only 5 guilders. the carpenter asks the merchant to keep his promise. the greedy merchant claims that he has 900 guilders in his wallet, not 800, in order to avoid giving him 100 guilders, and that the carpenter took money from the bag. the monk intervenes in the argument to defend the carpenter: he says that he knows him, that he is an honest man, and that he would never do such a thing. the argument becomes heated. the monk takes the merchant and the carpenter to the city court. the trial begins. the judge asks the merchant to swear by placing his hand on the bible that he has lost his 900 guilders. the merchant, without hesitation, places his hand on the bible and swears. the judge also asks the carpenter to swear by finding the 800 guilders. the carpenter also places his hand on the bible and swears. everyone is impatiently waiting for the judge's decision. the judge says that everything is clear as day and explains his decision as follows: — the carpenter found 800 guilders, and the merchant lost 900 guilders. therefore, the money that the carpenter found does not belong to the merchant. therefore, since the owner has not been found, these 800 guilders are given to the carpenter. the merchant can continue to search for the 900 guilders he lost. moral: greed traps a person. the stingy person who denies the truth eventually becomes a victim of his own lies; justice is on the side of the righteous. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The Skins of the Lambs", "stem": "the-skins-of-the-lambs", "category": "Justice and society", "slug": "edalet-ve-cemiyyet", "hay": "the skins of the lambs justice and society “it is better to shear the wool of rams than to skin lambs.” this saying is associated with azmzadeh asad pasha, who was the governor of damascus province during the ottoman period. the story goes like this: one day, asad pasha’s treasury was empty and he was in great distress. his advisors advised him to impose an additional tax on the weavers of damascus as a way to fill the treasury. asad pasha, who listened to the advice, asked: “how much income would we get if we imposed such a tax?” “we will buy fifty or sixty bags of gold,” they said. asad pasha objected: “these people are already struggling to make ends meet. how will they pay this tax?” the advisors said: “they will sell the gold and jewels in their houses, pasha,” and tried to persuade him. asad pasha says: “i will get this amount in a better way.” the advisors are silent. the next day, the pasha sends an invitation to the mufti and offers to meet secretly at night. at night, the mufti comes to visit the pasha. the pasha says: — mufti effendi! according to the information we have received, you are acting contrary to the sharia in your personal life, drinking alcohol secretly at home. i must report this situation to istanbul. however, i thought i would inform you in advance. hearing this, the mufti effendi begins to beg the pasha. in order not to inform istanbul, he offers him a thousand mecidiyes. the pasha does not accept. the mufti offers twice as much. the pasha does not accept again. in the end, they agree to six thousand mecidiyes. the next day, asad pasha invites the ghazi and tells him: “gazi effendi, we have learned from reliable sources that you are taking bribes and using your position for your personal gain.” this time, the ghazi effendi begins to beg the pasha. he, like the mufti, bargains with asad pasha: “oh, my lord, do not remove me from my post, or i will be disgraced among the people.” they also agree to six thousand mejidiye gazi. then asad pasha invites the treasurer, the chief of security, the merchants and the wealthy to a meeting one by one. at the end of this operation, asad pasha collects exactly two hundred bags of mejidiye gold. then he calls his advisors and asks: “have you heard that i imposed a tax on the people of damascus?” “no, we have not heard,” they say. the pasha says: “despite not imposing a tax, i have collected not fifty, but two hundred bags of mejidiye gold.” — how did you do this, pasha? the pasha replies: “it is better to shear the wool of rams than to skin lambs.” moral: it is more just to take the unjust profit of the wealthy than to rob the poor. the burden should be placed on the strong, not the poor. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The story of the Bedouin whose camel was stolen", "stem": "the-bedouin-whose-camel-was-stolen", "category": "Justice and society", "slug": "edalet-ve-cemiyyet", "hay": "the story of the bedouin whose camel was stolen justice and society a bedouin walking in the desert with his camel came across a man who was barely standing, his lips parched with thirst. the man begged the bedouin for water to drink. the bedouin got off his camel and gave him water. the man drank the water, then suddenly pushed the bedouin away, mounted the camel, and started to run away. when the bedouin realized that he could do nothing to save his camel, he shouted after the thief: — take the camel, it is yours. but never tell anyone about this story. surprised by the bedouin's strange request, the thief stopped for a moment, looked back, and asked why. — if you tell, this story will spread everywhere, and no one will ever help the needy people he meets again, — the bedouin replied. moral: sometimes hiding your own harm is to protect the faith of others in the goodness of others. one bad news can close the door to thousands of good things. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The world can be conquered with this nation", "stem": "with-this-nation-the-world-can-be-conquered", "category": "Justice and society", "slug": "edalet-ve-cemiyyet", "hay": "the world can be conquered with this nation justice and society one day, the king changed his clothes and went to the city to observe how the people lived. he began to inspect the city markets to learn about the quality of the goods, their prices, and the mood of the shopkeepers. the king entered the first shop at the beginning of the street. he greeted and said: — give me half a batman of butter, half a batman of honey, and some cheese. the shopkeeper, who greeted the customer with a smile, politely accepted the greeting and gladly took half a batman of butter. while giving the butter, he addressed the man in front of him, not knowing that he was the king, as follows: — my lord, if you wish, i can also give you honey and cheese. however, by selling this butter, i have already made a siptah — the first sale of the day. if you buy your other requests from my neighbor across the street, who has not yet made a siptah, i will be very pleased. the king, who was very happy with this, paid for the butter and went to the shop opposite. from there, he also asked for half a batman of honey and cheese. after the old owner of the shop had tasted the honey: — thank god, thanks to you we have also tasted it today. but i ask you this: do not buy the cheese from me, but from the shop next door. it has not tasted it yet, — he said. the king, who was very impressed by what he saw, went to the shop next door and made his purchase, and said: — with such high morality in this nation, the whole world can be conquered, — he said and left the market happy and satisfied. moral: real power does not lie in weapons, but in the honesty and halalness of the people. not buying land, but conquering hearts is the real victory. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Why injustice when there is justice?", "stem": "why-injustice-when-there-is-justice", "category": "Justice and society", "slug": "edalet-ve-cemiyyet", "hay": "why injustice when there is justice? justice and society haji bektaş veli: why hatred when there is love? why war when there is peace? why quarrel when there is brotherhood? why enmity when there is friendship? why intolerance when there is tolerance? why captivity when there is freedom? why injustice when there is justice? moral: it is man's own fault to choose hatred, strife and injustice when love, peace, brotherhood and justice are possible. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Aging", "stem": "aging", "category": "Aging and time", "slug": "yaslanma-ve-zaman", "hay": "aging aging and time aging is like climbing a mountain: the higher you rise, the more tired you become and the shorter your breath, yet the wider your view of the world grows. moral: growing older is not merely a loss of strength; through experience and wisdom, it is also the ability to see a broader landscape. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "For those who have reached sixty...", "stem": "for-those-who-have-reached-sixty", "category": "Aging and time", "slug": "yaslanma-ve-zaman", "hay": "for those who have reached sixty... aging and time it is time to use the money you have saved up so far. use it and enjoy it. for those who are not aware of the sacrifices you have made to get it, do not save it. this is the ideal time to enjoy peace. do not worry about the financial situation of your children and grandchildren, and do not be upset that you have spent your money on yourself. you have taken care of them for years, taught them everything you could. you have given them education, food, shelter and support. now it is their duty to earn their own money. let them find their own way. now lead a healthy life without much physical exertion. exercise moderately, go for a walk every day, eat well and sleep well. at this age, it is easy to get sick and difficult to stay healthy. therefore, you should keep yourself in good shape, pay attention to your medical and physical needs. contact your doctor; get check-ups even if you feel fine. do not stress over small things. you have already overcome a lot in your life. you have had both good and bad memories. don't let the past hold you back and the future scare you. feel good now. minor troubles will soon be forgotten. regardless of your age, always live your love. love your partner, love life, love your family and remember: as long as your mind and compassion are in order, you are not old. don't stop going to the hairdresser, take care of your nails, visit a dermatologist and dentist, use perfumes and creams. when you are well-groomed on the outside, this feeling also seeps into you, you feel proud and strong. don't lose sight of age-appropriate fashion trends, but maintain your own style. read books, flip through newspapers, watch the news. go online, find out what people think. make sure you have an active email account and use at least a few social networks. respect the younger generation and their opinions. they may not agree with you, but the future is theirs and they will direct their lives in the direction they want. do not criticize, give advice and try to remind them that yesterday's wisdom is still valid today. never use the phrase \"in our time...\". now is your time. be a part of this time as long as you live. try to stay away from negative people and spend your time only with people who give you a good mood, positive energy. do not give up your hobbies. you can travel, walk, cook, read, dance. if you want, you can keep a cat or a dog. get out of the house, meet people you have not seen for a long time, experience something new or old anew. the main thing is to get out of the house from time to time. go to museums, walk in the park. try to always be polite, do not complain and criticize unless necessary. if someone has broken your heart, forgive them. apologize to the people you have broken your heart to. don't carry your pain with you. it will only hurt you. it doesn't matter who is right. they say that holding a grudge is like poisoning yourself and waiting for someone else to die. never accept that poison. forgive, forget and move on with your life. laugh, be happy. enjoy a peaceful life at this age of your life... be happy! moral: the era of self-sacrifice will end one day; in the autumn of your life, give yourself the right to live. a happy person spreads happiness to those around him. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "What has changed after sixty", "stem": "what-changed-after-sixty", "category": "Aging and time", "slug": "yaslanma-ve-zaman", "hay": "what has changed after sixty aging and time i asked one of my friends, who is in her 60s and approaching 70, what changes she has noticed in herself. she replied: i stopped telling the elderly, “you told me that story.” after all, these conversations help them revive their good memories and relive those wonderful moments. i learned not to correct people even when they make mistakes. after all, it is not my job to make everyone perfect. being present is more important than perfection. i give a lot of compliments. after all, this is pleasant not only to the person in front of me, but also to my own soul. i learned not to worry about a wrinkle or a stain on my clothes. ultimately, a person’s value comes from within, not from their appearance. i learned to stay away from people who are strangers to my world. after all, they are not able to establish and maintain good relationships; and i learned that too. i learned not to be ashamed of my feelings. after all, it is my feelings that make me human. i learned that it is better to put my ego aside than to destroy relationships. after all, ego leads me to loneliness, and relationships help me not to be lonely. i learned to do things that can make me happy. after all, i am responsible for my own happiness. i learned not to help without being asked. after all, a person who needs help may not want this help to come from me. i learned not to complain about my health, my neighbors, my relatives, my retirement! after all, despite all the difficulties, i must be grateful that i am alive and that my loved ones are with me. i stopped saying phrases like “when i was your age…” to young people; and i never use expressions like “i gave you my best years…”. after all, every time has its own rules, and it is not right to apply the criteria of the past to the present. i began to pay more attention to my wife, not forgetting that she was once young, strong, and full of joy — even though she is now a wrinkled, helpless, and sad old woman. after all, maybe she is the only person who really needs me right now. i stopped blaming myself for everything. after all, i have done everything i can in life for myself and for my children. and finally: i learned to ask my lord for strength to change the things i can, patience to change the things i cannot, and wisdom to know the difference between the two. after all, as a human being, i am not capable of everything. moral: age teaches a person the greatest wisdom: peace is better than perfection, and understanding is better than being right. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "A Fly in a China Shop", "stem": "a-fly-in-the-china-shop", "category": "Parables and fables", "slug": "temsiller-ve-meseller", "hay": "a fly in a china shop parables and fables a fly could never destroy a china shop by its own strength. but if it flew into a bull’s ear, the frightened animal might panic and wreck the entire shop. the fly represents superstition, falsehood, and prejudice. the bull represents an ignorant person who acts without thinking. moral: a small lie whispered into an ignorant ear can cause enormous destruction. knowledge, critical thinking, and strong moral values are our best protection against it. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Elephant and Rope", "stem": "the-elephant-and-the-rope", "category": "Parables and fables", "slug": "temsiller-ve-meseller", "hay": "elephant and rope parables and fables a traveler passes by an elephant enclosure and is surprised to see that these giant animals are tied to a stake by a front leg with just a short rope. there is no chain or cage. it would seem natural that the elephants could easily break the rope at any moment and escape, but they did not seem to be trying to escape. the man approaches a nearby trainer and asks why these magnificent animals stand there obediently and do not try to run away. the trainer explains: — when elephants are young, a short rope is enough to tie them and hold them. as they grow, these animals gradually begin to believe that they cannot break the rope and escape. they think that this rope will still hold them and will not let them go free. the man is amazed to hear this. imagine: these animals remained bound to the spot where they stood, believing freedom was impossible, even though they could break free at any moment. moral: often it is not the rope that binds us, but the distrust born of past failures. it is impossible to be free without overcoming the fear of “what will people say.” stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The donkey's law", "stem": "the-donkeys-law", "category": "Parables and fables", "slug": "temsiller-ve-meseller", "hay": "the donkey's law parables and fables one day, a donkey becomes the king of the forest. a few days later, he makes a law: everyone must go to bed at six in the evening and wake up at six in the morning. for twelve hours, there must be complete silence in the forest, and anyone who breaks the law must be punished. in this way, for a while, the forest is quiet. one day, a rooster wakes up at five in the morning and begins to crow. the world in the forest clashes with each other. a court is immediately set up and the rooster is executed by the donkey's verdict. after that day, no one touches the law for fear of the king. years pass, the donkey grows old and weak. he retreats to a corner of the forest and awaits his death. the lion comes to him, takes stock of his condition, and then asks: — you are already living your last days. we want to know: what was the wisdom of that law that ordered you to go to bed at six in the evening and get up at six in the morning? how many times have we tried to revolt and abolish it, but we held back because it would disrupt order in the forest. we said to ourselves that perhaps there is some wisdom in this law that we do not understand. the donkey smiled and replied: — indeed, i myself have been wondering about this for years: why did the wild animals of the forest obey this law that i derived from my donkeyness like sheep? moral: what keeps a meaningless law standing is not the power of the one who gave it, but the silence of those who obey. behind an order there is not always wisdom — sometimes only donkeyness — so do not hesitate to ask, for an unanswered question can turn even the oldest nonsense into law. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The Hunter's Tale", "stem": "the-hunters-description", "category": "Parables and fables", "slug": "temsiller-ve-meseller", "hay": "the hunter's tale parables and fables until the lion learns to write, every storyteller will write the hunter's tale. african proverb moral: he who tells the story becomes the hero. if you don't learn to write your own truth, you will always be told by others. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The Lion's Track", "stem": "the-lions-footprint", "category": "Parables and fables", "slug": "temsiller-ve-meseller", "hay": "the lion's track parables and fables a cowardly, yet arrogant hunter, following a lion, reaches a forest and asks the woodcutter who comes across him: — brother, have you come across a lion or its track around here? woodcutter: — i saw the lion himself; if you want, come and take you to where he is. the pale hunter: — no, my dear, thank you. i was actually looking for the lion's track, not the lion itself. moral: a person is known not by his pretensions, but by his behavior in the moment of trial. many people \"look for a lion\" from afar, but when they come face to face, most of them retreat. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "The tortoise's wrong calculation", "stem": "the-turtles-wrong-calculation", "category": "Parables and fables", "slug": "temsiller-ve-meseller", "hay": "the tortoise's wrong calculation parables and fables they ask the tortoise: — how long will it take you to reach this nearby village? — taking into account the rain, mud, wind, and ups and downs, it's a three-day journey, but i'll make it in six days... — the tortoise replies. after six days, they look and see that the tortoise has not yet reached the village. they search and finally find him halfway along the way: — well, you couldn't make it three days' journey in six days? — they ask in surprise. the tortoise replies in disappointment: — never mind, friends. i calculated the rain, mud, wind, and ups and downs, but unfortunately, it didn't occur to me to calculate the bad people. as soon as i started to move, they caught me and turned me upside down. moral: you can calculate every difficulty in life, but not people with bad intentions. may god not let you face those who prevent you from moving forward, but those who support you. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Two donkeys", "stem": "two-donkeys", "category": "Parables and fables", "slug": "temsiller-ve-meseller", "hay": "two donkeys parables and fables two donkeys are walking along a path. they see a monkey sitting in a tree. they say, let's tease this monkey: let's ask him if he has a friend or not? if he says \"yes\", we say \"which fool is your friend?\"; if he says \"no\", we say \"which fool would be your friend?\" they approach the tree and ask: \"monkey, do you have a friend?\" the monkey looks down at the donkeys who want to tease him and says: — wherever i look, they are all donkeys; who should i be friends with? moral: a trap set to humiliate someone often explodes on the head of the one who sets the trap. the one who wants to tease himself becomes ridiculous. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Water-carrying ant", "stem": "the-ant-that-carries-water", "category": "Parables and fables", "slug": "temsiller-ve-meseller", "hay": "water-carrying ant parables and fables according to legend, nimrod (a biblical character, the ruler of the land of shinar) wants to punish the prophet abraham, whom he considers an obstacle to his cruelty, by throwing him into the fire. he builds a huge bonfire in the middle of the square. all living things that see the bonfire run away. meanwhile, an ant with a drop of water in its mouth starts running towards the bonfire in a hurry. seeing it, another ant asks: — where are you running in such a hurry? — haven't you heard that nimrod is going to throw the prophet abraham into the fire? — the water-carrying ant does not hide his concern. — you probably haven't seen that bonfire. it's a huge bonfire, there's no other way but to run. will you put out that bonfire with this drop of water in your mouth? — the second ant asks mockingly. the water-carrying ant replies: — even if i can't put it out, at least it will be clear whose side i'm on. moral: even if you can't, at least stand by the truth. being there for your loved one when they're in trouble is a value in itself. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "A Magnificent Lesson", "stem": "a-magnificent-lesson", "category": "History and notable figures", "slug": "tarix-ve-taninmish-shexsiyyetler", "hay": "a magnificent lesson history and notable figures as a child, mehmed the conqueror, son of the ottoman sultan murad ii, would not sit quietly in class and was constantly disruptive. whenever his teacher, akşemseddin, corrected him, the boy replied: “you cannot tell me what to do. i am the sultan’s son!” the teacher was deeply troubled. he hesitated to complain to the ruler, but one day he gathered his courage, appeared before murad ii, and explained what had been happening. the sultan thought for a moment and whispered a plan into the teacher’s ear. akşemseddin protested that it would be impossible, but murad ii insisted: “it will be done.” the next day, young mehmed again misbehaved and answered his teacher with the same threat. at that moment, the sultan opened the door and entered the classroom. following the plan, akşemseddin pretended to be furious. he reprimanded the sultan for entering without permission, even slapped him, and ordered him to leave immediately. murad ii lowered his head, apologized, and left. seeing that even his powerful father had to obey the teacher’s rules, the prince was stunned. a short while later, someone knocked. this time the sultan asked permission to enter and apologized again. the plan worked. from that day forward, mehmed no longer disrupted his lessons. for the sake of his son’s upbringing, murad ii had set aside even a ruler’s pride. moral: in education, personal example is stronger than any lecture. a responsible parent may even set aside personal pride to help a child find the right path. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Albrecht Dürer", "stem": "albrecht-durer", "category": "History and notable figures", "slug": "tarix-ve-taninmish-shexsiyyetler", "hay": "albrecht dürer history and notable figures in a small village near nuremberg lived a family with ten children. their father worked eighteen hours a day to support the household. despite their hardship, the two eldest sons, albrecht and albert, dreamed of becoming renowned artists. they knew their father could not afford to educate them both at once. after long discussions by candlelight, the brothers decided to draw lots. the winner would study at the academy in nuremberg, while the other worked in a nearby mine to support him. after four years, they would exchange places. albrecht won the draw. he left for the academy, and albert began working in the mine. for four years, one brother studied art while the other labored underground. albrecht’s work soon attracted wide attention; some said that it surpassed the work of many of his teachers. by the time he completed his studies, albrecht was earning a good income. he returned home joyfully and told his brother: “dear brother, now it is your turn. go and pursue your dream; this time i will support you.” albert held out his hands and answered softly: “no, brother. it is too late. during these four years, the mine has crushed my hands and every finger. i can no longer draw.” albrecht took his brother’s hands in his own and wept. centuries later, the works of albrecht dürer (1471–1528) continue to be displayed in leading galleries around the world. the famous drawing associated with his brother’s sacrifice is known as praying hands. moral: behind success there often stands an unseen sacrifice. to recognize and honor that sacrifice is one of the finest expressions of humanity. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Sinan Brought Water to Istanbul but Died in a House Without It", "stem": "architect-sinan-dies-in-a-house-without-water", "category": "History and notable figures", "slug": "tarix-ve-taninmish-shexsiyyetler", "hay": "sinan brought water to istanbul but died in a house without it history and notable figures during the reign of suleiman the magnificent, rapidly growing istanbul suffered a severe water shortage. the solution became associated with the great architect koca sinan (1489–1588). one day, the sultan summoned him and asked: “chief architect, people pay dearly even for a packhorse’s load of water. can istanbul be supplied with enough water?” sinan requested permission to survey the surrounding country. with his assistants, he examined wells, valleys, and streams from çekmece to beşiktaş and calculated where dams and aqueducts could be built. when he returned, he told the sultan: “it can be done, but at a heavy price: sacks filled with gold would have to be placed end to end.” the sultan replied: “tell me only that it is possible. if necessary, i will place the sacks not end to end, but side by side.” sinan began work and created a magnificent system culminating in forty public fountains across the city. taps were fitted so that the precious water would not be wasted. the sultan declared the public fountains the property of the people and prohibited secret pipes into private homes. in recognition of sinan’s service, however, he verbally allowed a private line to the architect’s house. years passed. suleiman and those who knew sinan’s service firsthand died. one day, the elderly architect was summoned to the palace, where the council was investigating a complaint about the private water in his home. sinan explained that the sultan himself had granted permission. when asked for a written decree, he admitted: “at the time, i was ashamed to ask the ruler of the world for a document. i have no written order.” after a long debate, the council decided not to punish sinan for his past use of the water, but ordered the private line cut off. near the end of his life, sinan fell gravely ill. when those beside him tried to moisten his lips, the tap was dry. the great architect said: “we did not serve for profit, but for god’s pleasure. we await our reward in the hereafter and do not grieve that this world has denied water to our home.” moral: do not place excessive trust in fame, worldly privilege, or human remembrance. true service is rendered not for personal gain, but for what one knows to be right. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}, {"title": "Three important things for a city", "stem": "three-essential-things-for-a-city", "category": "History and notable figures", "slug": "tarix-ve-taninmish-shexsiyyetler", "hay": "three important things for a city history and notable figures there are three important things for every city: sewage, bath and library. because sewage cleans the dirt of the city, bath cleans the body, and library cleans the soul. fatih sultan mehmet moral: a city must keep both the body and the soul clean. the library cleans the soul, bath cleans the body, and sewage cleans the city. stories are taken from open internet sources; illustrations were created with ai."}];
